K, Goede JS, Valk PJ, Löwenberg B, Takizawa H, Manz MG. MPL expression on AML blasts predicts peripheral blood neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. Blood, The Journal of the American Society of Hematology, 2016. 128(18):2253–2257. doi: 10.1182/blood-2016-04-711986. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Zhang Q, Dai K, Bi L, Jiang S, Han Y, Yu K, Zhang S. Pretreatment platelet count predicts survival outcome of patients with de novo non-M3 acute myeloid leukemia. PeerJ. 2017;5:e4139. doi: 10.7717/peerj.4139. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Zein JG, Lee GL, Tawk M, Dabaja M, Kinasewitz GT. Prognostic significance of elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) in patients with severe sepsis. Chest. 2004;126(4):873S. doi: 10.1378/chest.126.4_MeetingAbstracts.873S. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Lu J, Wei Z, Jiang H, Cheng L, Chen Q, Chen M, Yan J, Sun Z. Lactate dehydrogenase is associated with 28-day mortality in patients with sepsis: a retrospective observational study. Journal of Surgical Research. 2018;228:314–321. doi: 10.1016/j.jss.2018.03.035. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Huang Z, Liu W, Guo Q, Liu C. Platelet parameters and expression of platelet membrane glycoprotein in childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Genet Mol Res. 2015;14(4):16074–16089. doi: 10.4238/2015.December.7.20. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Balduini CL, Noris P. Mean platelet volume for distinguishing between inherited thrombocytopenias and immune thrombocytopenia-response to B eyan. British journal of haematology, 2013. 163(3):413–414. doi: 10.1111/bjh.12504. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Chandra H, Chandra S, Rawat A, Verma S. Role of mean platelet volume as discriminating guide for bone marrow disease in patients with thrombocytopenia. International journal of laboratory hematology, 2010. 32(5):498–505. doi: 10.1111/j.1751-553X.2009.01212.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Vinholt PJ, Hvas AM, Nybo M. An overview of platelet indices and methods for evaluating platelet function in thrombocytopenic patients. European journal of haematology, 2014. 92(5):367–376. doi: 10.1111/ejh.12262. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Zhang S, Cui Y-L, Diao M-Y, Chen D-C, Lin Z-F. Use of platelet indices for determining illness severity and predicting prognosis in critically ill patients. Chinese medical journal. 2015;128(15):2012–2018. doi: 10.4103/0366-6999.161346. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Golwala ZM, Shah H, Gupta N, Sreenivas V, Puliyel JM. Mean platelet volume (MPV), platelet distribution width (PDW), platelet count and Plateletcrit (PCT) as predictors of in-hospital paediatric mortality: a case-control study. African health sciences, 2016. 16(2):356–362. doi: 10.4314/ahs.v16i2.3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',0,'2025-10-15 11:30:45','2025-10-15 11:30:45');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5724,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4729421','Emergency Medicine Resident Rotations Abroad: Current Status and Next Steps.','Morris SC, Schroeder ED.','2016','uncoupled chronicle vetting cecylia primack 0b013e3180305cf9 mckinley norcini 10suppl s53 0b013e318183e351 havryliuk jra talley','Abstract Introduction International rotations for residents are increasingly popular, but there is a dearth of evidence to demonstrate that these rotations are safe and that residents have appropriate training and support to conduct them. Methods A survey was sent to all U.S. emergency medicine (EM) residencies with publicly available e-mail addresses. The survey documents and examines the training and support that emergency medicine residents are offered for international rotations and the frequency of adverse safety events. Results 72.5% of program director responded that their residents are participating in rotations abroad. However, only 15.4% of programs reported offering training specific to working abroad. The results point to an increased need for specific training and insurance coverage. Conclusion Oversight of international rotations should be improved to guarantee safety and education benefit. INTRODUCTION International rotations for emergency medicine (EM) residents are becoming increasingly popular, but little is known about institutional support for this trend, specifically, educational supervision, safety activities, and insurance coverage related to these rotations. This research sought to determine how many EM programs in the United States were sending their residents abroad and the safety, training and insurance status of the residents, as well as any adverse events that occurred. BACKGROUND Over the last three decades the amount of funding for and projects in global health have significantly increased, as has awareness of global health issues within the mainstream U.S. medical system. At the same time, there has been an exponential surge in interest and involvement in international rotations among medical students and residents in the U.S. 1 , 2 Despite increased opportunities and funding, there is unmet demand from individuals who would like to work abroad. By 2004, 22% of U.S. medical students had participated in an international rotation. 3 Although there are many competing demands on a trainee’s time during residency, many U.S. programs allow participation in international rotations. As a specialty, EM engages in all aspects of the medical spectrum; thus, there are few clinical specialties as well suited to the global health clinical environment. Additionally, given the dynamic, high-paced, and unpredictable nature of EM, there are likely common characteristics that make global health interesting to EM providers. Several studies have shown that a significant percentage of EM residents participate in these rotations and that, at this point, there is no standardization of training for trainees who work abroad. 4 , 5 METHODS This study was given a waiver by Institutional Review Board (IRB) of the University of Washington. We conducted an online search of the 165 U.S.-based EM residency programs during the summer of 2013. The name of the program director or assistant director and their email was obtained from the residency website. If no contact information was available, the residency was not included in the survey. The authors created a nine-question survey that could be completed in 2–3 minutes. The study was opened on September 13, 2013, and closed on June 16, 2014. In the fall of 2013, the survey was sent to the 134 EM programs that had publicly available email addresses. Individuals who did not complete the survey were sent a series of reminder emails requesting participation or removal from the survey. The researchers made a single phone call to those who had not completed the survey requesting their participation towards the end of the survey period. The results were permanently uncoupled from the respondent’s email as per the requirement of University of Washington’s IRB waiver criteria. The first three questions identified the number of residents in the program, the length of the training program, and whether any of the residents participated in international rotations. Questions 4 and 5 inquired whether the program had dedicated global health faculty and an established international site for resident rotations. Question 6 asked if residents were given any training prior to participating in an international rotation and question 7 addressed the presence of liability coverage of the residents working abroad. Question 8 attempted to ascertain how rotation sites were assessed for safety and question 9 allowed respondents to chronicle safety events that had occurred to their residents during the preceding five years. RESULTS A total of 91 of 134 residency program or assistant program directors answered the survey for a response rate of 67.9%. The respondents represent 55% of all EM programs. Of the 91 responders, 66 (72.5%) responded that their residents participated in rotations abroad as part of their training program. An average 10.4% and 8.86% of residents in three- and four-year programs, respectively, were reported to have participated in a rotation longer than two weeks during the 12 months prior to the survey. Of all respondents, only 17.6% responded that they had three or more faculty whose primary areas of academic interest is global health; 38.5% reported their program had an “established relationship (defined as frequent educational contact, one or more ongoing projects, and faculty or resident exchange) with an emergency department or training facility outside of the U.S.” An estimate of the nature of the residents’ international work was given by 35 respondents. When asked to estimate the type of work performed abroad by category, results demonstrated clinical work 38%, EM development 18%, and public health work 12%. Project work that was less frequently conducted included observational and research, 9% each, and humanitarian response and disaster response, 7% each. Unfortunately and interestingly, many respondents did not complete this question. Only 15.4% of programs reported offering any special training prior to allowing their residents to work abroad. Of the small percentage of respondents who reported conducting some training, the themes were public health skills, personal health and safety, tropical medicine and ethical considerations. Liability insurance covered the residents’ work abroad at 47% of the responders’ institutions, while 31% were not covered and 22% were unsure of their institutional policies. Of the 65 responders who answered if their program sites where evaluated for safety by faculty, 29 (45%) said “yes” while 36 (55%) said “no” or were unsure. Several responders cited some other mechanism for safety evaluation including using local staff and online screening of political security. Very few security events and no deaths were reported by the respondents. Of note, 12% of responders reported major illness or political instability affecting their residents and 2% reported assault or an incident requiring evacuation. Key findings are summarized in Figure . Figure. Open in a new tab Summary of results. LIMITATIONS This study used a publicly generated list of residency directors in order to be eligible for IRB waiver and repeated contacts. This resulted in an incomplete survey of EM residency programs; however, our data on the number of residents participating is similar to prior studies. The study relied on the memory of the respondents resulting in potential recall bias. In a few cases both the residency director and assistant residency director may have responded to the survey resulting in redundant submissions. In addition, our study over represented four-year programs. Nationally four-year residencies represent 24.6% of all EM residencies while we had 37.5% of our responses from four-year residencies. DISCUSSION Our study revealed both positive and negative characteristics of the current policies regarding international rotations in EM. Of greatest importance to the authors, there are a large number of residents going abroad every year and overall the level of insecurity they are experiencing from health or safety risks is low. However, pre-travel training, vetting of work sites, presence of global health faculty and ensured liability are all lower than expected. Clinical work, EM development and public health projects dominated the nature of the work, while research, humanitarian and disaster work are at surprisingly low levels. Perhaps the most important outcome of this study is that it highlights the need for a uniform and comprehensive national education program for EM residents doing international rotations. A global health component of resident education, pre-departure safety training and use of attending physician-vetted sites to enhance resident safety and the quality of the educational experience should be considered minimum requirements for any program that allows residents to participate in rotations abroad. Pre-departure appropriate course work in working in austere environments, cultural sensitivity, tropical disease and public health should be considered. Use of mentorship should also be encouraged to maximize the resident’s experience and provide additional outlets for accessing faculty. CONCLUSION Our survey pointed out several important issues regarding international rotations for EM. First, as with other specialties, international rotations are common and heterogeneous with regard to activities conducted and supervision. Additionally, there is a low level of pre-rotation training, insurance coverage and site safety evaluation. Finally though safety incidents are rare, several serious events including major illness, assault and injury were reported. This study highlights the need for greater supervision, training and support of EM residents conducting rotations abroad. Footnotes Section Editor: Cecylia Kelley, DO Full text available through open access at http://escholarship.org/uc/uciem_westjem Conflicts of Interest : By the West JEM article submission agreement, all authors are required to disclose all affiliations, funding sources and financial or management relationships that could be perceived as potential sources of bias. The authors disclosed none. REFERENCES 1. Drain PK, Primack A, Hunt DD, et al. Global health in medical education: a call for more training and opportunities. Acad Med. 2007;82(3):226–30. doi: 10.1097/ACM.0b013e3180305cf9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. McKinley DW, Williams SR, Norcini JJ, et al. International exchange programs and U.S. medical schools. Acad Med. 2008;83(10suppl):S53–7. doi: 10.1097/ACM.0b013e318183e351. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Association of American Medical Colleges. 2003 Medical School Graduation Questionnaire All Schools Report. Washington, DC: Association of American Medical Colleges; 2003. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Havryliuk T, Bentley S, Hahn S. Global health education in emergency medicine residency programs. J Emerg Med. 2014;46(6):847–52. doi: 10.1016/j.jemermed.2013.11.101. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. King JRA, Liu KY, Talley BE, et al. Availability and potential impact of international rotations in emergency medicine residency programs. Emerg Med. 2013;44(2):499–504. doi: 10.1016/j.jemermed.2012.05.034. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',196,'2025-10-15 11:30:51','2025-10-15 11:30:51');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5725,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11185868','Opportunities of AI-powered applications in anesthesiology to enhance patient safety.','Kovacheva VP, Nagle B.','2024','spam dependable performant abstracting attacker undesired shapley ubiquitously bonde expereince mallampati thyromental mallampati thyromental lehane performant matava biosignal hpi decoder freehand transforaminal overlays klea gjuzi vpk 1k08hl161326 apsf bwh ignite vpk avania cro wo2021119593a1 ramrattan smorenburg gouma boermeester newbower mcpeek mishaps singap perianesthetic rodziewicz houseman hipskind meara leather hagander mukasa kovacheva ngan kee khaw critchley karmakar simpao lingappan galvez krall gabel extractable castano meza orozco ryder alsentzer fontoura vavilala sls bonde varadarajan bonde e471 e485 kloesel prielipp wildes kannampallil engoren woodall frerk samsoon cuendet schoettker yuce tavolara gurcan niazi mkk hayasaka kawano kurihara nakane kawamae cormack lehane matava pankiv raisbeck caldeira biro asahq salmasi maheshwari kendale postinduction e33 vistisen hatib scheeren twl wijnberge hol schneck habig protocolized tsoumpa kyttari matiatou maheshwari enevoldsen vistisen dibiasi agibetov kapral zeiner kimberger neradilek maheshwari greuters veerman loer sukuvaara koski makivirta kari maciag ballast cnossen struys ruskin hueske safavi nikolov tolia kronish misadventures nanji seger bashqoy brinton guffey yaster codonics omnicell nanji garabedian nanji garabedian langlieb bickford struys smet glen vereecke absalom schnider brogi cyr kazan giunta hemmerling rinehart bardaji bowness burckett amiri tehrani zade jalili majedi mirbagheri ahmadian yusong shuang kiong tildsley ikhsan scapular smistad leidig lervik bakeng lindseth margalit phalen transforaminal bowness burckett hutyra obermeyer vogeli mullainathan evgeniou gerke minssen e376 e379 e407 e408 rampton mittelman goldhahn e111 e112','Introduction Anesthesiology has been at the forefront of patient safety efforts for decades, resulting in one of the lowest percentages of adverse events out of any specialty. 1 The work of Cooper et al. in 1978 on medical errors and patient harm 2 started a national movement to increase awareness and improve patient safety. 3 The mortality from anesthesia has continued to decline steadily. 4 This has been done through advances in patient monitors, rigorous scholarship in drug and machine usage, and a culture of safety. Despite this tremendous success, adverse events during medical care remain one of the ten leading causes of morbidity and mortality worldwide. 5 More than 50% of these patient injuries are preventable, 5 and the majority of patient harm occurs in the perioperative period. 6 The highest number of safety events are in the following domains: patient morbidity and mortality, difficult airway, patient monitoring, medication errors, and technical procedures. The global need for safe surgery and anesthesia in the next ten years is expected to increase by more than 45%, yet the available resources are scarce. 7 The emerging artificial intelligence (AI) technologies can be leveraged to predict patients at risk for adverse events, collect and process data, and provide guidance with technical procedures. By harnessing AI technologies, anesthesiologists can deliver high-quality care across all major safety domains ( Fig. 1 ). Fig 1. Open in a new tab Perioperative patient safety domains with potential to be enhanced by artificial intelligence technologies. The purpose of this narrative review is to provide an overview of the most current AI technologies and how those can be used to enhance patient safety in anesthesiology. We review the available methodology and provide practical examples of how AI models can aid the clinician from the patient safety perspective. Definitions and types of AI technology AI is a sub-field of computer science focused on developing technologies that can perform complex automated tasks with human-like intelligence. This field has far-reaching applications that have become part of our daily lives, such as traffic navigation, spam filtering, shopping, and online searching. In anesthesiology, the amount and complexity of data have been increasing exponentially, 8 and AI technologies, ideally suited for processing these data, can provide valuable insights. In a broad sense, the term ‘AI’ has been applied to any computerized and robotic system. Many early AI systems are rule-based automation based on expert knowledge or known relationships in data. An example of such a system in which pre-defined changes in blood pressure trigger the administration of fixed or adjustable vasopressor doses. 9 These tools can also contribute to patient safety by ensuring adherence to clinical protocols such as compliance with perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis and clinical documentation. 10 While these systems facilitate basic automation and provide predictable and transparent output, they may be insufficient when encountering a new, undefined situation. Supervised machine learning Machine learning, a sub-field of AI, employs mathematical modeling to uncover complex, non-linear patterns within data and develop predictive tools. In most healthcare applications, machine learning algorithms are developed using supervised learning. The main goal is to discover the relationships between predictor variables and single or multiple labeled outcomes by training a model on these input-output pairs to generate accurate predictions when presented with new data. An example is a model to predict perioperative mortality using data from electronic health records. 11 Multiple supervised machine learning algorithms exist; most models are used for regression or classification tasks. In regression, the desired output is a continuous outcome, for example, predicting the duration of surgery. In classification, the output is in discrete categories, for example, the presence of a fracture in a set of X-ray images. The selection of which model to employ depends on the nature of the task and the characteristics of the available data. In studies with linear data, linear and logistic regression are often considered most suitable. Support vector machines are more complex models that work by finding the decision boundary that maximally separates data belonging to different classes. These models tend to work well with small datasets and generalize well but have lower interpretability. Decision trees and closely related random forest and gradient-boosted models have shown robust performance in biomedical structured data. Decision trees are based on breaking down the data into a series of hierarchical decision points. Random forest models have enhanced predictive accuracy by employing an ensemble of decision trees. Xgboost models sequentially build and further improve the performance of multiple decision trees. This type of model is commonly used with structured electronic health record data due to its capability to accommodate large datasets, high interpretability, and dependable results. Deep learning models have high power to achieve accurate predictions. This method is based on neural networks, consisting of connected layers of artificial neurons. These computational units process data mathematically by stepwise extraction and processing information, resulting in the propagation of the most relevant features of the data forward. Thus, it is not necessary to pre-define the specific features, as those are self-selected in the process of training with the goal of achieving the best prediction of the outcome. These models excel with complex, multimodal data, such as text, images, and waveforms. Examples of deep learning models include convolutional neural networks that can identify anatomical structures from ultrasound images. 12 While these types of models require high computational resources and may be difficult to interpret, they are highly performant and used in many healthcare applications today. Unsupervised learning Unsupervised learning is used to discover hidden patterns in data; in this case, the models extract meaningful information without pre-defined labels or outcomes. For example, clustering enables the classification of a group of patients with a disease into subgroups that can exhibit different responses to a particular treatment, thus allowing the selection of those individuals who would maximally benefit from that intervention. In the past five years, large self-supervised models known as transformers have been created. Those models are trained on an extensive volume of unstructured text data and, as a result, can achieve performance comparable to humans in tasks like abstracting and generating text. These large transformer models can adapt to new tasks without or with minimal explicit training. 13 While these types of models can accomplish complex medical knowledge tasks with strong performance, 14 direct implementation in clinical care requires further investigation. Some concerns with large language models are due to their ability to generate inaccurate outputs called hallucinations. Another security vulnerability, called prompt injection, is due to an attacker’s ability to manipulate the model’s output. For example, a model might be prompted to generate an undesired output or provide secure data that was used for the model’s training. These errors might have serious consequences, especially when accurate information is crucial. Some potential strategies to mitigate hallucinations include input preprocessing, which can increase the odds that a model will provide a desired output, and a technique called reinforcement learning with human feedback, which utilizes regular human review of the model’s responses and feedback on the desired output. Thus, rigorous investigation and development of strategies to ensure the safety and reliability of large language models are needed in order to integrate this technology into direct patient care successfully. Reinforcement learning The goal of this method is to develop a system that can improve its actions via interactions with the environment to achieve the highest possible reward. This method is utilized when the model needs to learn a series of actions and receives feedback on each action based on how well the action was measured. For example, a system using reinforcement learning was developed to control ventilation during the emergence from general anesthesia while preventing hemodynamic and ventilatory complications. 15 In this case, optimal cardiorespiratory status served as a reward, while values outside of pre-specified ranges served as a checkpoint for the model to further self-tune. Eventually, the model learned the optimal strategy for controlling ventilation. These types of models are best suited for decision-control systems and closed-loop control devices. Best practices and considerations for AI Model Performance Evaluation Model development and selection should be based on careful evaluation. The best practices of software development and data science should be followed. The models should be developed using large and granular datasets. The most commonly used data sources are electronic health records, administrative databases, and specialized datasets. 8 The data sources available for model development in anesthesiology have limitations as large databases may not contain sufficiently granular data. In contrast, electronic health records data have significant depth but may require extensive processing. Specialized datasets are created under specific conditions for the purposes of a study, for example, standardized airway images. 16 In most circumstances, the performance of the models is evaluated using a multitude of metrics, summarized in Table 1 . No single test is superior to the others, and whether the model will be useful depends on performance metrics and the specific circumstances in which the model will be used. When selecting a model, it is important to consider the model’s performance in comparison with the current standard of care and the clinical circumstances. Even a moderately performing model will be beneficial if the existing methods are lacking and the needs are high. For example, Lundberg et al. found that physicians currently predict around 15% of hypoxic events, but with the assistance of an AI algorithm, they can predict 30% of the hypoxic events, which was a 2-fold increase. 17 In this way, a model outperforming the current standard of care will add value and potentially prevent disease or aid earlier diagnosis. Table 1. Commonly used artificial intelligence (AI) model performance metrics and definitions True positive Total correctly predicted positive cases True negative Total correctly predicted negative cases False positive Negative cases that are falsely predicted as positive cases False negative Positive cases that are falsely predicted as negative cases Sensitivity Fraction of true positive values predicted as positive. Specificity Fraction of true negative values predicted as negative. Positive predicted value Fraction of predicted positive values that are true positive. Negative predicted value Fraction of predicted negative values that are true negative. Accuracy Correctly predicted cases divided by all cases Area under the receiver operator characteristic curve (AUC) A measure of the predictive power of a model; takes values between 0 and 1; 0.5 is equal to a random guess, the closer to 1, the better the model is. Open in a new tab Further insight into the model’s performance may be gained by using the explainability features, which allow us to investigate what features lead to the model’s output. Some models are inherently interpretable, such as linear and logistic regressions, where the coefficients can explain the basis for the predictions. However, most AI models are very complex and considered ‘black box’, and it is not straightforward to determine why a certain prediction was made. Shapley values 18 is one of the most commonly used explainability approaches in healthcare. This method, based on the game theory, allows estimating the mean contribution of each predictor to the model output. For example, a large model to predict postoperative complications had top predictors, including the procedure code, the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) class, and the age of patients. 19 By evaluating and comparing the top predictors with those known from prior research, we can gain confidence in the model’s performance and insight into the model’s decision-making process. Specific Applications to Aid Patient Safety Prediction of perioperative morbidity and mortality risk Preoperative evaluation to stratify the risk of anesthesia is performed for every patient. The current, ubiquitously utilized tool, the ASA classification, is subjective and lacks personalization. 20 Since most of the data needed to create such a tool are complex, and the relationships between predictors are non-linear, AI approaches using supervised machine learning are well-suited to address the task of risk prediction. These models are developed using retrospective data from large administrative databases and electronic health records. The advantage of the first approach is that vast amounts of data from multiple centers are available; as a result, these models generalize well to other institutions. For example, Bonde et al. created models to predict postoperative morbidity and mortality. 19 They used the American College of Surgeons National Surgical Quality Improvement Program database, which collects data from 722 hospitals in 15 countries and more than five million patients. These data were utilized to develop three deep neural network models, which had an area under the curve (AUC) of 0.84–0.88 and outperformed the existing risk calculators for mortality and 18 different postoperative complications. While there was no independent validation, this type of model is likely to perform well in external, similar patient populations. The advantage of using electronic health records data for model development is that multiple granular variables per patient are available, such as laboratory results, vital signs, and comorbidities. As a result, these models have higher predictive power, especially in the population in which they are created. For example, a random forest machine learning model, based on the electronic health records from 53,097 surgical patients, had an AUC of 0.93 in predicting postoperative mortality. 11 Models like this can be integrated into the electronic health records so that all predictor variables are entered as part of routine care and ingested automatically. Applying interpretable approaches in those cases would highlight which factors are the most significant contributors to risk, and potentially, before surgery, the patient’s health can be optimized to improve the outcome. Similarly, models developed for specific types of surgery or complications will likely perform better for that use case than a general perioperative risk model. For example, using data from the American College of Surgeons National Surgical Quality Improvement Program database from two million patients, an xgboost model accurately predicted the occurrence of perioperative blood transfusion and had an AUC of 0.93. 21 The large dataset and model design allowed the generalization of the predictions across different hospitals. When retrospectively validated using a single academic center’s data, the model recommended 15% less type and screen orders while maintaining 96% sensitivity. In the future, combining risk prediction tools with decision support systems to guide preoperative evaluation and preparation would reduce cost, case delays, and patient discomfort. More broadly, dynamic morbidity and mortality risk prediction can be applied throughout patients’ perioperative journey. A baseline postoperative complication risk can be assessed at the preoperative visit. Subsequently, during surgery, as more data is collected, these predictions can be updated and further personalized. As a result of this knowledge, the clinical team may increase vigilance for selected high-risk groups, leading to early detection, thus increasing patient safety. A model to predict postoperative deterioration after cardiac surgery developed by Mathis et al. combined structured data from the electronic health record with processed intraoperative waveform data and achieved more accurate predictions compared with each of these modalities alone. 22 Further studies are needed to collect prospective evidence if these systems lead to improved patient care and outcomes. Prediction of difficult airway Airway complications, such as failed intubation, are rare events in anesthesia; however, those events can result in high rates of morbidity and mortality. Despite significant advances in airway safety, such as capnography, supraglottic airways, and video laryngoscopy, there are still common themes underlying most airway complications. 23 Human factors such as poor judgment, lack of expereince, and ‘failure to plan’ were implicated in the majority of adverse airway events. Traditionally, physicians have relied on the physical exam to determine the risk of a difficult airway by evaluating the Mallampati class and thyromental distance, which have a sensitivity of 32%. 24 Machine learning models may improve difficult airway prediction. Statistical models can analyze 2D facial features to predict the risk of difficult intubation, and a random forest model used images from 970 patients. 25 The algorithm achieved an AUC of 0.78–0.81 for correctly predicting difficult airway in agreement with evaluations from trained physicians. A challenge to develop a model for this application is the need for a large dataset, as difficult intubation is a rare event. A possible solution is developing models for specialized patient subgroups and standardizing the patient images. Indeed, this approach achieved excellent performance in White male patients, 16 however, this methodology may not translate well in routine clinical practice with diverse patient populations. Subsequently, an ensemble of convolutional neural networks that analyzed 2D images achieved AUC of 0.71 and significantly outperformed Mallampati or thyromental distance but not both tests. 26 However, the model did not generalize well as there was little diversity in the original study population. To overcome the limited annotated data availability, a semi-supervised learning model achieved high performance with an AUC of 0.94 and was able to differentiate between the risks for difficult mask ventilation and difficult intubation. As the model was developed using only 30% of labeled samples and achieved results similar to a fully supervised model, the first approach can result in more efficient use of data. As data about the intubation was lacking and the model was developed using images of Chinese patients only, it may not generalize well outside the study conditions. Deep learning models utilizing convolutional neural networks have also been used successfully in another single-center study; however, the above concerns about study design and validation remain. 27 In another study in which lateral cervical spinal radiographs were available, a convolutional neural network model developed using images from the same institution predicted the Cochrane–Lehane grade 3 and 4 glottic views with an AUC of 0.96. 28 These early results are promising, and accurately predicting the patients at risk for difficult airway would allow better preparedness and decrease complications. To implement this technology, future studies need to develop highly performant models relevant to real-world, diverse clinical practice. Assistance with endotracheal intubation AI has the potential to assist and automate airway control, but due to the high-risk environment and rarity of the event, this technology is still in its early stages of development. Video laryngoscopy has transformed the practice of intubation, and multiple AI tools can provide real-time guidance in locating the glottic opening with 81% accuracy in a proof-of-concept study. 29 Using actual video images from emergency room intubations and physician examination, an AI tool based on convolutional neural networks achieved 74.5% accuracy; however, the performance was lower on more challenging views. 30 Further studies are needed to evaluate the performance in anesthesiology practice as there was no comparison with expert anesthesiologists. Matava et al. developed a dataset of 775 video laryngoscopy and bronchoscopy videos of normal airway anatomy. 31 They trained a convolutional neural network capable of accurately identifying and labeling vocal cords and tracheal rings in real-time video streams. While promising, these results warrant further prospective clinical validation. In the future, this approach can be augmented by including videos of difficult intubation cases; such a system can assist and potentially guide the operator in achieving successful intubation in challenging cases. A prototype robotic device using image recognition software that can autonomously perform endotracheal intubation has been developed. 32 When the device was used by trained anesthesiologists and lay participants on a manikin, both groups achieved similar success rates of 95%−100% successful intubation in comparable times. While the safety profile of this tool needs to be comprehensively investigated, such technology can aid education in technical skills and may assist in resource-limited settings where expert skills are not available. Prediction of adverse events Patient monitoring is required during every anesthetic. 33 Historically, the development of ASA monitoring standards increased patient safety and dramatically decreased the risk of death. Recognizing and predicting patient deterioration will allow early intervention to avoid adverse events. AI tools can aid both early prediction and decision support strategies for correction. Maintaining optimal blood pressure during surgery is associated with improved patient outcomes, such as lower risk for kidney injury, wound infection, and overall mortality. 34 Significant research in predicting hypotension has resulted in the development of several AI tools, one of which is FDA-approved. These models utilize biosignal data 35 or data from electronic health records 36 to predict the risk of developing hypotension 5–15 minutes in advance. The performance of these systems has been investigated, and a study by Davies et al. demonstrated in a cohort of 255 patients from two centers that the Hypotension Prediction Index (HPI) can predict a mean arterial pressure < 65 mmHg 5 minutes before the event with an AUC of 0.93. 37 , 38 Subsequent device investigation in randomized trials has demonstrated mixed results. 39 – 42 These results may be due to a selection bias, and thus, additional investigation is needed. 43 Further, rigorously designed large studies are needed for validation, and these early results demonstrate the potential of this technology. Similarly, multiple AI models have been developed to predict intraoperative adverse events such as hypoxemia, 17 hypothermia, 44 and bradycardia associated with hypotension. 45 These models are developed using clinical patient data such as demographics, comorbidities, laboratory results, and vital signs and will benefit clinical practice when integrated into electronic health records to provide personalized risk prediction. This approach can be further enhanced when coupled with decision support systems, which can assist in selecting the best corrective path. Such devices are being developed to guide goal-directed fluid administration and avoid intraoperative hypotension. As a result, patient management is optimized, and this has been demonstrated in a multicenter prospective clinical study, 46 in which a fluid management device assisted in assessing the fluid responsiveness of the patient and recommended fluid administration. Of the device-recommended fluid boluses, 66% resulted in a desired increase in the stroke volume compared to only 41% (P<0.001) of the clinician-initiated boluses without the device. This approach can be further automated using closed-loop systems, as discussed in the following section. Another safety consideration related to monitoring is due to data and alert fatigue. False alarms and alarm fatigue are important considerations, as only 5% of alarms require immediate action, even in moderate-risk surgery. 47 Machine learning methods to reduce unnecessary alarms have been developed in the past 30 years. 48 Most recently, a retrospective study demonstrated that during routine maintenance of anesthesia, two approaches to decrease false alarms are feasible: complication detection and anomaly detection. 49 The first approach was based on supervised learning and utilized labels for intraoperative events, such as hypotension. For the second approach, an Encoder-Decoder Long Short-Term Memory architecture model was trained only on cases without complication and was used to predict deviations from the norm; moreover, rather than focusing on a specific complication, this model can be used for any abnormal observation. This study also highlights the potential for ‘generalist models’, which can be used for multiple outcomes. As the current paradigm in patient monitoring is that a single device has a single indicator, the anesthesiologist has to integrate the output of multiple monitors to gain insights into the overall patient well-being. Operating rooms today are becoming increasingly complex and more sophisticated, and the possibility of error due to missing impending patient deterioration is increased. 50 Automating the processing of multiple monitors using remote surveillance is feasible and can provide highly actionable information. 51 While such systems would require complex AI models, and those remain to be developed for intraoperative use, the benefits for patient safety will be substantial. Enhancing medication safety Medication safety is one of the main domains for patient safety and has been investigated over the past 40 years. 52 The most common medication errors are related to the wrong dose, wrong label, or failure to deliver the correct medication. 53 Compared to the manual process, automating the process of medication dispensing and subsequent labeling can decrease medication discrepancy by 37% and increase the accuracy of the labels to 100%. 54 This process can be further enhanced by adding clinical decision support within the electronic health records, which can provide missing-medication-triggered alerts and patient-specific dosing alerts. 55 For example, in a randomized trial performed in a simulation setting, the physicians in the intervention group who used the clinical decision support system achieved the medication administration tasks faster and more efficiently than the control group. 56 When implemented in practice, such a system could lead to correct renal dosing and timely blood pressure control, associated with improved workflow and patient care leading. Closed-loop control offers the advantage of automating infusion titration to a target variable. These systems have been developed since 1950, 57 but the clinical use of these devices is limited in the US. 58 There are technologies to automate the administration of sedatives, opioids, vasopressors, and neuromuscular blockers. Automated closed-loop systems have been shown to be safe and effective and achieve better than manual control in maintaining the variable within the target range. 59 Most systems utilize a rules-based approach, for example, vasopressor administration based on blood pressure measurements. 9 Joosten et al. demonstrated the superiority of a closed-loop control system compared to manual administration of norepinephrine infusion to prevent hypotension 38 . Over the years, these systems have become increasingly complex, integrating multiple functions such as anesthetic depth, cardiac flow, and ventilation, which has resulted in improved recovery compared with standard of care. 60 A possible limitation of those systems is the lag time between measurements and drug administration, which may result in sub-optimal treatment decisions. In the future, predicting a target variable and prophylactic drug administration using AI technologies may result in better target control. Ultrasound guidance for regional anesthesia Computer vision technologies, defined as AI tools that process images and video using machine learning, hold immense potential to improve the safety of regional anesthesia and interventional pain procedures. There is significant experience using these models in radiology and pathology; in anesthesiology, the highest potential lies in the automatic acquisition and processing of ultrasound images. Utilizing ultrasound guidance for regional anesthesia and vascular access allows faster and safer performance compared to blind techniques, especially in patients with difficult anatomy. 61 AI methods enhanced image processing to improve the identification of the anatomy and the relationship between anatomical structures. 12 By highlighting the relevant structures, AI-enhanced ultrasound imaging can reduce the risk of unwanted needle trauma to the surrounding structures in 62.9–86.4% of cases and reduce the risk of block failure in 81.3% of scans. 62 Recent work has also focused on using machine learning to precisely localize and track the needle tip during 2D ultrasound imaging. 63 These approaches are even more valuable during steep and deep needle insertions, where needle tip localization may be challenging. To aid in selecting the accurate insertion site for neuraxial anesthesia in real time, automated image processing systems were developed. This type of AI-augmented tool can expedite neuraxial placement and was successful in 92%−97.8% of the patients. 64 , 65 This assistive technology can reduce unsuccessful attempts and improve patient safety and satisfaction. 66 Adding real-time ultrasound guidance during regional anesthesia procedures can significantly enhance the speed and accuracy of performance. For example, several systems can provide guidance to the users regarding the optimal position of the ultrasound probe. 67 Furthermore, robotic automation of neuraxial procedures can improve accuracy compared to a freehand technique, as demonstrated by a study using a model of a lumbar transforaminal injection. 68 Recently, the FDA approved a device that displays color overlays on ultrasound images to aid anesthesiologists in identifying the anatomic structures and achieving successful regional anesthesia. 69 These types of systems show great promise during training in regional anesthesia. Using such an assistive ultrasound device compared to a standard device, novice anesthesiologists achieved 11.3% higher accuracy, particularly in obtaining the correct view and identifying the anatomic landmarks. 70 Further, real-world and randomized studies are needed to investigate the value of this technology in patient care. Considerations and Future Directions There has been a tremendous development in healthcare AI technologies relevant to the practice of anesthesiology. Substantial evidence shows that emerging AI technologies have the potential to improve patient safety across all six domains. Most technologies are still in the early phase, and to be successfully and meaningfully implemented, these AI models need to be properly developed, validated, and implemented. The barriers to AI implementation have been investigated, and in addition to cost, are related to 1.) data and technology, 2.) culture and personnel. 71 For AI technologies to become widely adopted in the anesthesia workflow, these tools need to be developed using state-of-the-art methodology. Large, diverse, and well-annotated healthcare datasets are needed. Using limited data or excluding patient groups may introduce bias, and as a result, some patients may not benefit equitably. 72 The research community has developed guidelines for the creation and reporting of AI research, including the validation, bias, and transferability of the findings. 73 The early steps in regulating the use of health AI have been made in the US 74 and Europe 75 . As most studies of AI technology in anesthesiology are retrospective or single-center, further prospective, randomized trials are needed to demonstrate the value of these technologies in patient care. To ensure safe implementations, anesthesiologists need to be familiar with the AI technologies and their inherent limitations The need to add AI technologies in medical graduate and postgraduate education curricula is well recognized. 76 , 77 Exposure to and experience with this technology early in medical education would be important for clinical adoption and acceptance. Continuing medical education courses and society meetings can also be important for keeping clinicians informed of the latest advancements in AI and its applications in the field of anesthesiology. Conclusion Anesthesiology has made tremendous progress in patient safety in the past; despite these efforts, risks in perioperative care remain. Now, new opportunities to improve safety emerge as AI technologies are steadily making strides toward the daily clinical practice of anesthesiologists. These tools can offer significant advantages for patient care by predicting perioperative risk, assisting with technical procedures, monitoring, preventing adverse events and medication errors, and aiding regional anesthesia. The most mature AI technologies and FDA-approved devices are used for intraoperative monitoring and ultrasound guidance in regional anesthesia. Organizations, companies, and hospitals need to develop rigorous testing and monitoring workflows for AI technologies to be successfully implemented to enhance safety and improve outcomes in anesthesia care. Acknowledgement: We thank Klea Gjuzi for her help with literature review. Sources of Support: VPK reports funding from the NIH/NHLBI grants 1K08HL161326-01A1, Anesthesia Patient Safety Foundation (APSF), and BWH IGNITE Award. Footnotes Competing Interests: VPK reports consulting fees from Avania CRO unrelated to the current work and patent #WO2021119593A1 for the control of a therapeutic delivery system assigned to Mass General Brigham. REFERENCES 1. de Vries EN, Ramrattan MA, Smorenburg SM, Gouma DJ, Boermeester MA. The incidence and nature of in-hospital adverse events: a systematic review. Qual Saf Health Care. 2008;17(3): 216–223. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Cooper JB, Newbower RS, Long CD, McPeek B. Preventable anesthesia mishaps: a study of human factors. Anesthesiology. 1978;49(6): 399–406. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cooper JB. Towards patient safety in anaesthesia. Ann Acad Med Singap. 1994;23(4): 552–557. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Pollard RJ, Hopkins T, Smith CT, et al. Perianesthetic and Anesthesia-Related Mortality in a Southeastern United States Population: A Longitudinal Review of a Prospectively Collected Quality Assurance Data Base. Anesth Analg. 2018;127(3): 730–735. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Organization WH. Patient Safety; 2023. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/patient-safety . Accessed September 11, 2023 2023. 6. Rodziewicz TL, Houseman B, Hipskind JE. Medical Error Reduction and Prevention. StatPearls. Treasure Island (FL); 2023. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Meara JG, Leather AJ, Hagander L, et al. Global Surgery 2030: evidence and solutions for achieving health, welfare, and economic development. Lancet. 2015;386(9993): 569–624. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Mukasa CDM, Kovacheva VP.