gothenburg ventures biomesence e70417 e70417 e70417 s005 e70417 e70417 e70417 s006 admc thereto u01ag061359 u19ag063744 kaddurah daouk admc alzheimergut admc adnimetab od026929 novaseq od026929 r01ag070973 r01ag083883 vilas p30ag062715 wallenberg alfgbg addf adsf adsf adsf adsf metrology neurobiostand 22hlt07 bluefield olav thon erling familjen ronstroms stiftelse stiftelsen gamla tjanarinnor hjarnfonden fo2022 skłodowska miriade jpnd2021 ukdri cobas trademark trademarks neurotoolkit rotkreuz khatib heston compositional e70417 jea lora khatib margo heston naccdata qiita qiita sonnenburg backhed nature18846 seekatz koropatkin degrades hase nchembio kerby dill microbiomes nouchi ferreiro ryou eabo2984 abo2984 darst trimethylamine s13195 nabizadeh valizadeh fallahi s40520 silcode s13195 sochocka donskow łysoniewska diniz kurpas brzozowska leszek s12035 heston hanslik zarbock heintz buschart wilmes chernikova kilroy labus nu13124497 kenna bakeberg fnins ananthakrishnan yajnik chom paley metagenomics laske preische alzbiom fnins koscik jonaitis dadm darst koscik dekosky chertkow betthauser koscik jonaitis awz378 hulle jonaitis betthauser multipanel marotz gogul metagenomics biotechniques kieran adapterama stubs combinatorially itru inext e7755 nurk salido leaderboard metagenomics s13059 marotz cavagnero msystems e01329 e01320 msystems sepich poore kopylova s41388 fastp fastq preprocessor i884 i890 bty560 asri ebler pangenome rhie nurk cechova grch38 haploid minimap2 btab705 danecek bonfield liddle samtools bcftools gigascience giab008 gigascience giab008 fastq synchronization fastq tange gnu lulu rust btv573 navas kosciolek qiita s41592 metagenome bypassing msystems e00167 e00122 msystems hakim wandro zengler zebra msystems e0075822 msystems bolyen rideout extensible qiime s41587 betthauser cody zammit koeppe tb01338 pielou conserv kongelige danske videnskabernes selskab biologiske skrifter lozupone hamady lozupone unifrac birdman olyarchuk orthology bti430 marotz compositional msystems msystems permutational permanova statsref stat07841 pedregosa varoquaux gramfort scikit jmlr v12 pedregosa11a virtanen gommers oliphant scipy s41592 walt colbert varoquaux numpy mcse matplotlib mcse waskom seaborn softw joss fnins kaiyrlykyzy kozhakhmetov babenko kazakhstan byun e0278276 zajac estus corsini kellingray 201900071r hang nagpal neth ebiom fnins verhaar hendriksen hma haran bhattarai e00632 e00619doi cammann bacteroides fragilis microbiomes ebpβ wasen vincentini bacteroidota claesson cusack wilmanski s42255 rosini simoni bartolini multitarget ejmech maczurek hager kenklies addr holmquist stuchbury berbaum pharmthera tritschler wessel radic s0891 s11064 perdigon solas aliaga rescues vglut1 bba bbadis romeiro cerqueira arl oxid longev pignatelli nuccio piattelli curia nucleatum microorganisms11092358 zepeda minot bouzek nucleatum dominates nucleatum fnagi diao nucleatum fnins fcell rouskas mamalaki ntanasi sjb mcnair e01794 e01822 metagenomics metabolomes ijms231911560 feher scuderi exger e075212 heinken aktar parkar stentz bacteroides thetaiotaomicron licinio wesselingh solanki karande ranganathan dahiya nigam synbiotics ijms24043074 e70417 e70417 e70417 s005 e70417 e70417 e70417 s006 naccdata qiita qiita','Abstract BACKGROUND The gut microbiome is a potentially modifiable risk factor for Alzheimer''s disease (AD); however, understanding of its composition and function regarding AD pathology is limited. METHODS Shallow‐shotgun metagenomics was used to analyze the fecal microbiome of participants in the Wisconsin Microbiome in Alzheimer''s Risk Study, leveraging clinical data and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) biomarkers. Differential abundance and ordinary least squares regression analyses were performed to find differentially abundant gut microbiome features and their associations with CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies. RESULTS Gut microbiome composition and function differed between individuals with and without AD dementia. The compositional difference was replicated in an independent cohort. Differentially abundant gut microbiome features were associated with CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies. DISCUSSION These findings enhance our understanding of alterations in gut microbial composition and function in AD, and suggest that gut microbes and their pathways are linked to AD pathology. Highlights Gut microbiome composition and function differ between people with Alzheimer''s disease (AD) dementia and cognitively unimpaired (CU) individuals. Co‐occurring gut microbes show differential abundance across AD‐related groups (AD vs CU, amyloid status+ vs amyloid status−, and apolipoprotein E ( APOE ) ε4 status+ vs APOE ε4 status−). Gut microbiome composition also differs between people with AD dementia and CU individuals in a larger validation cohort. Differentially abundant gut microbiome composition and function between AD and CU groups are correlated with cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers for AD and related pathologies. Keywords: Alzheimer''s disease, biomarkers, cerebrospinal fluid, composition, differential abundance, function, gut microbiome, pathology 1. BACKGROUND The human gut microbiome is widely recognized as an important modifiable factor in health and disease. The composition of the gut bacteria influences overall gut health by maintaining intestinal barrier integrity and immune homeostasis via the balanced composition and production of microbial metabolites such as short‐chain fatty acids (SCFAs). 1 , 2 , 3 Evidence across developmental and neurodegenerative contexts also indicates that the microbiome may play a role in regulating neurological conditions through physiological pathways including bi‐directional vagus nerve signaling and maintenance or degradation of the intestinal barrier, and through inflammatory and metabolic systems. A growing body of literature suggests that changes in the gut microbiome and its metabolism may be influential in exacerbating the development of Alzheimer''s disease (AD). Compositional differences in gut microbiota, including relative abundance and diversity, have been observed between control and AD groups. 4 Other studies have reported that gut microbiome composition is altered among people with AD dementia, individuals with mild cognitive impairment (MCI), or preclinical AD compared with cognitively unimpaired (CU) individuals. 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 To better determine the relationship between the gut microbiome and AD pathology, studies have leveraged measures of AD biomarkers obtained via cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis, 9 positron emission tomography (PET), 10 and plasma. 11 In addition, inflammatory markers have been utilized to find associations with gut inflammation–driven AD pathology. 12 , 13 The shift toward functional analysis provides deeper insights into the metabolic or functional potential of the microbiome; given that multiple lines of evidence indicate that gut microbial pathways and associated metabolites influence disease development, it is important to jointly examine both the gut microbiome and its metabolism as a mode of understanding the natural history of AD and identifying potential therapeutic targets. 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 A small number of studies have examined gut microbiome composition and function together with markers of AD pathology in humans, 8 , 18 , 19 , 20 but many other studies to date are limited to the composition of gut microbes in a single cohort. With multiple cohorts involved and a focus on multi‐omic analysis, the Alzheimer Gut Microbiome Project (AGMP) initiative continues to leverage the gut microbiome and metabolome to better understand metabolic processes that influence AD pathology. To identify differences in composition and function as well as relationships between gut microbiome and AD pathology, this study collected stool samples from participants enrolled in the Microbiome in Alzheimer''s Risk Study (MARS) and analyzed them in collaboration with the AGMP. Differences in microbiome diversity as well as abundance were compared between AD‐related groups (diagnosis, amyloid status, and apolipoprotein E [ APOE ] ε4 carriage), and the co‐occurrence of the common gut microbiota features was analyzed among comparison groups. In addition, validation of gut microbiota composition that was differentially abundant between AD compared to CU was performed in a larger cohort of participants who are part of the AGMP. Furthermore, determination of microbiome functional differences was compared between people with AD dementia and CU individuals. Finally, associations between any differentially abundant gut microbiome features were examined in relation to CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies to identify the potential microbes and microbial pathways that relate to AD pathology. We hypothesized that gut microbiome alterations in composition and function would be present among people with AD dementia compared to CU, as well as associated with AD pathology. 2. METHODS 2.1. Participants Participants ( n = 232, Table 1 ) included in this study were recruited from the Wisconsin Alzheimer''s Disease Research Center (ADRC) Clinical Core and the Wisconsin Registry for Alzheimer''s Prevention (WRAP). 21 The WRAP study enrolls participants between 40 and 65 years of age at study entry, and the cohort is enriched for parental history of AD dementia. The Wisconsin ADRC Clinical Core enrolls participants who span the AD continuum. Participants undergo APOE genotyping using competitive allele‐specific polymerase chain reaction (PCR)–based KASP genotyping assays (LGC Genomics, Beverly, MA), 22 as well as biannual blood draws and assessments of cognitive function. A subset of the cohort also completed CSF collection and PET neuroimaging for AD biomarker analysis. Participants co‐enrolled in MARS had fecal samples collected for gut microbiome analysis. Participants completed questionnaires including medical history and diet, at the time of fecal sample collection. TABLE 1. Participant demographics at fecal sample collection by clinical diagnosis. Variable N Overall, N = 232 a Dementia‐AD, N = 24 a CU, N = 208 a p‐ value b Age 232 67 (±7) 71 (±7) c , * 66 (±7) 0.014 Sex 232 >0.9 Female 142 (61%) 16 (67%) 126 (61%) Male 90 (39%) 8 (33%) 82 (39%) Race 232 >0.9 Black or African American 10 (4.3%) 1 (4.2%) 9 (4.3%) White 222 (96%) 23 (96%) 199 (96%) APOE genotype 227 <.001 ε 2 ε 3 22 (9.7%) 0 (0%) 22 (11%) ε 3 ε 3 120 (53%) 5 (21%) c , ** 115 (57%) ε 2 ε 4 5 (2.2%) 0 (0%) 5 (2.5%) ε 3 ε 4 64 (28%) 11 (46%) 53 (26%) ε 4 ε 4 16 (7.0%) 8 (33%) c , *** 8 (3.9%) APOE ε4 carrier status 227 <.001 Negative (non‐carrier) 142 (63%) 5 (21%) 137 (67%) Positive (carrier) 85 (37%) 19 (79%) c , *** 66 (33%) Bristol stool type 232 0.3 1 16 (6.9%) 0 (0%) 16 (7.7%) 2 19 (8.2%) 3 (13%) 16 (7.7%) 3 35 (15%) 9 (38%) 26 (13%) 4 105 (45%) 6 (25%) 99 (48%) 5 37 (16%) 4 (17%) 33 (16%) 6 19 (8.2%) 2 (8.3%) 17 (8.2%) 7 1 (0.4%) 0 (0%) 1 (0.5%) BMI 232 28.2 (±5.4) 26.0 (±4.7) 28.4 (±5.4) 0.3 Amyloid status 145 <.001 0 (A−) 104 (72%) 0 (0%) 104 (78%) 1 (A+) 41 (28%) 12 (100%) c , *** 29 (22%) Medication status 232 >0.9 Medicated 213 (92%) 23 (96%) 190 (91%) Non‐medicated 19 (8.2%) 1 (4.2%) 18 (8.7%) Open in a new tab Note : The Bristol stool types are classification tools for the diagnosis of human feces form. Abbreviations: A, amyloid status; APOE , apolipoprotein E; BMI, body mass index; CU, cognitively unimpaired; Dementia‐AD, dementia due to Alzheimer''s disease; N, the number of individual participants. a Mean (±SD); n (%). b Kruskal–Wallis rank sum test; Pearson''s chi‐square test. c Significantly different AD dementia versus CU. * p < .05; ** p < .01, *** p < .001 ( p values are Bonferroni test corrected). RESEARCH IN CONTEXT Systematic review : Although mechanistic studies show the relationship between gut microbiome and Alzheimer''s disease (AD) pathology, human studies on the role of gut microbiome composition and function in AD pathology are limited. Interpretation : This study leveraged the human gut microbiome metagenomics and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) biomarkers and found that many of the gut microbiome compositional and functional features that are differentially abundant between AD and CU correlate with CSF biomarkers for AD and related pathologies. Future directions : Stratification of key human gut microbes and microbial pathways associated with AD and related pathologies may be utilized in other preclinical and clinical studies. Further research is needed on metabolomics, genomics, and exposomics that mediate gut microbes and related pathways specific to AD pathology. Participant diagnosis of AD was determined by a multidisciplinary consensus diagnostic panel and based on National Institute on Aging–Alzheimer''s Association (NIA‐AA) criteria. 23 , 24 Amyloid status was assessed using dynamic C 11‐Pittsburgh compound B (PiB) or F 18‐Florbetapir (FBP) PET scans and CSF biomarkers of amyloid beta (Aβ)42 and Aβ40 assayed via the Roche NeuroToolKit (NTK). Amyloid positivity (A+) was determined with PET imaging via PiB quantification (global cortical distribution volume ratio [DVR] >1.19), 25 FBP expert visual rating, and with CSF amyloid peptide ratio (Aβ42/Aβ40 <0.046). 26 Prior to admission into the WRAP, Wisconsin ADRC, and MARS studies, participants provided written informed consent in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. Study procedures were approved by the University of Wisconsin Institutional Review Board (IRB; WRAP and Wisconsin ADRC umbrella protocol ID: 2013‐0178; MARS protocol ID: 2015‐1121). An independent cohort of participants who are part of the AGMP and who were recruited from multiple NIA‐funded ADRCs across the United States ( n = 448, Table S1 ), was included for validation of differential abundance (DA) analysis. Clinical data used in the analysis were derived from the National Alzheimer''s Coordinating Center (NACC). AGMP participants from Wisconsin were excluded to ensure a unique validation sample. 2.2. Fecal sample collection and metagenomic data sequencing Fecal samples were collected as described previously. 4 Briefly, participants collected their stool samples at home with standardized fecal collection kits provided by the study team. Participants returned their samples in insulated containers, which were chilled with frozen gel packs. Returned samples were immediately weighed and scored on the Bristol stool scale. Fecal samples were then subsampled using sterile straws and stored at −80°C until processing. Fecal samples were processed for DNA extraction as described previously. 27 Briefly, samples were extracted using the MoBio PowerMag Soil DNA isolation kit with a magnetic bead plate. Extracted genomic DNA (gDNA) was quantified with Quant‐iT PicoGreen double‐stranded DNA (dsDNA) assay kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc.), and underwent a miniaturized KAPA HyperPlus library preparation using an iTru indexing strategy. 28 , 29 Libraries were normalized and pooled based on concentration, PCR‐cleaned, and then size‐selected (300‐700 bp) using the Sage Science PippinHT. Libraries were sequenced on an Illumina NovaSeq 6000 as a paired‐end 150‐cycle run at the University of California San Diego (UCSD) IGM Genomics Center as part of AGMP initiative. 30 2.3. Metagenomic data processing The metagenomic data processing was performed as described previously. 31 The sequence data were filtered for all adapters known to fastp (version 0.23.4) in paired‐end mode by explicitly specifying a known adapters file. 32 Fastp also removed sequences shorter than 45 nucleotides with a flag to filter the minimum length of each sequence. Each sample was then filtered against each genome in the human pangenome, 33 as well as both T2T‐CHM13v2.0, 34 and GRCh38, 35 using minimap2 36 (version 2.26‐r1175) with a short read mode. The data were first run in paired‐end mode, and then run in single‐end mode, per genome. Each successive run was converted from SAM to FASTQ using samtools 37 (version 1.17). The single‐end data were repaired using fastq_pair 38 (version 1.0) specifying a table size of 50 M. Compute support was provided with GNU Parallel 39 (version 20180222). Single‐end FASTQ output from samtools was split into R1 and R2 with a custom Rust program, with rust‐bio for parsing 40 (version 1.4.0). Data were multiplexed with sed and demultiplexed using a custom Python script. Shotgun sequencing data were then uploaded to and processed through Qiita 41 (Study ID 13663). Sequence adapter and host filtering were executed using qp‐fastp‐minimap2 version 2022.04. Subsequently, Woltka 42 version 0.1.4 (qp‐woltka 2022.09) with the Web of Life 2 database was employed for taxonomic and functional predictions. Genomic coverages were computed, and features with less than 25% coverage were excluded. 43 To enhance data quality, a prevalence filter using QIIME 2 v2023.5 44 was applied, eliminating features present in less than 10% of samples and samples with a sampling depth of less than 500,000 reads to mitigate the inclusion of erroneous and low‐quality reads. The resulting feature table was utilized for downstream analysis. 2.4. Biomarker measurements 2.4.1. CSF biomarkers CSF samples were collected via lumbar puncture in the morning after fasting for 8–12 h as described previously. 26 CSF biomarkers were measured using the NTK, a panel of exploratory robust prototype assays (Roche Diagnostics International Ltd, Rotkreuz, Switzerland). The following biomarkers were quantified on the Cobas e 601 module (Roche Diagnostics International Ltd): ​​Aβ42, phosphorylated tau‐181 (p‐tau181, total tau (t‐tau), S100 calcium binding protein B (S100B), and interleukin‐6 (IL‐6); and the remaining biomarkers were assayed on the Cobas e 411 analyzer: Aβ40, neurofilament light protein (NfL), neurogranin, α‐synuclein, glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP), chitinase‐3‐like protein 1 (YKL‐40), and soluble triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells 2 (sTREM2). Each biomarker was measured as markers of AD and related pathologies, which were amyloid pathology (Aβ42/Aβ40), tau pathophysiology (p‐tau181 and t‐tau), neurodegeneration (NfL), synaptic dysfunction and injury (neurogranin and α‐synuclein), inflammation (IL‐6), and glial activation (S100B, astrocytic activation [GFAP, YKL‐40], and microglial activation [sTREM2]). 2.4.2. PET biomarkers Dynamic 11 C‐PiB scans and late‐frame 18 F‐FBP scans were acquired using a Siemens ECAT EXACT HR+ tomograph as described previously. 45 , 46 11 C‐PiB DVRs were estimated with Logan graphical analysis and a cerebellar gray matter reference region. Global cortical DVR was calculated using eight Automated Anatomical Labeling Atlas 3–defined regions (anterior cingulate cortex, angular gyrus, middle temporal gyrus, posterior cingulate cortex, precuneus, supramarginal gyrus, superior temporal gyrus, and ventromedial prefrontal cortex); global DVR >1.16 was used to determine PiB A+ status. 18 F‐FBP images were evaluated by an expert visual rater (S.C.J.) to determine A+ status. 2.5. Statistical analysis The overall summary of schematic flow diagram of data layers and statistical approaches used for the main findings of the study is depicted in Figure S1 . 2.5.1. Participant demographics Statistical analysis of participant demographics was performed across clinical diagnoses using the Kruskal–Wallis rank sum test for continuous variables and Pearson''s chi‐square test for categorical variables. Analysis with multiple comparisons was corrected for multiple tests employing the Bonferroni correction method. Because multiple comparisons were made between groups for continuous variables (i.e., age and BMI), we applied Bonferroni correction to control for the family‐wise error rate. The corrected significance threshold for continuous variables was set to 0.05 divided by the number of continuous variables tested (α = 0.05/2 = 0.025). Similarly, for categorical variables (i.e., sex, race, APOE genotype, APOE ε4 allele, Bristol stool type, amyloid status, and medication status), we applied the Bonferroni correction. The significance threshold for categorical variables was set to 0.05 divided by the number of categorical variables tested (α = 0.05/7 = 0.00714). 2.5.2. Diversity analysis of the gut microbiota composition Gut microbiome diversities were calculated using QIIME 2 tools. 44 Alpha diversity indices including Shannon, 47 Evenness, 48 and Faith''s phylogenetic diversity (PD) 49 were calculated to determine the diversity of gut microbial species within each sample. The Shannon index estimates diversity by considering both species richness (the number of unique taxa) and the evenness (the distribution of abundances across species) of their abundance. Evenness focuses solely on the proportional distribution of taxa. Finally, Faith''s PD captures the phylogenetic differences among taxa. Beta diversity indices including Bray–Curtis dissimilarity, 50 and Weighted, 51 and Unweighted 52 UniFrac were calculated to determine the diversity of gut microbial communities between different samples for each AD‐related group. Bray–Curtis dissimilarity measures both the shared abundance of microbes between samples and the total number of microbes present. Although Unweighted UniFrac considers only the phylogenetic distances between microbes in the samples, Weighted UniFrac also accounts for their relative abundances. We adjusted p ‐values for multiple comparisons across the AD risk phenotypes using the Bonferroni correction (α = 0.05/3 = 0.0167). 2.5.3. DA analysis of the gut microbiome composition and function The Bayesian Inferential Regression for Differential Microbiome Analysis (BIRDMAn) pipeline was used for microbiome DA analysis. 53 Microbiome features (composition and function) were outcome variables and each AD‐related group (clinical diagnosis, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 status) was a predictor adjusting for covariates including age, sex, body mass index (BMI), Bristol type, medication status, and time between fecal collection and diagnosis/amyloid measurement. Log ratios of Top (features with greater abundances in AD, A+ status, and APOE ε4 carriage) and Bottom (features with lower abundances in AD, A+ status, and APOE ε4 carriage); ranked features of each AD‐related group (log[Top features/Bottom features]) were calculated and the Kruskal–Wallis test was used to test whether log ratios differed significantly across levels of each predictor, with p ‐values corrected using the Bonferroni adjustment method (α = 0.05/3 = 0.0167). 2.5.4. Co‐occurrence analysis of gut microbiota features across clinical diagnosis, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 status groups Co‐occurrence analysis was performed to find common gut microbiota features across different AD conditions. Venn diagrams were created to illustrate overlapping taxa associated with multiple AD‐related groups within each category—Top (more abundant in AD‐related groups) and Bottom (less abundant in AD‐related groups)—at each taxonomic level (phylum, family, genus, and species). 2.5.5. Comparative validation of gut microbiota composition in two distinct cohorts Log ratios of Top and Bottom features of each AD group (log[Top features/Bottom features]) were also calculated and analyzed using the Kruskal–Wallis test in MARS and validation cohorts from AGMP to identify similarities in microbiome features that differ between the AD and CU groups in both cohorts. Because the AGMP cohort had clinical diagnosis data available, these analyses were completed using diagnosis as a predictor in both the MARS and AGMP samples to validate our compositional findings in a second independent cohort. 2.5.6. Functional analysis of gut microbiome pathways in a clinical diagnosis group To identify microbiome‐related functional differences across the AD continuum, differentially abundant KEGG Orthology (KO) 54 pathways and their associated species were determined using BIRDMAn. 53 Microbiome features (KO pathways) were outcome variables and clinical diagnosis was a predictor adjusting for covariates including age, sex, BMI, Bristol type, medication status, and time between fecal collection and diagnosis measurement. Log ratios of Top and Bottom functional features (log[Top features/Bottom features]) were calculated and statistical significance was determined by the Mann–Whitney U test between AD and CU groups. Robust aitchison principal component analysis (RPCA) from Gemelli (version 0.0.10) was additionally used to identify sparse compositional KO 54 microbiome pathway features that are separated by sample variations. 55 RPCA is known to handle sparse and high‐dimensional datasets and is sensitive to datasets with outliers. 55 Microbiome datasets are often sparse and zero‐inflated; thus we employed RPCA to identify microbiome features that could explain the separation between groups. RPCA results were visualized with scores plots and biplots. Statistical analysis on RPCA was performed with permutational multivariate analysis of variance (PERMANOVA) 56 between groups. The PERMANOVA was conducted to compare two groups (AD and CU) across multiple microbiome pathway features, with the p ‐value corrected for multiple comparisons. 2.5.7. Association analysis of gut microbiome features and CSF biomarkers in a clinical diagnosis group To explore the relationships between key microbial compositional features and functional pathways identified via BIRDMAn and CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies, we applied an ordinary least squares (OLS) linear regression approach. Prior to fitting the linear regression model, CSF biomarkers were z‐score standardized using the StandardScaler (version 0.24.1) from the scikit‐learn library. 57 To address issues with sparse, compositional data, we used the multiplicative_replacement function from the scikit‐bio (version 0.5.7) skbio.stats.composition module to preprocess the metagenomics data. This function replaces zeros with small positive values, preserving the compositional nature of the data. Subsequently, a centered log ratio (CLR) transformation was applied to the metagenomics data to account for compositionality. Finally, OLS linear regression was performed on the microbial features that were previously found to be differentially abundant in AD versus CU, in relation to each CSF biomarker. Features'' β coefficients were visualized using heatmaps. To account for multiple comparisons, p ‐values were corrected using the Bonferroni method (composition: α = 0.05/2,706 = 1.848e‐5 and function: α = 0.05/396 = 1.263e‐4). All other statistical analyses were performed using Python libraries SciPy (1.13.0), 58 scikit‐learn (1.4.2), 57 and NumPy (version 1.26.4). 59 All figures were generated using Python libraries Matplotlib (version 3.6.0) 60 and Seaborn (version 0.11.2). 61 3. RESULTS 3.1. Participant demographics Participant characteristics are shown by clinical diagnosis in Table 1 . Participants were between 47 and 90 years of age, 96% were White (4% Black), and 61% were female. The mean age differed significantly in dementia AD versus CU ( p = 0.014). The percent ratio of APOE ε3/ε3 genotype was significantly lower and the percent ratio of APOE ε4/ε4 genotype was higher in dementia AD compared to CU ( p < 0.001). Amyloid positivity was higher in the AD dementia group ( p < 0.001). Statistical significance was determined after adjusting for multiple comparisons using the Bonferroni correction. 3.2. Diversity results in gut microbiota composition The alpha diversities did not differ significantly differ between CU and AD participants within the diagnosis group, between amyloid negative and positive individuals within the amyloid status group, or between individuals in the APOE ε4 negative/non‐carrier status ( APOE ε4−) group and those in the APOE ε4 positive/carrier status ( APOE ε4+) group after correcting for multiple testing (Figure S2 ). All beta diversity indices showed significant differences or a trend toward differences in species‐level composition between groups based on clinical diagnosis (Figure 1A–C ; Bray–Curtis: F = 2.35, P = 0.009, Weighted UniFrac: F = 4.07, p = 0.009, Unweighted UniFrac: F = 2.09, p = 0.08). There were no differences in beta diversity indices between amyloid‐positive and amyloid‐negative individuals (Figure 1D–F ; Bray–Curtis: F = 1.62, p = 0.19, Weighted UniFrac: F = 1.51, p = 1.00, Unweighted UniFrac: F = 0.88, p = 1.00). Individuals positive and negative for APOE ε4 demonstrated significant differences with the Bray–Curtis metric (F = 2.11, p = 0.009) and a trend toward differences with the Weighted UniFrac metric (F = 3.57, p = 0.06), but not with the Unweighted UniFrac metric (F = 1.26, p = 1.00) (Figure 1G–I ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Beta diversity metrics across AD‐related groups. (A–C) Differences in beta diversity metrics (Bray–Curtis, Weighted UniFrac, and Unweighted UniFrac, respectively) for individuals categorized by clinical diagnosis (CU vs AD dementia). (D–F) Metrics for amyloid status (negative vs positive). (G–I) Metrics across APOE ε4 status (negative vs positive). PC1 and PC2 axes represent the most variance in data. Each plot is color‐coded by the respective group, highlighting the spatial distribution and clustering based on the dissimilarity indices. PERMANOVA was used to determine statistical significance. All p ‐values are Bonferroni test corrected. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; CU, cognitively unimpaired; APOE , apolipoprotein E; PCs, principal coordinate; PERMANOVA, permutational multivariate analysis of variance. 3.3. Gut microbiota composition in clinical diagnosis, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 status groups DA analysis between AD and CU in the clinical diagnosis group showed a distinct gut microbiota composition at each taxonomic level (Figure 2A–D ). At the phylum level, the abundance of phylum Firmicutes_A was lower in AD compared to CU (Bottom), and the abundance of phyla Bacteroidota, Patescibacteria, and Fusobacteriota was higher in AD compared to CU (Top) (Figure 2A ). At the family level, families such as Clostridiaceae , Turicibacteraceae , Pasteurellaceae , Dialisteraceae , Enterococcaceae , and Ruminococcaceae were in the Bottom group and Fusobacteriaceae , Nanogingivalaceae , Gemellaceae , and Bacteroidaceae were in the Top group (Figure 2B ). At the genus level, genera including Clostridium_P , Ruminococcus , and Cryptobacteroides were in the Bottom group and Fusobacterium_A , Fusobacterium , Nanogingivalis , and Gemella were in the Top group (Figure 2C ). At the species level, species included in the Bottom group were Cryptobacteroides spp., Clostridium_P perfringens , Turicibacter sanguinis , Prevotella hominis , and Prevotella copri , and in the Top group were Fusobacterium_A mortiferum , Fusobacterium nucleatum , Fusobacterium animalis , Nanogingivalis gingivitcus , Collinsella stercoris , and Collinsella tanakaei (Figure 2D ). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab DA across AD‐related groups. Forest plots illustrate the DA of microbial features associated with AD‐related groups. (A–D) Contrasts in the abundance of various bacterial taxa at the (A) phylum, (B) family, (C) genus, and (D) species levels between AD dementia and CU. (E–H) The differences in abundance at these taxonomic levels between A+ and A− individuals. (I–L) The microbial features differentially abundant between the APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− groups. The x‐axes quantify the log ratio of presence between groups, with values above 1 indicating a higher abundance in the first‐mentioned group. Circles denote “Top” features, indicating a positive association with AD‐related groups (dementia diagnosis, amyloid positivity, and APOE ε4 positivity), whereas triangles denote “Bottom” features, indicating a negative association. The lines are color‐coded by unique phylum as labeled in the legend. DA analysis was conducted using BIRDMAn. A, amyloid status; AD, Alzheimer''s disease; APOE , apolipoprotein E; BIRDMAn, Bayesian Inferential Regression for Differential Microbiome Analysis; DA, differential abundance; CU, cognitively unimpaired. DA analysis between amyloid status positive (A+) and amyloid status negative (A−) showed distinct gut microbiota composition at each taxonomic level (Figure 2E–H ). At the phylum level, the abundance of phylum Firmicutes_C was lower in A+ compared to A− (Bottom), and the abundance of phyla Thermoplasmatota and Campylobacterota was higher in A+ compared to A− (Top) (Figure 2E ). At the family level, families such as Neisseriaceae , Anaeroplasmataceae , Turicibacteraceae , and Ruminococcaceae were in the Bottom group, and Nanogingivalaceae , Campylobacteraceae , and Coriobacteriaceae were in the Top group (Figure 2F ). At the genus level, genera including Prevotella , Eubacterium_R , and Lactococcus were in the Bottom group, and Fusobacterium , Nanogingivalis , and Acidaminococcus were in the Top group (Figure 2G ). At the species level, species included in the Bottom group were Dialister hominis , Prevotella copri , and Prevotella hominis , and in the Top group were Nanogingivalis gingivitcus , Collinsella tanakaei , and Fusobacterium animalis (Figure 2H ). DA analysis between APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− in APOE ε4 status showed distinct gut microbiota composition at each taxonomic level (Figure 2I–L ). At the phylum level, the abundance of phyla Firmicutes_A, Firmicutes_C, Spirochaetota, and Synergistota was lower in APOE ε4+ compared to APOE ε4− (Bottom), and the abundance of phylum Bacteroidota was higher in APOE ε4+ compared to APOE ε4− (Top) (Figure 2I ). At the family level, families such as Selenomonadaceae , Neisseriaceae , Pasteurellaceae , Turicibacteraceae , and Clostridiaceae were in the Bottom group, and Lactobacillaceae , Eubacteriaceae , and Bacteroidaceae were in the Top group (Figure 2J ). At the genus level, genera including Ruminococcus and Clostridium_P were in the Bottom group, and Enterobacter , Hafnia , and Lactobacillus were in the Top group (Figure 2K ). At the species level, species included in the Bottom group were Prevotella hominis , Dialister spp., and Ruminococcus spp., and in the Top group were Enterobacter hormaechei_A , Hafnia proteus , Collinsella stercoris , Enterobacter cloacae , and Collinsella tanakaei (Figure 2L ). Overall, there were significant count differences in microbiome features within each AD‐related group (diagnosis, amyloid, and APOE ε4) (Figure 3A,E,I ). Features that showed significant differences between AD and CU also varied between APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− (Figure 3A,C ), but not between A+ and A− (Figure 3B ). The differences in features observed between A+ and A− were also present in the comparison between AD and CU (Figure 3D,E ); however, no significant differences were found between APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− (Figure 3F ). Features that exhibited significant differences between APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− were also found to differ between AD and CU (Figure 3G,I ); however, no differences were observed between A+ and A− (Figure 3H ). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Comparative analysis of Top and Bottom features across AD‐related groups. Box plots comparing the distribution of log‐transformed ratios of differentially abundant microbial species in relation to diagnosis, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 status. (A–C) The log‐transformed ratios of microbes for diagnosis groups (AD vs CU). (D–F) The log‐transformed ratios of amyloid‐related microbes (A+ vs A−). (G–I) The log‐transformed ratios of APOE ε4‐related microbes ( APOE ε4+ vs APOE ε4−). Each column compares the CU and AD groups (A, D, and G), A+ and A− groups (B, E, and H), and APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− groups (C, F, and I). Each panel includes a Kruskal–Wallis test statistic and associated p ‐value with correction using the Bonferroni method, indicating the statistical significance of the differences observed. A, amyloid status; AD, Alzheimer''s disease; APOE , apolipoprotein E; CU, cognitively unimpaired. 3.4. Common gut microbiota features in clinical diagnosis, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 status groups At the phylum level, Bacteroidota co‐occurred between diagnosis and APOE ε4 in the Top group (features with greater abundances in AD‐related groups) (Figure 4A ). Firmicutes_A co‐occurred between diagnosis and APOE ε4, and Firmicutes_C co‐occurred between amyloid and APOE ε4 in the Bottom group (features with lower abundances in AD‐related groups) (Figure 4B ). FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Venn diagram of co‐occurrence of microbial features across AD‐related groups. The diagrams on the first row (A, C, E, and G) depict the Top (positively‐associated) differentially abundant features, whereas those on the second row (B, D, F, and H) show the Bottom (negatively‐associated) differentially abundant features. (A and B) Top and Bottom microbial phyla, respectively. These diagrams identify unique and shared phyla associated with each of the three AD‐related groups (AD diagnosis, amyloid presence, and APOE ε4 presence). (C and D) Top and Bottom microbial families, respectively. These diagrams highlight the family‐level microbial differences that correlate with AD‐related groups. (E and F) Top and Bottom microbial genera, respectively. These diagrams provide insight into the genus‐level microbial composition influenced by the specified AD‐related groups. (G and H) Top and Bottom microbial species, respectively. These diagrams detail the number of species that are unique and shared across the three AD‐related groups. Each diagram contains colored regions representing intersections between the groups: Red for dementia, green for amyloid, and blue for APOE ε4. The numbers within each segment of the diagrams indicate the count of microbial features unique to or shared between the conditions. Specific microbial features are listed in Table S2 . AD, Alzheimer''s disease; APOE , apolipoprotein E. In the Top group at the family level, the family Lactobacillaceae co‐occurred across all conditions, families Bacteroidaceae and Coprobacteraceae between diagnosis and APOE ε4, and families Nanogingivalaceae and Aerococcaceae co‐occurred between diagnosis and amyloid (Figure 4C ). In the Bottom group, UBA1829 and Turicibacteraceae co‐occurred across all conditions (diagnosis, amyloid, and APOE ε4), families CAG‐508 , CAG‐74 , Oscillospiraceae , Clostridiaceae , Pasteurellaceae , and CAG‐138 co‐occurred between diagnosis and APOE ε4, families Ruminococcaceae , Anaeroplasmataceae , and CAG‐312 co‐occurred between diagnosis and amyloid, and family Neisseriaceae co‐occurred between amyloid and APOE ε4 (Figure 4D ). In the Top group, the genus Veillonella_A co‐occurred across all conditions, and the following numbers of genera co‐occurred between each intersection, that is, diagnosis and APOE ε4: 7, diagnosis and amyloid: 6, and amyloid and APOE ε4: 4 (Figure 4E ). In the Bottom group, 13 genera co‐occurred across all conditions including Prevotella and Turicibacter , and the following numbers of genera co‐occurred between each intersection, that is, diagnosis and APOE ε4: 30, diagnosis and amyloid: 9, and amyloid and APOE ε4: 8 (Figure 4F ). In the Top group at the species level, 12 species including Bacteroides ovatus , Collinsella tanakaei , Prevotella corporis , and more co‐occurred across all conditions, and the following numbers of species co‐occurred between each intersection, that is, diagnosis and APOE ε4: 18, diagnosis and amyloid: 10, and amyloid and APOE ε4: 7 (Figure 4G ). In the Bottom group, 27 species including Ruminococcus_C callidus , Dialister succinatiphilus , Prevotella copri , and more co‐occurred across all conditions, and the following numbers of species co‐occurred between each intersection, that is, diagnosis and APOE ε4: 42, diagnosis and amyloid: 13, and amyloid and APOE ε4: 16 (Figure 4H ). All specific microbial taxa identified in this analysis are listed in Table S2 . 3.5. Validation of shallow‐shotgun data in the MARS with the ADRC dataset on gut microbiome composition The gut microbiome compositional features that were differentially abundant in the MARS cohort (Kruskal–Wallis = 31.81, p < 0.001; Figure 5A ) were also found to be differentially abundant in the larger AGMP validation cohort (Kruskal–Wallis = 5.59, p = 0.02; Figure 5B ). FIGURE 5. Open in a new tab Comparison of log‐transformed ratios of differentially abundant microbial features in CU and AD dementia across two cohorts. (A) Results from the MARS cohort. Box plots show the distribution of log‐transformed ratios of Top (features enriched in AD dementia relative to CU) to Bottom (features depleted in AD dementia relative to CU) microbial features in the MARS cohort for CU individuals (light blue) and AD dementia (dark blue) (Kruskal–Wallis = 31.81, p < 0.001). (B) The results from a larger validation cohort ( n = 448). Box plots present the distribution of log‐transformed ratios of Top to Bottom microbial features found in the AGMP cohort for CU individuals (light blue) and people with AD dementia (dark blue) (Kruskal–Wallis = 5.59, p = 0.02). Each point represents an individual sample, with the boxes indicating the IQR and the whiskers extending to 1.5 times the IQR. The horizontal line within each box denotes the median value. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; AGMP, Alzheimer Gut Microbiome Project; CU, cognitively unimpaired; IQR, interquartile range; MARS, Microbiome in Alzheimer''s Risk Study. 3.6. Gut microbiome functional pathways in a clinical diagnosis group Among gut microbiome pathway features, the abundance of pathways associated exclusively with either the AD group (Top; 15 pathways) or the CU group (Bottom; 6 pathways) showed significant differences ( p < 0.001) in the log ratios of Top/Bottom features between AD and CU (Figure 6A ). Furthermore, upon further exploration of the significantly different pathway features identified earlier, we found that among the gut microbiome taxonomic features associated with these pathways (36 features), the abundance of 28 features was higher in the AD group compared to the CU group (Top), whereas the abundance of 8 features was lower in the AD group compared to the CU group (Bottom) (Figure 6B ). For example, a pathway, naphthalene degradation, was one of the Bottom pathways, and microbes associated with this pathway were species Turicibacter sanguinis , Bifidobacterium angulatum , and Lactococcus lactis (Figure 6B ). Another example in the Top pathway is benzoate degradation, which is associated with microbes including Anaerostipes caccae , Bacteroides finegoldii , and Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron (Figure 6B ). All differentially abundant gut microbiome pathways between the AD and CU groups are listed in Table S3 . FIGURE 6. Open in a new tab Differentially abundant microbial pathways between AD and CU. (A) The distribution of the log ratios of Top/Bottom pathway features between AD (orange) and CU (blue) was shown in a box plot. Mann–Whitney U test was performed to determine statistical significance. Asterisks indicate a significant difference between AD (4.90) and CU (2.74) groups in the median of the log ratios of Top/Bottom pathway features ( p < 0.001). (B) A total of 36 differentially abundant features of microbial species and their corresponding pathways between AD and CU were displayed in a forest plot. Circles denote “Top” features, indicating a positive association with AD, whereas triangles denote “Bottom” features, indicating a negative association. The lines are color‐coded by unique species and their corresponding pathways. DA analysis was conducted using BIRDMAn. (C) RPCA on the clinical diagnosis group and a biplot of microbiome pathway features and their corresponding species. Each point represents an individual sample color‐coded by the respective group, with CU colored in blue and AD colored in orange. Vectors represent the direction (arrows) and magnitude (length) of the contribution of feature variables to the PCs. Vectors in red indicate Top features and vectors in green indicate Bottom features. PC1 and PC2 axes represent the most variance in data. Statistical analysis on RPCA was performed with PERMANOVA between AD and CU groups and the p value from the analysis was adjusted for multiple comparisons. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; CU, cognitively unimpaired; DA, differential abundance; BIRDMAn, Bayesian Inferential Regression for Differential Microbiome Analysis; PCs, principal components; PERMANOVA, permutational multivariate analysis of variance; RPCA, robust aitchison principal component analysis. RPCA on microbiome pathway features (36 features from DA analysis) visualized with a biplot indicated a significant separation between the clinical diagnosis group (AD and CU, P = 0.004) (Figure 6C ). Principal component (PC) 1 explained 52.3% of the variance across the participant sample and was predominated by microbial features more abundant in AD participants, whereas PC2 explained 24.7% of the variance and included several microbial features more abundant in CU individuals. In addition, microbial features that belonged to the Top group were indicated by the red vector directed toward many AD subjects indicated by the orange dots. Microbial features that belonged to the Bottom group were indicated by the green vector directed toward many CU subjects indicated by the blue dots. For instance, multiple pathways related to the species Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron were co‐localized with AD participants on the RPCA biplot, suggesting a potentially stronger association between the Top features and AD group (Figure 6C ). In contrast, pathways from species Turicibacter sanguinis , Bifidobacterium angulatum , and Lactococcus lactis , which belonged to the Bottom group, were co‐localized with the CU group, suggesting a potentially weaker association between the Bottom features and AD group (Figure 6C ). 3.7. Associations between gut microbiome compositional and functional features in clinical diagnosis group and CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies Overall, in the association between gut microbiome compositional features and CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies, most species that were more abundant in AD compared to CU individuals were positively associated with CSF biomarkers and with a lower Aβ42/Aβ40 ratio (Figure 7A ). Conversely, species that were less abundant in AD were generally negatively associated with CSF biomarkers and with higher Aβ42/Aβ40. FIGURE 7. Open in a new tab Heatmap illustrates the associations between gut microbiome compositional and functional features and CSF biomarkers in AD and related pathologies. (A) This heatmap represents the coefficients of regression analysis between the Top and Bottom 20 gut microbial species linked to dementia and CSF biomarkers in two groups: Top (more abundant in AD, denoted by the pink bar) and Bottom (less abundant in AD, denoted by the green bar). The color scale indicates the strength and direction of the associations, with red representing positive associations and blue representing negative associations. The intensity of the color corresponds to the magnitude of the coefficient. Listed on the left are the gut microbiome species that were identified as more or less abundant in AD through BIRDMAn. (B) The heatmap depicts the coefficients of regression analysis between the gut microbial pathways and CSF biomarkers. Coefficients are scaled by colors indicating the strength and direction of the associations, with red representing positive associations and blue representing negative associations. The intensity of the color corresponds to the magnitude (strength) of the coefficient. Microbial species and their associated pathway features are listed on the left of the plot and two groups (Top: more abundant in AD, denoted by the light pink bar; and Bottom: less abundant in AD or more abundant in CU, denoted by the light green bar) from DA analysis using BIRDMAn are displayed on the right of the plot. For both plots, the biomarkers listed along the bottom include amyloid pathology (Aβ42/Aβ40), tau pathophysiology (p‐tau181 and t‐tau), neurodegeneration (NfL), synaptic dysfunction and injury (neurogranin and α‐synuclein), inflammation (IL‐6), and glial activation (S100B, GFAP, YKL‐40, and sTREM2). It should be noted that a lower Aβ42/Aβ40 ratio is associated with a higher risk of having AD pathology, whereas higher levels of the rest of the CSF biomarkers are associated with a higher risk of having AD pathology. Asterisks indicate the level of statistical significance of the associations: *** p < 0.001, ** p < 0.01, and * p < 0.05 (​​uncorrected). Aβ, amyloid beta; AD, Alzheimer''s disease; BIRDMAn, Bayesian Inferential Regression for Differential Microbiome Analysis; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; CU, cognitively unimpaired; DA, differential abundance; GFAP, glial fibrillary acidic protein; IL, interleukin; NfL, neurofilament light protein; p‐tau, phosphorylated‐tau; S100B, S100 calcium binding protein B; sTREM2, soluble triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells 2; t‐tau, total‐tau; YKL‐40, chitinase‐3‐like protein 1. Species that were more abundant in AD were generally positively associated with CSF biomarkers. For example, Nanogingivalis gingivitcus , more abundant in AD, was positively associated with S100B, neurogranin, p‐tau181, and t‐tau. Fusobacterium_A mortiferum was positively correlated with neurogranin, p‐tau181, t‐tau, and α‐synuclein, and negatively associated with CSF amyloid (Aβ42/Aβ40). Fusobacterium animalis was positively associated with neurogranin, p‐tau181, t‐tau, and α‐synuclein. CAG‐1031 sp000431215 , a species within the Bacteroidetes phylum, was positively correlated with NfL, YKL‐40, p‐tau181, t‐tau, and α‐synuclein. Berrvella sp001552935 was positively associated with YKL‐40, sTREM2, t‐tau, and α‐synuclein. In addition, Lactobacillus acidophilus and Bifidobacterium vaginale were positively associated with S100B, CAG‐977 sp000434295 was associated with ‐p‐tau181 and t‐tau, Fusobacterium nucleatum was associated with t‐tau, Limosilactobacillus vaginalis and Collinsella tanakaei were associated with p‐tau181, and CAG‐177 sp000431775 was associated with IL‐6. Species that were less abundant in AD were generally negatively associated with CSF biomarkers. Species SFMI01 sp004556155 , Turicibacter sanquinis , and Dialister hominis , all within the Firmicutes phylum, were negatively associated with neurogranin, p‐tau181, t‐tau, and α‐synuclein. UBA5809 sp002417965 , another Firmicutes species, was also negatively associated with neurogranin, p‐tau181, t‐tau, and α‐synuclein, as well as sTREM2. UBA11524 sp000437595 , another Firmicutes species, was negatively associated with NfL. Cryptobacteroides sp900544195 and Cryptobacteroides sp000432515 , both species under phylum Bacteroidetes, were negatively associated with S100B. In the association between gut microbiome functional features and CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies, multiple microbial pathways we identified as more abundant in AD were also positively correlated with CSF biomarkers, whereas pathways more abundant in CU were negatively associated with CSF biomarkers (Figure 7B ). Microbial functional features in the Top group showed overall positive associations with CSF biomarkers, with the exception of Aβ42/Aβ40. Multiple Bacteroides spp. and their related pathways were positively associated with several CSF biomarkers including NfL, neurogranin, α‐synuclein, p‐tau181, and t‐tau. Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron and its associated pathways including benzoate degradation; ubiquinone and other terpenoid‐quinone biosynthesis; biosynthesis of various plant secondary metabolites; inositol phosphate metabolism; lipoic acid metabolism; biosynthesis of various antibiotics; beta‐alanine metabolism; carbapenem biosynthesis; neomycin, kanamycin, and gentamicin biosynthesis; polyketide sugar unit biosynthesis; ascorbate and aldarate metabolism; and taurine and hypotaurine metabolism had generally positive relationship with CSF biomarkers including NfL, YKL‐40, neurogranin, α‐synuclein, p‐tau181, and t‐tau, and negative relationship with IL‐6. Collinsella stercoris and polyketide sugar unit biosynthesis pathway showed a positive correlation with GFAP and sTREM2 and negative correlation with Aβ42/Aβ40. Collinsella stercoris and O‐antigen repeat unit biosynthesis pathway showed negative correlation with Aβ42/Aβ40. Microbial functional features in the Bottom group showed overall negative associations with CSF biomarkers for AD and related pathologies. Two pathways, ether lipid metabolism and alpha‐linolenic acid metabolism, related to Parabacteroides merdae , a species more abundant in the CU group, were associated with lower CSF Aβ42/Aβ40. It implies that higher abundance of these pathways of Parabacteroides merdae in CU individuals is associated with more brain amyloid. Moreover, bacterial chemotaxis pathway from Coprococcus eutactus was negatively associated with NfL and YKL‐40, and naphthalene degradation pathway from Turicibacter sanguinis was negatively associated with α‐synuclein, p‐tau181, and t‐tau. Initial analyses indicated statistically significant associations between microbiome features and CSF biomarkers; however, after applying the correction for multiple comparisons, these associations were no longer significant. 4. DISCUSSION In this study, we compared gut microbiome composition and function across AD‐relevant groups, including those with clinical diagnoses, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 carrier status. Our goal was to identify gut microbial features linked to AD and their relationships with CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies. Alpha diversity indices showed no significant differences across AD‐related groups. However, beta diversity, particularly Bray–Curtis dissimilarity, revealed significant compositional differences between AD and CU, and between APOE ε4+ and APOE ε4− groups, but not between A+ and A− individuals. These findings suggest that although the overall diversity within these groups remains relatively stable, the specific composition and abundance of microbial taxa differ significantly in the clinical diagnosis and APOE ε4 status groups. In addition, the Weighted UniFrac metric distinguished AD from CU, but not with the Unweighted UniFrac metric, suggesting that the relative abundance of major taxa rather than community membership is important in driving these differences. Prior studies have found that alpha and beta diversities do not differ between AD versus CU 62 , 63 and A+ versus A−, 64 whereas other studies showed significant differences in alpha and beta diversity indices across the AD continuum in humans 4 , 7 and mice. 65 , 66 , 67 The present study''s diversity analysis suggests that amyloid positivity alone may not strongly influence gut microbiota composition, whereas cognitive impairment (i.e., disease stage) may have a greater impact. The analysis also highlights the importance of considering different diversity metrics to capture the nuances of gut microbiome alterations in AD. AD participants had lower abundance of phylum Firmicutes; families Ruminococcaceae , Clostridiaceae , and Turicibacteraceae ; and genera Dialister , Clostridium , and Turicibacter ; and greater abundance of phylum Bacteroidetes (a.k.a. Bacteroidota); families Bacteroidaceae and Gemellaceae ; and genera Bacteroides and Gemella . This aligns with prior meta‐analyses on gut microbiome compositional differences in AD across 11 studies. 7 Results across studies are mixed; some have reported similar results, 6 , 68 whereas others have identified contradicting findings that fewer Bacteroidetes and Bacteroides and more Firmicutes were present in MCI or AD compared to CU. 69 , 70 Variability across studies may reflect differences in sample size, population heterogeneity, disease stage, sequencing methods, or confounders. 71 We mitigated methodological sources of bias through robust sequencing and analytical methodologies. Our validation of the DA analysis in a larger AGMP cohort largely recapitulates what we found in the smaller MARS sample, demonstrating consistent associations of specific taxa with AD diagnosis and suggesting that alterations in gut microbiome composition may be present in AD dementia. The DA and co‐occurrence analyses identified microbial signatures that may influence both amyloid pathology and APOE activity to contribute to AD pathology. To address the confounding effects of APOE ε4, we analyzed AD diagnosis, amyloid status, and APOE ε4 status separately, including co‐occurrence analyses. Studies have shown gut microbiota differences between CU A+ and CU A−. 8 , 11 , 72 One of the studies showed that the phylum Bacteroidetes was enriched in CU A+ participants and that genera Faecalibacterium and Bilophila , negatively correlated with the global brain Aβ burden, were enriched in CU A− participants. 11 The co‐occurrence of certain gut microbiome features across the three predictors suggests that while AD diagnosis, amyloid pathology, and APOE may each have distinct microbial associations, amyloid and APOE may act together, in part through gut microbial pathways, to contribute to AD development. Moving beyond the limitations of compositional analysis, this study further examined the functional pathways of gut microbiome in a clinical diagnosis group between AD and CU. Pathways exclusively associated with the AD group were largely driven by Bacteroides spp. ( B . finegoldii , B . thetaiotaomicron , and B . ovatus ). Multiple studies have reported the association between Bacteroides and AD. 4 , 73 , 74 , 75 Administration of B . fragilis to AD mice increased Aβ plaques, and inhibition of microglial clearance of Aβ was observed after introduction to B . fragilis . 76 Bacteroides has been reported as dominant in older adults compared to younger or healthier controls. 77 , 78 However, contrasting findings have been reported related to Bacteroides 8 , 79 and further strain‐specific studies are needed to understand the role of Bacteroides in AD pathology. Lipoic acid metabolism in B . thetaiotaomicron emerged as particularly significant due to its potential role in modulating oxidative stress and energy metabolism, both critical in AD pathophysiology. Mounting evidence has suggested that lipoic acid harbors antioxidant capacities, including abilities to scavenge free radicals, regenerate other antioxidants, and mitigate oxidative stress, essential factors in neurodegenerative diseases. 80 , 81 , 82 , 83 Lipoic acid is also crucial for energy metabolism, supporting mitochondrial function and glucose utilization essential for neuronal health, potentially protecting against energy deficits in AD. 84 , 85 , 86 Alterations in lipoic acid metabolism might reflect compensatory mechanisms in response to elevated oxidative stress or mitochondrial dysfunction in AD. This study emphasizes the metabolic role of Bacteroides spp. in AD pathophysiology, warranting further targeted research. Finally, to determine the biomarker implications of our findings, we highlighted the relationships of gut microbiome features with CSF biomarkers of AD and related pathologies. Key microbiome species, particularly Fusobacterium nucleatum , F . animalis , and Nanogingivalis gingivitcus , were enriched in AD and A+ individuals, correlating with increased tau pathophysiology (p‐tau181 and t‐tau) and/or synaptic dysfunction and injury (neurogranin and α‐synuclein). Notably, F . nucleatum , an oral bacterium linked to periodontal diseases and colorectal cancer, 87 , 88 produces lipopolysaccharides (LPS) that activate microglia and elevate proinflammatory cytokines, increasing Aβ accumulation and tau phosphorylation in mouse models. 89 , 90 Conversely, Dialister hominis , which co‐occurred between CU and A−, negatively correlated with AD pathology (p‐tau181), neuronal damage (t‐tau), and synaptic dysfunction and injury (neurogranin and α‐synuclein). These findings are similar to previous works, which identified genus Dialister (less in AD) to be more abundant in CU individuals. 91 , 92 Similar correlations were observed for Turicibacter sanguinis , which was less abundant in AD compared to CU individuals. In both animal models and human studies, T . sanguinis has been reported to be less abundant in AD compared to controls. 4 , 93 , 94 These results suggest protective microbiota features in CU individuals, while others may increase susceptibility to AD. Furthermore, biomarkers including neurodegeneration (NfL), synaptic dysfunction and injury (neurogranin and α‐synuclein), and tau pathophysiology (p‐tau181 and t‐tau) showed a positive relationship with B . thetaiotaomicron and its functions. Studies have linked B. thetaiotaomicron abundance with AD, as it was significantly higher in AD mice and related to poorer spatial learning. 95 Increased abundance of B . thetaiotaomicron was reported in AD participants. 96 , 97 However, in a non‐AD model, B . thetaiotaomicron was suggested to regulate enteric neuronal cell populations and neurogenic function. 98 The biosynthesis of antibiotics by B . thetaiotaomicron showed a positive relationship with biomarkers of AD and related pathologies (NfL, neurogranin, α‐synuclein, p‐tau181, and t‐tau). Long‐term antibiotic biosynthesis may lead to gut dysbiosis, potentially exacerbating neuroinflammatory processes and contributing to the pathophysiology of AD through mechanisms involving impaired neuronal health and exacerbated tau pathology. 99 , 100 , 101 , 102 These findings suggest that alterations in gut microbiome composition and function are associated with AD and related pathological markers measured in CSF. Although the observed correlations were weak and were no longer significant after multiple test corrections, the multifactorial nature of AD and coordinated activity across the gut microbiome suggest that the combined effects of intestinal bacteria could result in clinically meaningful resilience or susceptibility to AD pathology. The main limitation of our study is the small number of people with AD dementia relative to CU participants. Because clinical measures, fecal samples, and CSF biomarkers were all collected asynchronously, matching temporally proximal measurements of each data layer reduced the cohort''s sample size and therefore yielded low statistical power. This may explain weak CSF–microbiome correlations and lack of effect significance after multiple test corrections. Similarly, the sample had low representation among A+ and APOE ε4+ individuals, which could bias the magnitude of effects observed. Although we employed rigorous statistical methods to address this limitation, it is possible that some true effects were not detected or were underestimated. In addition, the modest effect sizes observed in certain analyses could reflect genuine biological variability but may also be influenced by limited power, warranting cautious interpretation. These factors may impact the generalizability of our findings, particularly to broader populations with differing demographic or clinical characteristics. Future studies will benefit from larger samples with these pathogenic characteristics, allowing for a clearer distinction between disease effects and symptom effects. Moreover, due to the cross‐sectional approach, we were unable to capture longitudinal effects of the microbiome on AD progression. Although the ADRC cohort facilitated the assessment of microbiome differences across populations, limited biomarker data precluded CSF–microbiome association testing in this group. In addition, due to the observational design of the study, these results are correlational; future mechanistic studies are needed to identify causal relationships between gut microbiome features and biomarkers for AD pathology. Finally, beyond person‐level biological factors that may influence microbial composition and function, future studies are needed to examine the environmental exposures and the exposome more broadly, as they may impact the development of AD through gut microbiome mechanisms. In conclusion, this study suggests that gut microbiome composition and function differ between people with AD dementia and CU individuals. These findings identify specific gut microbes and microbial pathways as potential targets for stratification, offering insights into their relationship with AD pathology and their potential role in developing microbiome‐based AD therapeutics. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT Dr. Kaddurah‐Daouk is an inventor of a series of patents on use of metabolomics for the diagnosis and treatment of central nervous system (CNS) diseases and holds equity in Metabolon Inc., Chymia LLC, and PsyProtix. Dr. Rob Knight is a scientific advisory board member and consultant for BiomeSense, Inc., and has equity and receives income. He is a scientific advisory board member and has equity in GenCirq. He is a consultant for DayTwo, and receives income. He has equity in and acts as a consultant for Cybele. He is a co‐founder of Biota, Inc., and has equity. He is a cofounder of Micronoma, and has equity and is a scientific advisory board member. The terms of these arrangements have been reviewed and approved by the University of California San Diego in accordance with its conflict of interest policies. Dr. Zetterberg has served on scientific advisory boards and/or as a consultant for Abbvie, Acumen, Alector, Alzinova, ALZPath, Amylyx, Annexon, Apellis, Artery Therapeutics, AZTherapies, Cognito Therapeutics, CogRx, Denali, Eisai, LabCorp, Merry Life, Nervgen, Novo Nordisk, Optoceutics, Passage Bio, Pinteon Therapeutics, Prothena, Red Abbey Labs, reMYND, Roche, Samumed, Siemens Healthineers, Triplet Therapeutics, and Wave; has given lectures in symposia sponsored by Alzecure, Biogen, Cellectricon, Fujirebio, Lilly, Novo 