INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5734,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1725845','Sports medicine and the accident and emergency medicine specialist.','Abernethy L, McNally O, MacAuley D, O''Neill S.','2002','paisley bedford macauley bjsm boath mucklow oracle boendermaker schuling meyboom zwierstra buckler bjsm mcnally macauley bjsm fentem tb01376 grimble hutson taggart bjsm martyn plunkett rendall mohtadi rowell bjsm stableforth halloran harrigan preregistration mcnally macauley bjsm vergeer watters elton','Abstract Background: Sport and exercise related injuries are responsible for about 5% of the workload in the accident and emergency (A&E) department, yet training in sports medicine is not a compulsory part of the curriculum for Higher Specialist Training. Aim: To determine how A&E medicine consultants and specialist trainees view their role and skill requirements in relation to sports medicine. Method: A modified Delphi study, consisting of two rounds of a postal questionnaire. Participants were invited to rate the importance of statements relating to the role and training of the A&E specialist in relation to sports injuries (six statements) and the need for knowledge and understanding of defined skills of importance in sports medicine (16 statements). Value of research: This provides a consensus of opinion on issues in sport and exercise medicine that have educational implications for A&E specialists, and should be considered in the curriculum for Higher Specialist Training. There is also the potential for improving the health care provision of A&E departments, to the exercising and sporting population. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (66.9 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Baldwin P. J., Paisley A. M., Brown S. P. Consultant surgeons'' opinion of the skills required of basic surgical trainees. Br J Surg. 1999 Aug;86(8):1078–1082. doi: 10.1046/j.1365-2168.1999.01169.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bedford P. J., Macauley D. C. Attendances at a casualty department for sport related injuries. Br J Sports Med. 1984 Jun;18(2):116–121. doi: 10.1136/bjsm.18.2.116. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Boath E., Mucklow J., Black P. Consulting the oracle: a Delphi study to determine the content of a postgraduate distance learning course in therapeutics. Br J Clin Pharmacol. 1997 Jun;43(6):643–647. doi: 10.1046/j.1365-2125.1997.00602.x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Boendermaker P. M., Schuling J., Meyboom-de Jong B. M., Zwierstra R. P., Metz J. C. What are the characteristics of the competent general practitioner trainer? Fam Pract. 2000 Dec;17(6):547–553. doi: 10.1093/fampra/17.6.547. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Buckler D. G. General practitioners'' training for, interest in, and knowledge of sports medicine and its organisations. Br J Sports Med. 1999 Oct;33(5):360–364. doi: 10.1136/bjsm.33.5.360. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cullen M., McNally O., Neill S. O., Macauley D. Sport and exercise medicine in undergraduate medical schools in the United Kingdom and Ireland. Br J Sports Med. 2000 Aug;34(4):244–245. doi: 10.1136/bjsm.34.4.244. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fentem P. H. ABC of sports medicine. Benefits of exercise in health and disease. BMJ. 1994 May 14;308(6939):1291–1295. doi: 10.1136/bmj.308.6939.1291. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Goodman C. M. The Delphi technique: a critique. J Adv Nurs. 1987 Nov;12(6):729–734. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2648.1987.tb01376.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Grimble S., Kendall I. G., Allen M. J. An audit of care received by patients injured during sporting activities. Arch Emerg Med. 1993 Sep;10(3):203–208. doi: 10.1136/emj.10.3.203. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Hutson M. Why sports injuries clinics? Br Med J (Clin Res Ed) 1987 Nov 7;295(6607):1210–1210. doi: 10.1136/bmj.295.6607.1210. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Jones R. S., Taggart T. Sport related injuries attending the accident and emergency department. Br J Sports Med. 1994 Jun;28(2):110–111. doi: 10.1136/bjsm.28.2.110. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Murphy A. W., Martyn C., Plunkett P. K., O''Connor P. Sports injuries and the accident and emergency department--ten years on. Ir Med J. 1992 Mar;85(1):30–33. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rendall E. O., Mohtadi N. G. Survey of competitive distance runners in Alberta: satisfaction with health care services with respect to running injuries. Clin J Sport Med. 1997 Apr;7(2):104–112. doi: 10.1097/00042752-199704000-00006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rowell S., Rees-Jones A. Injuries treated at a sports injury clinic compared with a neighbouring accident and emergency department. Br J Sports Med. 1988 Dec;22(4):157–160. doi: 10.1136/bjsm.22.4.157. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Stableforth P. G. Why sports injury clinics? Br Med J (Clin Res Ed) 1987 Oct 3;295(6602):799–799. doi: 10.1136/bmj.295.6602.799. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Stewart J., O''Halloran C., Harrigan P., Spencer J. A., Barton J. R., Singleton S. J. Identifying appropriate tasks for the preregistration year: modified Delphi technique. BMJ. 1999 Jul 24;319(7204):224–229. doi: 10.1136/bmj.319.7204.224. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Thompson B., McNally O., Neill S. O., Macauley D. What is a sports medicine specialist? A pilot study. Br J Sports Med. 2000 Aug;34(4):243–244. doi: 10.1136/bjsm.34.4.243. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Vergeer I. Family physicians and sports-injury care. Perceptions of coaches. Can Fam Physician. 1997 Oct;43:1755–1761. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Watters D. A., Brooks S., Elton R. A., Little K. Sports injuries in an accident and emergency department. Arch Emerg Med. 1984 Jun;1(2):105–111. doi: 10.1136/emj.1.2.105. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',25,'2025-10-15 11:31:30','2025-10-15 11:31:30');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5735,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9334735','Evolution of sedation management in the intensive care unit (ICU).','Stollings JL, Balas MC, Chanques G.','2022','kress titrating mends sedcom padis abcdef spice nonseda mends treggiari analgosedation muellejans rass mends rass sedcom abcdef abcdef shehabi chanques padis padis padis padis spice mends spice mends nonseda analgosedation abcdef reengage abcdef 134_2022_6806_moesm1_esm rass jls mcb mcb r01hl14678 aspen puntillo gelinas dasta kress 0b013e3182783b72 skrobik gelinas slooter weinhouse nunnally rochwerg e825 e873 jacobi coursin riker wittbrodt chalfin masica bjerke coplin egol greenbaum jacobi nasraway schein spevetz shehabi reade seppelt weisbrodt 0522oc weinert zawistowski bemis berney bienvenu 0b013e318232da75 pun balas byrum abcdef chanques conseil prades belafia cisse shehabi kadiman reade nejmoa1904710 mailloux anzueto hoskins pun orun nejmoa2024922 nedergaard oxlund wian ytrebo kroken korkmaz lauridsen nonsedation nejmoa1906759 pun badenes heras calle orun linville hinojal olmedillo cueva chanques gelinas jabaudon absalom balas burry colantuoni coursin scepter meiser wallenborn guenther bracht schwarzkopf knafelj faltlhauser 134_2022_6806_moesm1_esm rass','Intensive care unit (ICU) sedation practices have dramatically changed over the last 20 years [ 1 – 4 ]. Historically, patients were often deeply sedated to “let them rest” [ 4 ]. Strong evidence indicates these practices are dangerous and associated with poor outcomes [ 5 , 6 ]. Current practice is to use minimum sedation and wake patients up daily while assuring patient comfort [ 2 ] (Supplementary Fig. 1). ICU sedation management has changed over the years (Fig. 1 ). The first set of guidelines on sedation management were published in 1995. These guidelines recommended using midazolam or propofol for short-term sedation and lorazepam for long-term sedation [ 4 ]. The Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Sustained Use of Sedatives and Analgesics in the Critically Ill Adult (SAG) [ 3 ] were published in 2002. Sedation guidelines were recommended based off data by Brook et al. in 1999 showing protocol-driven sedation resulted in decreased time on ventilation [ 3 ]. SAG also suggested to set a daily sedation goal or provide daily interruption of sedation. This recommendation was based off data from Kress et al. showing that a spontaneous awakening trial (SAT) resulted in decreased time on ventilation [ 3 ]. In terms of medications, SAG recommended propofol if patients needed rapid neurologic assessments and midazolam for short-term sedation. Lorazepam was recommended as sedation for most patients; no mention of titrating lorazepam to light levels of sedation occurred. Statements indicating paradoxical agitation and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) with light sedation were included. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab This is timeline of selected landmark trials involved in the evolution of sedation in the ICU. References for this figure are listed from left to right starting at the top in the Electronic Supplementary Material 2. IV , intravenous; SAT , spontaneous awakening trial; SCCM , Society of Critical Care Medicine; SAG , The Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Sustained Use of Sedatives and Analgesics in the Critically Ill Adult; SBT , spontaneous breathing trial; MENDS , Maximizing the Efficacy of Sedation and Reducing Neurological Dysfunction; SEDCOM , Safety and Efficacy of Dexmedetomidine versus Midazolam; PICS , Post Intensive Care Syndrome; PAD , The Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Pain, Agitation, and Delirium in Adult ICU Patients; ABCDE , Awakening and Breathing Coordination, Delirium monitoring/management, and Early exercise/mobility; SOS , Immediate interruption of sedation compared with usual sedation care in critically ill postoperative patients; PADIS , The Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Management of Pain, Agitation/Sedation, Delirium, Immobility, and Sleep Disruption in Adult Patients in the ICU; ABCDEF , “A” for Assessment, Prevention, and Manage pain; “B” for Both Spontaneous Awakening Trials and Spontaneous Breathing Trials; “C” for Choice of Analgesia and Sedation; “D” for Delirium Assess, Prevent, and Manage; "E" for Early Mobility and Exercise; and “F” for Family Engagement and Empowerment; SPICE 3 , Sedation Practice in Intensive Care Evaluation; NONSEDA , No Sedation or Light Sedation in Critically Ill, Mechanically Ventilated Patients; ARDS , Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome; MENDS 2 , Maximizing the Efficacy of Sedation and Reducing Neurological Dysfunction and Mortality in Sepsis The Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Pain, Agitation, and Delirium in Adult ICU Patients (PAD) were published in 2013 [ 1 ]. PAD recommended sedatives be titrated to a light level of sedation unless contraindicated. This recommendation was based on studies like Girard et al. which showed that daily SAT coordinated with daily spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) resulted in better clinical outcomes [ 1 ] and Treggiari et al. which showed that patients receiving deep sedation were more likely to have PTSD [ 1 ]. The concept of “analgosedation” was introduced in the early 2000s by Muellejans et al. [ 1 ] using remifentanil based sedation, while Strom et al. implemented a “no sedation” strategy using intermittent morphine [ 1 ]. The Richmond Agitation Sedation Scale (RASS) and Sedation Agitation Scale (SAS) were determined to be the most accurate level of arousal assessment tools. PAD suggested non-benzodiazepines may be preferred to improve clinical outcomes. Carson et al. found that patients randomized to propofol had shorter time on ventilation versus patients that received intermittent lorazepam [ 1 ]. Maximizing the Efficacy of Sedation and Reducing Neurological Dysfunction (MENDS) showed that patients that received dexmedetomidine had less delirium and coma and were more likely to be within 1 point of target RASS versus lorazepam [ 1 ]. The Safety and Efficacy of Dexmedetomidine versus Midazolam (SEDCOM) study showed that patients that received dexmedetomidine spent less time on ventilation and had less delirium [ 1 ]. The ICU Liberation Bundle (ABCDEF: “A” Assessment, Prevention, and Manage pain; “B” Both SAT SBT; “C” Choice of Analgesia and Sedation; “D” Delirium Assess, Prevent, and Manage; "E" Early Mobility and Exercise; and “F” Family Engagement and Empowerment) was developed to implement PAD [ 7 ]. ABCDEF has now been studied in over 25,000 patients showing improvement in clinical outcomes. In 2012, Post Intensive Care Syndrome (PICS) was defined as patients that have physical, cognitive, and/or psychological impairment post critical illness [ 6 ] which is often secondary to delirium and increased time in the ICU. In 2012, Shehabi et al. found that early deep sedation resulted in increased time on ventilation and mortality [ 5 ]. In postoperative patients without severe acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), Chanques et al. reported that the immediate interruption of sedation compared to moderate sedation was associated better clinical outcomes [ 8 ]. The Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Management of Pain, Agitation/Sedation, Delirium, Immobility, and Sleep Disruption in Adult Patients in the ICU (PADIS) were published in 2018 [ 2 ]. PADIS suggested light sedation. PADIS suggested using propofol in cardiac patients and either propofol or dexmedetomidine in medical or non-cardiac surgical patients. Since publication of PADIS, Sedation Practice in Intensive Care Evaluation (SPICE 3) [ 9 ] and Maximizing the Efficacy of Sedation and Reducing Neurological Dysfunction and Mortality in Sepsis (MENDS 2) [ 10 ] have been published showing no difference in dexmedetomidine versus usual care (SPICE III) or propofol (MENDS 2) in clinical outcomes. No Sedation or Light Sedation in Critically Ill, Mechanically Ventilated Patients (NONSEDA) randomized patients to no sedation versus light sedation finding no difference in 90 day mortality or ventilator/ICU free days [ 11 ]. Greater than 503 million cases of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) have been reported as of April 2022. Patients with severe COVID-19 can develop ARDS sometimes require deep sedation placing patients at risk for delirium and coma. The COVID-D study found that 64% and 71% of patients received benzodiazepines and propofol for a median of 7 days respectively [ 12 ]. In patients with ARDS, choosing the best ventilator setting should be prioritized over increasing analgosedation [ 13 ]. However, it is clear that light sedation is not possible in most with severe ARDS or during neuromuscular blockade. This highlights the importance of daily interprofessional discussion of the ABCDEF Bundle to ensure that an individualized approach is taken toward sedation management and utilization of light sedation occurs again when possible. High quality evidence is lacking in sedation research secondary to heterogeneity of data and resistance to practice change. Future directions include using a standardized approach allowing for meaningful comparisons between studies [ 14 ]. Additionally, use of inhaled anesthetics for sedation of critically ill patients is promising [ 15 ]. This paper provided an overview of how ICU sedation practices have evolved over the last 20 years. Contemporary ICU sedation practices include light levels of sedation, SAT, and use of non-benzodiazepines. The current COVID-19 pandemic has placed unprecedented demands on ICU workforce and challenged sedation practices [ 13 ]. It is urgent that clinicians reengage bundled based strategies such as the ABCDEF Bundle to promote liberation from the ventilator and promote recovery and survivorship. Supplementary Information Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. 134_2022_6806_MOESM1_ESM.pdf (57.9KB, pdf) Supplementary file1 Approach to Sedation Management in the Intensive Care Unit. This shows an approach to using sedation in patients in the Intensive Care Unit. RASS=Richmond Agitation Sedation Scale, SAS=Sedation Agitation Scale, SAT=Spontaneous Awakening Trial, SBT=Spontaneous Breathing Trial (PDF 57 kb) Supplementary file2 (PDF 89 kb) (88.3KB, pdf) Conflict of interests JLS is the Vice Co-Chair of the Society of Critical Care Medicine’s most recent Pain, Agitation, Delirium, Immobility and Sleep Clinical Practice Guideline Committee. MCB is the Co-Chair of the Society of Critical Care Medicine’s most recent Pain, Agitation, Delirium, Immobility and Sleep Clinical Practice Guideline Committee. GC is the Analgesia Lead of the Society of Critical Care Medicine’s most recent Pain, Agitation, Delirium, Immobility and Sleep Clinical Practice Guideline Committee Funding MCB currently receives research support from the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute of the National Institutes of Health under award number R01HL14678-01. She has received past honoraria from the Society of Critical Care Medicine for her work related to the ICU Liberation Collaborative. GC received fees for speaker (Orion Pharma, Aspen Medical) and for expert board (Orion Pharma). Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Barr J, Fraser GL, Puntillo K, Ely EW, Gelinas C, Dasta JF, Davidson JE, Devlin JW, Kress JP, Joffe AM, et al. Clinical practice guidelines for the management of pain, agitation, and delirium in adult patients in the intensive care unit. Crit Care Med. 2013;41(1):263–306. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0b013e3182783b72. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Devlin JW, Skrobik Y, Gelinas C, Needham DM, Slooter AJC, Pandharipande PP, Watson PL, Weinhouse GL, Nunnally ME, Rochwerg B, et al. Clinical practice guidelines for the prevention and management of pain, agitation/sedation, delirium, immobility, and sleep disruption in adult patients in the ICU. Crit Care Med. 2018;46(9):e825–e873. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000003299. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Jacobi J, Fraser GL, Coursin DB, Riker RR, Fontaine D, Wittbrodt ET, Chalfin DB, Masica MF, Bjerke HS, Coplin WM, et al. Clinical practice guidelines for the sustained use of sedatives and analgesics in the critically ill adult. Crit Care Med. 2002;30(1):119–141. doi: 10.1097/00003246-200201000-00020. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Shapiro BA, Warren J, Egol AB, Greenbaum DM, Jacobi J, Nasraway SA, Schein RM, Spevetz A, Stone JR. Practice parameters for intravenous analgesia and sedation for adult patients in the intensive care unit: an executive summary. Society of Critical Care Medicine. Crit Care Med. 1995;23(9):1596–1600. doi: 10.1097/00003246-199509000-00021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Shehabi Y, Bellomo R, Reade MC, Bailey M, Bass F, Howe B, McArthur C, Seppelt IM, Webb S, Weisbrodt L, et al. Early intensive care sedation predicts long-term mortality in ventilated critically ill patients. Am J Respir Crit Care Med. 2012;186(8):724–731. doi: 10.1164/rccm.201203-0522OC. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Needham DM, Davidson J, Cohen H, Hopkins RO, Weinert C, Wunsch H, Zawistowski C, Bemis-Dougherty A, Berney SC, Bienvenu OJ, et al. Improving long-term outcomes after discharge from intensive care unit: report from a stakeholders'' conference. Crit Care Med. 2012;40(2):502–509. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0b013e318232da75. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Pun BT, Balas MC, Barnes-Daly MA, Thompson JL, Aldrich JM, Barr J, Byrum D, Carson SS, Devlin JW, Engel HJ, et al. Caring for critically ill patients with the ABCDEF bundle: results of the ICU liberation collaborative in over 15,000 adults. Crit Care Med. 2019;47(1):3–14. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000003482. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Chanques G, Conseil M, Roger C, Constantin JM, Prades A, Carr J, Muller L, Jung B, Belafia F, Cisse M, et al. Immediate interruption of sedation compared with usual sedation care in critically ill postoperative patients (SOS-Ventilation): a randomised, parallel-group clinical trial. Lancet Respir Med. 2017;5(10):795–805. doi: 10.1016/S2213-2600(17)30304-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Shehabi Y, Howe BD, Bellomo R, Arabi YM, Bailey M, Bass FE, Bin Kadiman S, McArthur CJ, Murray L, Reade MC, et al. Early sedation with dexmedetomidine in critically ill patients. N Engl J Med. 2019;380(26):2506–2517. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1904710. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Hughes CG, Mailloux PT, Devlin JW, Swan JT, Sanders RD, Anzueto A, Jackson JC, Hoskins AS, Pun BT, Orun OM, et al. Dexmedetomidine or propofol for sedation in mechanically ventilated adults with sepsis. N Engl J Med. 2021;384(15):1424–1436. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa2024922. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Olsen HT, Nedergaard HK, Strom T, Oxlund J, Wian KA, Ytrebo LM, Kroken BA, Chew M, Korkmaz S, Lauridsen JT, et al. Nonsedation or light sedation in critically ill, mechanically ventilated patients. N Engl J Med. 2020;382(12):1103–1111. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1906759. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Pun BT, Badenes R, Heras La Calle G, Orun OM, Chen W, Raman R, Simpson BK, Wilson Linville S, Hinojal Olmedillo B, Vallejo de la Cueva A, et al. Prevalence and risk factors for delirium in critically ill patients with COVID-19 (COVID-D): a multicentre cohort study. Lancet Respir Med. 2021;9(3):239–250. doi: 10.1016/S2213-2600(20)30552-X. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Chanques G, Constantin JM, Devlin JW, Ely EW, Fraser GL, Gelinas C, Girard TD, Guerin C, Jabaudon M, Jaber S, et al. Analgesia and sedation in patients with ARDS. Intensive Care Med. 2020;46(12):2342–2356. doi: 10.1007/s00134-020-06307-9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Ward DS, Absalom AR, Aitken LM, Balas MC, Brown DL, Burry L, Colantuoni E, Coursin D, Devlin JW, Dexter F, et al. Design of clinical trials evaluating sedation in critically ill adults undergoing mechanical ventilation: recommendations from Sedation Consortium on Endpoints and Procedures for Treatment, Education, and Research (SCEPTER) Recommendation III. Crit Care Med. 2021;49(10):1684–1693. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000005049. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Meiser A, Volk T, Wallenborn J, Guenther U, Becher T, Bracht H, Schwarzkopf K, Knafelj R, Faltlhauser A, Thal SC, et al. Inhaled isoflurane via the anaesthetic conserving device versus propofol for sedation of invasively ventilated patients in intensive care units in Germany and Slovenia: an open-label, phase 3, randomised controlled, non-inferiority trial. Lancet Respir Med. 2021;9(11):1231–1240. doi: 10.1016/S2213-2600(21)00323-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 134_2022_6806_MOESM1_ESM.pdf (57.9KB, pdf) Supplementary file1 Approach to Sedation Management in the Intensive Care Unit. This shows an approach to using sedation in patients in the Intensive Care Unit. RASS=Richmond Agitation Sedation Scale, SAS=Sedation Agitation Scale, SAT=Spontaneous Awakening Trial, SBT=Spontaneous Breathing Trial (PDF 57 kb) Supplementary file2 (PDF 89 kb) (88.3KB, pdf)',1103,'2025-10-15 11:31:31','2025-10-15 11:31:31');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5736,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10145860','A bibliometric analysis of obstructive sleep apnea and anesthesia.','Öner Ö, Ecevit MC, Gökmen AN.','2023','hypercarbia apneas hypopneas pauses hypopnea dilator narcotics coauthored wosviewer girish frances frances auckley chadwick mcnicholas loud snorers cala kimoff memtsoudis stavros frances mahmoud luscri deegan mcnicholas sultana adrian byotorhinolaryngology anestesiologia canadien anesthesie anestesiologica paracelsus vincents wosviewer wosviewer snoring impulses mechanoreceptors mechanoreceptors hiremath narcotic pneumatic splint snoring terror adenotonsillar ozlem oner necati gokmen ozlem oner necati gokmen ozlem oner cenk ecevit ozlem oner ozlem oner necati gokmen ozlem oner cenk ecevit necati gokmen necati gokmen ozlem oner necati gokmen hypopnea oner ecevit gokmen e32993 cenk ecevit mcenk ecevit necati gokmen necatigokmen auckley chowdhuri benjafield ayas budhiraja aasm pomerantz kaw pasupuleti mokhlesi huamani alcaide landreneau leydesdorff rafols pagel hudetz pagel hudetz choudhri oldid citefactor petrini buccioli hiremath hillman memtsoudis cozowicz nagappa hillman respirology subramani kushida littner hirshkowitz bilevel rotenberg murariu flattened engleman hypopnoea sahs antic heeley lorenzi strollo respirology guilleminault schwengel dalesio stierer posner domino araslanova paradis rotenberg butow duolikun shariq hillman bahammam','Abstract To conduct a bibliographic analysis of obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) which has reached epidemic proportions and is a frequent, unknown, and important cause of perioperative morbidity and mortality, by examining the internationally most cited articles. For OSA, the most cited articles in the field of anesthesiology and reanimation, appropriate access terms were compiled and combined, and related publications were searched using the Thompson Reuters Web of Science Citation Indexing search engine. A total of 79 journal publications were found on OSA and anesthesia, with an average of 14.86 citations per article. The most cited publication was the “Society for Ambulatory Anesthesia Consensus Statement on Preoperative Selection of Adult Patients with Obstructive Sleep Apnea Scheduled for Ambulatory Surgery” published in the journal Anesthesia and Analgesia and was conducted by Joshi et al. It was found that 38 of the 79 studies reached as a result of the search were articles, and the average number of citations was 21.13. The Hirsch index of these articles, which were cited 803 times in total, was 15. A total of 31 articles (81.57%) were cited at least once, while the remaining 7 articles (18.43%) were not cited at all. The majority of the articles obtained are from the research fields of anesthesiology (n = 20; 52.63%), followed by otorhinolaryngology (n = 5; 13.15%), pediatrics (n = 5; 13.15%), respiratory system (n = 5; 13.15%), internal medicine (n = 4; 10.52%), and the rest were in various fields. Publications on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia” have increased rapidly in the last decade. Anesthesia management and airway safety, patient management, including pain control in the postoperative period, and noninvasive mechanical ventilation treatment methods, such as continuous positive airway pressure, are hot topics. Keywords: airway safety, anesthesia, bibliometric analysis, obstructive sleep apnea 1. Introduction Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a sleep-related respiratory disorder in which the upper airway (UA) is partially or completely obstructed during sleep. It is characterized by hypoxia, hypercarbia, disrupted night sleep, daytime sleepiness, increased sympathetic activity, various medical complications, and higher mortality expectations. [ 1 ] OSA is highly associated with obesity and is an important public health problem estimated to affect 1 billion adults aged 35 to 69 in years world. [ 2 ] According to the American Academy of Sleep Medicine, the number of apneas and hypopneas is defined as at least 10 seconds of respiratory pauses per hour (apnea-hypopnea index [AHI]); mild OSA can be classified as 5/h, moderate OSA 5 to 15/h, and severe OSA as 30 and above/hour. [ 3 ] The prevalence of moderate to severe OSA is approximately 10 to 20% in the population. [ 2 ] Different underlying mechanisms play a role in the pathogenesis of OSA, such as impairment of the activity of the UA dilator muscles, arousal threshold, and control of respiration, which vary from person to person. [ 3 ] Since the onset of the disease is insidious, most individuals are unaware of its symptoms. [ 4 ] Therefore, almost a quarter of patients admitted to the hospital for elective surgery have OSA, and >80% of these patients are not diagnosed and treated during surgery. [ 5 ] Surgical patients with OSA are at risk for cardiovascular and cerebrovascular diseases, metabolic disorders, and impaired neurocognitive functions. [ 6 ] Preoperative hypoxia due to apnea of an unknown cause is often misdiagnosed as sudden cardiac arrest due to other causes. The risk of cardiopulmonary side effects was 2 to 3 times higher in this patient group. [ 5 ] In addition, the perioperative period is risky for patients with OSA due to the negative effects of anesthetic agents, narcotics, and sedatives on OSA and possible complications during patient transfer to intensive care units. [ 4 ] Bibliometrics is a quantitative, interdisciplinary science that uses mathematical and statistical methods to evaluate scientific research. [ 7 ] In this branch of science, citation frequency is applied to reflect the role of articles in academic communication, and the higher the citation frequency of an article, the greater its impact in the related field. [ 8 ] The Web of Science (WOS) online database is a non-free database for its users, which includes journal articles from all major disciplines since 1900, and also provides analysis tools to generate representative figures. [ 9 ] The Hirsch index (H-index) is a valuable measure for evaluating academic productivity. [ 10 ] The index can also be applied to the academic productivity of a department, university, country, scientist, and journal. [ 11 ] Impact factor (IF) is a measure of success, calculated by examining the citations of articles in scientific journals and showing the impact of journals in their field. [ 12 ] The aim of this research was to conduct a bibliographic analysis of OSA, which has reached epidemic proportions and is a frequent, unknown, and important cause of perioperative morbidity and mortality, by examining the internationally most cited articles with aspects of authors, journals, countries, and their topics through the Thompson Reuters WOS Citation Indexing (SCI) database in the field of anesthesiology and reanimation. 2. Methodology 2.1. Search strategy The Thompson Reuters SCI citation indexing database was searched on June 4, 2021, after receiving approval from the university ethics committee (approval date: May 27, 2021/approval no. 2021/16-20). The requirement for informed consent was waived due to the study design. The reason for searching the WOS (Clarivate Analytics, USA) database in our study is that it includes various bibliometric indicators and literature from different disciplines. [ 13 ] In the current study, citation trends and hotspots of publications on “OSA and anesthesia” were evaluated. The data collection and analysis process is detailed below. 2.2. Strategy MeSH terms As the main hypothesis of this research was to determine the number of internationally most cited articles, citation trends, and hotspots in the field of anesthesiology and reanimation of OSA, appropriate access terms were compiled, combined, and related publications using the Thompson Reuters SCI search engine. Searched “Obstructive Sleep Apnea,” “OSA,” “Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Anesthesia,” “OSAS,” “OSA and Anesthesia,” “Obstructive Sleep Apnea Syndrome and Anesthesia” and the keywords “OSAS And Anesthesia” were used as search terms. 2.3. Data collection and analysis The keywords used in the search were limited to English. The database search date for the published articles was set from 1970 to 2021. A total of 79 articles were identified. Only articles were included, and other types of publications were excluded. Performance analysis, collaboration analysis, and scientific mapping were performed on authors, citation frequency, author collaboration, and keywords for scientific analysis to gain insights into any scientific field. 2.4. Performance analysis In the performance analysis, data on the growth of publications, the most active countries and institutions, the most cited journals, and the mapping of publications and keywords were analyzed to measure the main performance of published research by quantifying the research area. At this stage, citation analysis was performed. The Journal Citation Reports dataset was used to determine 2021 IF of each journal. [ 14 ] The H-index and IF were used as indicators of the effect of publications. 2.5. Collaboration analysis To analyze the scientific cooperation of publications, scientific collaboration analysis was carried out to highlight the relationships between influential actors, namely authors, and countries. This results in an analysis network in which the nodes of the network are authors or countries. Links to the network were provided by the authors and countries that coauthored this article. 2.6. Scientific mapping Scientific mapping is a representative mapping system of how “disciplines, fields, specialties, and individual articles or authors” relate to each other. In our study, the most cited authors, scientific collaborations, and common word analysis were conducted using the Wosviewer method. Mapping figures for relationship created with Vosviewer version 1.6.15 ( https://www.vosviewer.com/ ). 3. Results 3.1. Performance analysis The Thomson Reuters SCI database was scanned on June 4, 2021. A total of 79 journal publications were found on OSA and anesthesia, with an average of 14.86 citations per article. The time interval for the publication search was between the date of the first publication on the subject and the time interval of the search, 1987 to 2021. The most cited publication was the “Society for Ambulatory Anesthesia Consensus Statement on Preoperative Selection of Adult Patients with Obstructive Sleep Apnea Scheduled for Ambulatory Surgery” published in the journal Anesthesia and Analgesia and was conducted by Joshi et al (Table 1 ). When publications published on this subject were analyzed, it was determined that the first 3 types were articles, meeting abstracts, and reviews (Table 2 ). Table 1. Top 10 most cited articles. Citation rate rank Citation First author Senior author Title Corresponding author institution Country 1 140 Joshi, Girish P. Chung, Frances Society for Ambulatory Anesthesia consensus statement on preoperative selection of adult patients with obstructive sleep apnea scheduled for ambulatory surgery University of Texas USA 2 130 Chung, Frances Auckley, Dennis Society of Anesthesia and Sleep Medicine guidelines on preoperative screening and assessment of adult patients with obstructive sleep apnea University of Toronto Canada 3 68 BERRY, RB LIGHT, RW Effect of upper airway anesthesia on obstructive sleep-apnea Long Beach VA Medical Center USA 4 60 CHADWICK, GA MCNICHOLAS, WT Obstructive sleep-apnea following topical oropharyngeal anesthesia in loud snorers University College, Dublin, Ireland 5 56 Cala, SJ Kimoff, RJ Effect of topical upper airway anesthesia on apnea duration through the night in obstructive sleep apnea Royal Victoria Hospital, McGill University Canada 6 46 Memtsoudis, Stavros G. Chung, Frances Society of Anesthesia and Sleep Medicine guideline on intraoperative management of adult patients with obstructive sleep apnea Weill Cornell Medical College and Hospital USA 7 46 Mahmoud, Mohamed Fleck, Robert Effect of increasing depth of dexmedetomidine and propofol anesthesia on upper airway morphology in children and adolescents with obstructive sleep apnea Cincinnati Children’s Hospital Medical Center USA 8 40 Luscri, Nathan Tobias, Joseph D. Monitored anesthesia care with a combination of ketamine and dexmedetomidine during magnetic resonance imaging in 3 children with trisomy 21 and obstructive sleep apnea University of Missouri School of Medicine USA 9 29 DEEGAN, PC MCNICHOLAS, WT Topical oropharyngeal anesthesia in patients with obstructive sleep-apnea Department of Respiratory Medicine, University College, Dublin Ireland 10 27 Sultana, Adrian Schumann, Roman Special indications for opioid free anaesthesia and analgesia, patient and procedure related: including obesity, sleep apnea, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, complex regional pain syndromes, opioid addiction and cancer surgery Sydney Institute for Obesity Surgery and University of New South Wales Australia Open in a new tab The first 3 articles on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia” were published in Anaesthesia and Analgesia (140 citations), Anaesthesia and Analgesia (130 citations), and American Journal of Respiratory And Critical Care Medicine (68 citations), respectively (Table 1 ). Table 2. Types of 79 articles reached. Document types Frequency % of 79 Article 38 48.101 Meeting abstract 19 24.051 Review 10 12.658 Letter 9 11.392 Editorial material 2 2.532 Proceedings paper 2 2.532 Book chapter 1 1.266 Open in a new tab The citation trend of publications from the first publication published in 1987 to the present is shown in Figure 1 . When the annual growth of “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia” publications was examined, an increasing pattern was seen over the study period (Fig. 1 ). It was found that the publications obtained as a result of the search were written in three different languages, primarily English (n = 77; 97.46%), followed by French (n = 1; 1.26%), and German (n = 1; 1.26%). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Graphics of total publications by years between 1987 and 2021. The red dotted line indicates the moving median. The red line indicates the trend line. It was found that 38 of the 79 studies reached as a result of the search were articles, and the average number of citations was 21.13. The H-index of these articles, which were cited 803 times in total, was 15. A total of 31 articles (81.57%) were cited at least once, while the remaining 7 articles (18.43%) were not cited at all. The majority of the articles obtained were from the research fields of anesthesiology (n = 20; 52.63%), followed byotorhinolaryngology (n = 5; 13.15%), pediatrics (n = 5; 13.15%), respiratory system (n = 5; 13.15%), and internal medicine (n = 4; 10.52%), and the rest were from various fields (Table 3 ). Table 3. The areas with the most cited articles in the OSA. Research areas Frequency % (n = 38) Anesthesiology 20 52.632 Otorhinolaryngology 5 13.158 Pediatrics 5 13.158 Respiratory system 5 13.158 General internal medicine 4 10.526 Research experimental medicine 3 7.895 Dentistry oral surgery medicine 2 5.263 Neurosciences neurology 2 5.263 Physiology 1 2.632 Sport sciences 1 2.632 Surgery 1 2.632 Open in a new tab OSA = obstructive sleep apnea. When the articles were analyzed according to their countries of origin, the first was the US (n = 22; 57.89%), followed by Canada (n = 8; 21.05%), Australia (n = 3; 7.89%), Germany (n = 3; 7.89), Japan (n = 3; 7.89%), Austria (n = 2; 5.26%), and other countries (n = 10; 31.58%). The publication ranking determined that the four most active continents in the world are North and South America, Europe, and Asia. There were no publications on the subject from the African continent (Table 4 ). Journals were listed according to impact factors, numbers of published articles and citations (Table 5 ). Institutions were listed according to number of publications of the articles in the field of OSA (Table 6 ). Table 4. List of top 10 active countries in publishing articles on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia.” Countries/Regions Number of publications % of 38 USA 22 57.895 Canada 8 21.053 Australia 3 7.895 Germany 3 7.895 Japan 3 7.895 Austria 2 5.263 Ireland 2 5.263 People’s Republic of China 2 5.263 Chile 1 2.632 Greece 1 2.632 Peru 1 2.632 Portugal 1 2.632 Singapore 1 2.632 South Korea 1 2.632 Switzerland 1 2.632 Thailand 1 2.632 Open in a new tab Table 5. Journals according to impact factors, number of published articles, and citations. Impact factor Number of published articles Citation American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine 17.452 2 126 American Review of Respiratory Disease 17.452 1 60 Journal of Applied Physiology 3.056 1 56 Journal of Clinical Anesthesia 6.039 2 46 Pediatric Anesthesia 2.311 1 40 Best Practice & Research-Clinical Anaesthesiology 2.727 1 27 European Archives of Oto-Rhino-Laryngology 1.809 1 23 Anesthesiology 7.067 1 21 Pediatric Clinics of North America 2.042 1 16 Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine 7.015 1 15 Revista Brasileira de Anestesiologia 0.867 1 15 Anaesthesia 5.739 1 14 Journal of Anesthesia 6.039 1 11 International Journal of Pediatric Otorhinolaryngology 1.241 2 9 Canadian Journal of Anaesthesia-Journal Canadien d Anesthesie 3779 1 5 Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine 3.586 1 5 Laryngoscope 2.465 2 5 Anaesthesist 1.025 1 2 Minerva Anestesiologica 2.498 1 2 Bmc Anesthesiology 1.695 1 1 Journal of Clinical Monitoring and Computing 2.108 1 1 Journal of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery 1.642 2 1 Medicine 1.552 1 1 Respiratory Medicine Case Reports N\A 1 1 Sleep and Breathing 2.162 1 1 International Journal of Clinical and Experimental Medicine 0.166 1 0 Iranian Journal of Pediatrics 0,370 1 0 Ja Clinical Reports 1.454(2017) 1 0 Principles and Practice of Anesthesia for Thoracic Surgery N\A 1 0 Open in a new tab Table 6. Institutions that publish the most articles in the field of OSA. Organizations Number of articles % (n = 38) University of Toronto 5 13.158 Duke University 3 7.895 Mcgill University 3 7.895 Stanford University 3 7.895 Chiba University 2 5.263 Hosp Special Surg 2 5.263 Mayo Clinic 2 5.263 Natl Univ Ireland Univ Coll Dublin 2 5.263 Paracelsus Med University 2 5.263 St Vincents Hospital 2 5.263 Open in a new tab OSA = obstructive sleep apnea. 3.2. Collaboration analysis International collaboration has been accepted as research done by authors from >1 country. In this study, it is seen that the studies with international collaboration took place between 9 countries. These collaborations have been found to occur between Australia, Austria, Canada, Chile, Germany, Peru, Singapore, Switzerland, and the US. In the current study, countries with at least 1 document with international cooperation were visualized using WoSviewer (Fig. 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Mapping of countries in international collaboration with at least 1 publication on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia.” 3.3. Growth of citations Figure 3 shows the annual growth of citations for “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia.” The annual increase in the number of citations for “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia” showed a slow growth until 2014, followed by a dramatic increase in the last 10 years. The highest number of citations was seen in 2019, with a total of 110 citations. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Graphics of citations by year between 1996 and 2021. The line indicates the number of citations. 3.4. Scientific mapping Scientific mapping of the most cited authors and keyword analysis using the Wosviewer method are shown in (Figs. 4 and 5 ). Figure 4. Open in a new tab Citation visualization map among authors with at least 1 publication on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia.” The lines connecting the authors are indicative of citations. Relatively more cited authors are represented by larger circle size or font size. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Keyword visualization map of articles on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia” that are mentioned at least once. Converging lines are indicative of occurrence relationships in articles. Keywords represented by larger circle size or font size appeared relatively more in articles. 4. Discussion OSA syndrome (OSAS) is a sleep-related respiratory disease. Patients with OSAS are sensitive to anesthesia due to the collapse of anesthetic and sedative drugs in the UA, and the perioperative period is risky for this patient group. In our study, the bibliographic analysis of the most cited works of the global literature on anesthesiology and reanimation published in the field of OSAS was performed. Anesthesia management and airway safety, patient management, including pain control in the postoperative period, and continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) noninvasive mechanical ventilation in treatment methods were identified as hot spots. Today, anesthetists encounter many undiagnosed OSA patients to undergo elective surgery. The most cited article on OSA and anesthesia, which essentially concerns the provision of airway safety in the perioperative period, is a systematic review suggesting that patients who are diagnosed with OSA based on screening tools such as the STOP-Bang (Snoring history, Tired during the day, Observed stop breathing while sleep, High blood pressure, BMI more than 35 kg/m 2 , Age more than 50 years, Neck circumference more than 40 cm and male Gender) questionnaire and who are stable in terms of comorbidities, which if they can use a CPAP device in the postoperative period and if their pain during this period can be managed with predominantly non-opioid analgesic techniques, may be suitable for outpatient surgery. This review is titled, “Society for Ambulatory Anesthesia Consensus Statement on Preoperative Selection of Adult Patients with Obstructive Sleep Apnea Scheduled for Ambulatory Surgery,” published in 2012 by Joshi et al in the Journal of Anesthesia and Analgesia. The fact that the diagnosis of OSA is usually made as a result of medical evaluation performed in preparation for the surgical operation and that adverse developments in the respiratory and cardiovascular systems in the perioperative period are associated with OSA explains the number of references to this guideline. Since there is significant evidence that the risk of complications increases during the postoperative period, it is necessary to ensure the optimal preoperative evaluation of patients diagnosed with or suspected of OSA in order to improve patient safety. The second most cited research examining this issue is a guideline named “Society of Anesthesia and Sleep Medicine Guidelines on Preoperative Screening and Assessment of Adult Patients with Obstructive Sleep Apnea,” which was published in Anesthesia and Analgesia journal in 2016 by Chung et al. It has been determined that UA anesthesia with local anesthetic agents impairs the arousal response to airway obstruction, and the impulses from UA mechanoreceptors have an effect during obstructive events. The third most cited research examining the correlation between local anesthetics and UA is an article named “Effect of Upper Airway Anesthesia On Obstructive Sleep-Apnea” which was published in the American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine in 1995 by Berry et al. This is supported by another study showing that UA mechanoreceptors provide feedback of inspiratory effort during apnea; however, this function is weakened as night progresses and with UA anesthesia. OSAS is a risk factor for difficult airway management during anesthesia. The demonstration in our study that airway safety issues were investigated in patients with OSA may be related to the perception of OSA as an important risk factor for difficult airway management. Although there are few randomized controlled studies on this subject, some prospective and retrospective controlled studies have supported this assumption. [ 15 ] In a retrospective study, Hiremath et al [ 16 ] found OSA in 53% of patients with difficult airways using AHI ≥ 10 as the cutoff value according to the AHI. Similarly, Kim et al [ 17 ] showed that patients with AHI ≥ 40 have a significant prevalence of difficult intubation. In this study, the incidences of difficult intubation in OSA patients with AHI ≤ 40, 40 to 70, and ≥ 70 were 3.3%, 19.3%, and 27.6%, respectively. Skeletal and soft tissue changes in patients with OSA may contribute to a difficult airway and explain the positive relationship between a difficult airway and OSA. This situation increases the importance of difficult airway management for anesthesiologists and intensivists in terms of airway safety in patients with OSA. Therefore, as shown in the present study, the most cited publication on OSA and anesthesiology was the Society of Anesthesia and Sleep Medicine guideline, focusing on the preoperative diagnosis and perioperative airway management. It is controversial whether anesthetic, sedative, and analgesic agents increase the severity of OSA by decreasing pharyngeal tone and suppressing respiratory responses to hypoxia and hypercapnia. [ 18 ] According to the updated 2018 guidelines of Anesthesia and Sleep Medicine, patients with OSA were considered to be at higher risk for difficult airways compared to patients without OSA. [ 18 ] Anesthetic and analgesic drugs can alter consciousness and sleep state, anatomy and physiology of UA, arousal responses, and muscle activation, and interact with the respiratory drive or affect these. Anesthetic and analgesic drugs can alter consciousness and sleep state, anatomy and physiology of UA, arousal responses, muscle activation, and interact with or affect the respiratory drive. Anesthetic and analgesic drugs can alter consciousness and sleep state, anatomy and physiology of UA, arousal responses, and muscle activation, and interact with the respiratory drive or affect these. This may increase the risk of perioperative complications in patients with OSA. [ 19 ] In addition, dose titration is important for pain control in the postoperative period because of the high sensitivity of adult and pediatric patients with OSA to narcotic agents. [ 20 ] The justified concern about airway safety and anesthesia management in patients with OSA was reflected in the number of publications, and as we detected in our study, the highest publication rate was in this field of anesthesiology. In our study, we conducted a bibliometric analysis of OSA-related publications and found that CPAP therapy is a hot topic in the field of otorhinolaryngology. CPAP can be used to treat OSA by acting as a pneumatic air splint that keeps the UA open during sleep. [ 21 ] Although CPAP is the gold standard treatment for symptomatic moderate OSA, the rate of patients continuing treatment is approximately 50% with the use of CPAP for an average of 4.4 hours per night. [ 22 , 23 ] In addition to adaptation problems, it has also been shown that CPAP application does not reduce mortality in patients with moderate-to-severe OSA. [ 24 ] Especially in the last 2 decades, the incidence of patients with severe OSA who can benefit from CPAP treatment but have adaptation problems for treatment and moderate-to-severe OSA patients who are less likely to benefit from CPAP treatment has increased in sleep clinics. [ 25 ] The clinic of OSA disease in the pediatric age group, which manifests itself with more behavioral problems such as night snoring, sleep terror, night urinary incontinence, school problems, hyperactivity, depression, and psychiatric problems, is different from the adult disease. [ 26 , 27 ] When the most cited articles were analyzed according to age groups, OSA and related publications in the pediatric age group were in third place. Multiple factors such as adenotonsillar hypertrophy, obesity, anatomical and neuromuscular factors, and hypotonic neuromuscular disease may play a role in the etiology. [ 28 ] In the pediatric population, the prevalence of OSA is between 1 and 6% in all children and 59% in obese children. [ 29 ] There is a high risk of perioperative complications. Among 111 pediatric tonsillectomy cases, 57% of children with death or near-death events met the criteria for being at risk for OSA, and apnea was observed in 46% of these patients. [ 30 ] Despite the high prevalence of OSA and the tendency for complications in the pediatric age group, the number of publications on OSA and anesthesia in this age group is less than expected. This may be attributed to the fact that research funding prioritization is adult-oriented, partly due to the high adult disease burden and insufficient financial support from the pharmaceutical industry, as well as specific ethical concerns and methodological barriers in pediatric research. [ 31 ] In addition, because there are uncertain areas in pediatric OSA management, more research efforts and funding are required. [ 32 ] Publications on OSA and anesthesiology have increased over the last 2 decades. In our current study, it was shown that the USA especially contributed to most of the publications on OSA and Anesthesia. Similarly, in another analysis of the 100 most influential articles on OSA, the USA was found to have absolute advantages in OSA research. [ 33 ] Among the reasons for the high number of publications on OSA in the USA, it can be seen that the USA has the highest number of sleep laboratories. [ 34 ] However, other countries may have lagged behind the USA in this race due to the low interest in sleep medicine on a global scale, the high rate of under-diagnosis of OSA, the need for more sleep medicine specialists and sleep laboratories for diagnosis, and economic difficulties in finding research funding. [ 35 ] 5. Limitations Publications on OSA and anesthesiology in the current study were obtained from the WOS, and the data were analyzed objectively and comprehensively. However, this study has some limitations. First, a large number of new research articles are added to the WOS, and the number of citations changes daily. Therefore, our analysis reflects June 4, 2021. After this date, there may have been changes in the number of citations and rankings. In addition, indexing is performed by the WOS, which assigns the keywords in the studies; therefore, some publications may not be included in the analysis due to WOS, although it is very unlikely. All studies indexed to the WOS had the same limitations. In addition, the keywords were only in English, so publications in which keywords or abstracts were not in English were excluded from the study. Another limitation was that the search was limited to WoS only, and other databases such as Scopus and PubMed were not included in the search. 6. Conclusion Publications on “Obstructive Sleep Apnea” and “Anesthesia” have increased rapidly in the last decade. Anesthesia management and airway safety, patient management including pain control in the postoperative period, and noninvasive mechanical ventilation treatment methods such as CPAP are hot spots. OSAS is a risk factor for difficult airway management during anesthesia. In this bibliometric analysis, it was determined that the most cited publications were the guidelines for the problems and solutions encountered in the diagnosis, treatment, and postoperative period of patients with OSAS during anesthesia. More randomized controlled studies are needed to determine the OSAS and anesthesia requirements. Author contributions Conceptualization: Özlem Öner, Ali Necati Gökmen. Data curation: Özlem Öner. Formal analysis: Ali Necati Gökmen. Investigation: Özlem Öner, Mustafa Cenk Ecevit. Methodology: Özlem Öner. Project administration: Özlem Öner, Ali Necati Gökmen. Resources: Özlem Öner. Supervision: Mustafa Cenk Ecevit, Ali Necati Gökmen. Validation: Ali Necati Gökmen. Writing – review & editing: Özlem Öner, Ali Necati Gökmen. Abbreviations: AHI apnea-hypopnea index CPAP continuous positive airway pressure H-index Hirsch index IF impact factor OSA obstructive sleep apnea OSAS OSA syndrome SCI WOS Citation Indexing UA upper airway WOS Web of Science The authors have no funding to disclose. The authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are not publicly available, but are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. How to cite this article: Öner Ö, Ecevit MC, Gökmen AN. A bibliometric analysis of obstructive sleep apnea and anesthesia. Medicine 2023;102:17(e32993). Contributor Information Mustafa Cenk Ecevit, Email: mcenk.ecevit@gmail.com. Ali Necati Gökmen, Email: necatigokmen@yahoo.com. References [1]. Kapur VK, Auckley DH, Chowdhuri S, et al. Clinical practice guideline for diagnostic testing for adult obstructive sleep apnea: an American academy of sleep medicine clinical practice guideline. J Clin Sleep Med. 2017;13:479–504. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Benjafield AV, Ayas NT, Eastwood PR, et al. Estimation of the global prevalence and burden of obstructive sleep apnoea: a literature-based analysis. Lancet Respir Med. 2019;7:687–98. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Berry RB, Budhiraja R, Gottlieb DJ, et al. Rules for scoring respiratory events in sleep: update of the 2007 AASM Manual for the Scoring of Sleep and Associated Events. Deliberations of the Sleep Apnea Definitions Task Force of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine. J Clin Sleep Med. 2012;8:597–619. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Wolfe RM, Pomerantz J, Miller DE, et al. Obstructive sleep apnea: preoperative screening and postoperative care. J Am Board Fam Med. 2016;29:263–75. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Kaw R, Chung F, Pasupuleti V, et al. Meta-analysis of the association between obstructive sleep apnoea and postoperative outcome. Br J Anaesth. 2012;109:897–906. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Lyons PG, M