50. 10.1186/s13012-018-0739-4. 10.1186/s13012-018-0739-4 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The data sets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',31,'2025-10-15 11:32:02','2025-10-15 11:32:02');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5746,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2564344','The new consultant survey 2005.','Beckett M, Hulbert D, Brown R.','2006','faem freehand managerially secondments itu secondments secondments secondment secondments secondment doubted faem tolerating faem itu secondment secondment viva viva hulbert faem plymouth lenton dison modernising','Abstract Background Consultants in emergency medicine have to deal with a wide range of problems, many of which they will not have encountered during their training. One way to assess the adequacy of specialist training is to ask recently appointed consultants whether or not they feel adequately prepared for their role. Methods A questionnaire was sent out to 60 newly appointed consultants in emergency medicine in January and February 2005 and the results analysed. Results Many respondents feel that there should be greater emphasis on acquiring clinical skills, partly by greater consultant supervision and partly by providing more experience of anaesthetics and intensive care. New consultants also feel inadequately prepared for their management responsibilities, and this is a source of great stress. Conclusions Specialist training in emergency medicine needs to pay more attention to the acquisition of clinical skills and to preparation for management responsibility. Keywords: consultants, survey, training The purpose of specialist training is to prepare for life as a consultant. Consultants in emergency medicine have to deal with a wide range of clinical, administrative, and management problems, many of which they will not have encountered during their training. Trainees are expected to acquire generic skills and competencies that can be applied to a variety of situations, especially when dealing with managerial issues. One way to assess the adequacy of specialist training, therefore, is to ask recently appointed consultants whether they feel adequately prepared for their role. If there are areas where significant numbers of new consultants feel poorly prepared, then changes should be made to the way registrars are trained. Surveys of newly appointed consultants in emergency medicine were conducted in 1997 (unpublished survey by J Marrow), 2001, 1 and 2005. We here report the results of the 2005 survey with references to the earlier surveys for comparison. In the three diets (18 months) before the latest survey was conducted, there had been changes in the Faculty of Accident and Emergency Medicine (FAEM) exit examination with greater emphasis on assessing clinical skills. Methods A questionnaire was sent out to 60 newly appointed consultants in emergency medicine in January and February 2005. A consecutive series of new consultant appointments was chosen; those who had previously held consultant posts elsewhere were excluded but those who had only previously held locum posts were included. The survey was anonymous. Fifty replies were returned in response to the initial letter or a reminder (83% response rate). The 1997 survey was sent to 47 consultants (response rate 73%). The 2001 survey sent to 60 consultants obtained a 90% response. Copies of the questionnaire form are available from the authors. Results Consultants had been in their post for between 2 months and 2 years, with an average 10.2 months. The form was designed to elicit answers in a standardised format to specific questions with space to add freehand comments if desired. The questions and answers are given below. Do you feel your training adequately prepared you in these areas? (on a scale of 0 “not at all” to 10 “completely”): - clinically: mean 8.6 - academically: mean 6.7 - managerially: mean 6.0 - generally prepared for consultant role: mean 7.6 Usefulness of secondments. Anaesthetics and intensive therapy unit (ITU) secondments were considered the most useful by 65%, and 16% said 6 months or more would be preferable. Secondments should have a more formal structure and aims according to 48%, while 30% thought there should be a management secondment. Surgery was thought the least useful of the secondments. Some thought a surgical secondment was of no value: “I was treated like a medical student” and “I could already spot an acute abdomen, what more do you need to know?”. Exposure to different departments. Registrars had worked in between two and six different departments in their rotations; most had worked in four. Regardless of the actual figure, almost all considered the number that they had worked in was “about right”. Suggestions for improvement of training: - 33% wanted more 1:1 teaching from consultants - 29% wanted more management experience, but two doubted whether this could properly be learned before taking on consultant responsibility - 16% thought the record of in‐training assessment (RITA) should be more robust Consultant confidence. In which area do you feel least confident? (some respondents indicated more than one area): - handling management issues: 63% - dealing with psychiatric patients: 26% - other clinical problems: 30% Impressions of the FAEM exit examination. Sixty two per cent either said there should be a larger clinical component or approved of the recent changes to achieve this (“We must shed the office consultant image”). Although many said the clinical component should be emphasised, only two said that it should be made more difficult. What do you find most stressful in your work? (on a scale of 0 “not at all” to 10 “very stressful”) - conflict resolution (patients): mean 3.9 - conflict resolution (colleagues): mean 6.2 - financial management: mean 5.2 - targets (department, trust, and DoH): mean 7.9 - balancing life and job: mean 5.5 Examples of difficult experience. In response to the question “What was your most difficult experience since becoming a consultant?”, only two respondents gave a clinical situation: in both cases this involved managing a patient with a severe gastro‐intestinal haemorrhage, with no specialist help available. All other replies gave examples of managerial difficulties or conflicts with senior colleagues such as dealing with poorly performing junior doctors or “tolerating fellow consultants who do not share the same work ethic”. There were some lengthy accounts of serious problems which had clearly been extremely stressful. What has been the biggest surprise about being a consultant? Fourteen gave positive replies reflecting the greater respect, power, and influence consultants may have. Most of the other comments were negative, due to factors such as the responsibility, administrative load, stress, and difficulty in making change happen. Discussion Other specialties have conducted surveys of new consultants'' opinions concerning their training. Since 1966 psychiatrists have been asked to rate their training experiences, 2 and occasional surveys of paediatricians 3 and geriatricians 4 have shown perceived deficiencies in areas such as research training and organisational skills. In accident and emergency medicine, a survey in 1988 showed concern over management issues. 5 There have of course been many changes since 1988 such as the introduction of structured training, the exit examination, and multi‐consultant departments. Training in emergency medicine is undergoing continuous change and refinement. The exit examination is designed to reflect all areas of competence, and is considered by the Faculty board to be fit for purpose. It covers clinical, academic, and managerial activities and is currently undergoing further revision. The validity of training and assessment is best demonstrated by the performance of successful and unsuccessful candidates over a period of time. However, in a specialty such as ours where work practice is constantly changing and individual performance is difficult to measure because of team working and shared responsibility, other markers need to be used to support standard assessment data. Surveying consultants'' opinions and personal experience can be a useful way of assessing the effectiveness of their training. Repeating such surveys can give information reflecting a complex situation in which specialist training, the structure of the FAEM exit examination, and the nature of consultant practice are continually changing. However, there are some persistent trends in all three surveys. Many respondents feel that there should be greater emphasis on acquiring clinical skills, partly by greater consultant supervision and partly by providing more experience of anaesthetics and intensive care. The 2001 survey found that 14% thought there should have been better consultant supervision compared to 33% in our current survey. It is interesting that many new consultants think there should be more 1:1 teaching of trainees, since this could potentially be very demanding of consultant time. In addition, there have been recent changes to the recommendations on the amount of time registrars should be under direct supervision. At present there is no agreed definition of what is meant by supervision, and in practice this can vary from the consultant standing alongside the trainee to being available but occupied elsewhere in the department. It seems that increasing the presence and immediate feedback to the trainee on clinical performance would be welcome. There is widespread support for the increased emphasis on assessing clinical skills in the exit examination. Candidates must now pass the clinical section of the examination which accounts for 50% of the total marks. Although many wish they had received better training in these skills, clinical problems are not a major cause of stress in the life of a new consultant, so this may reflect young consultants'' aspirations for the specialty in the future rather than the pressures of today''s practice. The desire for increased exposure to anaesthetics and ITU may be met by the proposed “run through” training where all trainees will receive a year of anaesthetics and critical care. 6 The clinical area where most feel poorly prepared is psychiatry, which may be due to the fact that this is not an essential secondment, and few emergency medicine trainees have had significant postgraduate experience in psychiatry. A recurring theme of these surveys, however, is that new consultants feel inadequately prepared for their management responsibilities, and this remains a source of great stress. The 2001 survey found that 47% felt inadequately prepared for their role, all citing insufficient management training as a reason; in 2005 67% felt that management was the area for which they were least prepared, so the situation may have worsened. There are a few practical suggestions as to how this can be improved: 24% suggested a formal secondment in management and 17% had taken a locum consultant post, mostly only for a few weeks, but some up to 1 year. A few found this helpful and one suggested that a two tier consultant system would be useful. An alternative to the present management viva might be the completion of a management project as an assessment method. Most candidates pass the current management viva but it seems they still feel vulnerable and this is an area which must be addressed in both training and examination. Many of the managerial problems encountered were shop floor issues such as conflict resolution with colleagues, leadership, and supervision difficulties. Even those who strongly advocate that the emergency medicine consultants'' principal role should be as a front line clinical specialist, must recognise the importance of dealing with these problems effectively. Our new consultants feel inadequately trained in dealing with the managerial issues generated by today''s emergency departments. If specialist training in emergency medicine is to be considered “fit for purpose” it needs to address this issue. Footnotes Competing interests: none declared References 1. Beckett M, Hulbert D. The new consultant survey. Poster at FAEM Scientific Meeting, Plymouth, UK 2001 2. Brook C P. Postgraduate psychiatric training. Med Educ 198115(5)335–339. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lenton S W, Dison P J, Haines L C. A BPA survey of newly appointed consultants. Arch Dis Child 199471(4)381–385. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Sandler M. Training experience and views of newly appointed consultants in geriatric medicine. J R Coll Physicians Lond 199226(1)44–46. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Driscoll P, Cope A, Miles S A. Adequacy of senior registrar training in accident and emergency medicine over the last 5 years. Arch Emerg Med 19885(3)162–168. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. McGowan A. Modernising medical careers. Emerg Med J 200522(suppl)7–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-15 11:32:09','2025-10-15 11:32:09');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5747,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11588876','Managing blood glucose control in the intensive care unit.','Gunst J, Umpierrez GE, Van den Berghe G.','2024','fueled glucometers cholestatic evoking aggravating antidiabetics controling lucid tighter gvdb geu 1842724n gvdb methusalem flemish meth14 advg adg geu nats 3ul1tr002378 05s2 2p30dk111024 dexcom glycare geu dexcom glycare vanhorebeek langouche gunst weekers verwaest bruyninckx schetz vlasselaers ferdinande lauwers bouillon meersseman milants wijngaerden bobbaers bouillon vlasselaers milants desmet vanhorebeek mesotten casaer meyfroidt ingels cromphaut schetz mesotten gielen sterken claessens vlasselaers lemiere lagae gewillig eyskens vanhorebeek finfer chittock dhingra dodek heritier heyland myburgh finfer chittock myburgh dhingra dodek heyland casaer mesotten schetz meyfroidt cromphaut ingels meersseman vlasselaers debaveye desmet assche vanderheyden fivez kerklaan mesotten verbruggen vanhorebeek debaveye vlasselaers desmet casaer hanot gunst debaveye guiza bruyn dauwe troy casaer vlieger haghedooren meyfroidt ingels vlasselaers desmet mebis stessel geebelen vandenbrande gruyters pauw vermassen peperstraete hoste waele herck depuydt herpe hooijdonk coart assche veraghtert moor wauters mesotten adigbli chatoor devaux billot bilotta bohe brunkhorst cavalcanti laroche hoedemaekers iapichino kalfon lahooti mahendran melot oksanen polli preiser vlok tanna finfer evidoa2400082 bohe abidi brunot klich klouche sedillot tchenio quenot roudaut mottard thiolliere dellamonica wallet souweine lautrette preiser vacheron hssain maucort boulch coidlgs controling finnis anstey bihari biradar finfer martensson secombe udy deane lucid ctg honarmand sirimaturos hirshberg bircher agus farrington krinsley lanspa nagpal preiser jacobi e161 e181','Most critically ill patients develop insulin resistance and hyperglycemia, which are associated with mortality risk. However, the severity of hyperglycemia also reflects illness severity, highlighting the need for randomized controlled trials (RCTs) to determine whether hyperglycemia directly impairs outcome [ 1 ]. We summarize RCT evidence and propose a practical framework for glucose control in intensive care unit (ICU) patients. Landmark RCTs on tight glucose control The interest in tight glucose control (TGC) sparked in 2001, after a RCT in surgical ICU patients in Leuven ( N = 1548) showed significant morbidity/mortality benefit from lowering blood glucose into the normal range (80–110 mg/dL [4.4–6.1 mmol/L]) versus accepting hyperglycemia up to 215 mg/dL (11.9 mmol/L) [ 2 ]. Subsequently, the Leuven investigators confirmed benefits in medical ( N = 1200) and pediatric ICU patients ( N = 700) [ 3 , 4 ]. Additionally, in children, 4-year neurocognitive outcomes improved [ 5 ]. Mechanistic studies attributed benefit to prevention of glucose toxicity rather than other insulin-mediated effects [ 1 ]. These data led many centers to swiftly adopt some form of glucose control as simple, cost-effective intervention. Yet, in 2009, the multicenter NICE-SUGAR RCT ( N = 6104) reported increased mortality with TGC compared with maintaining blood glucose < 180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L), which was attributed to a 14-fold increase in hypoglycemia [ 6 , 7 ]. The different results of TGC in Leuven and NICE-SUGAR fueled a long-standing debate regarding clinical implications. The opposing outcome effects have been attributed to methodological differences, including different TGC and nutrition protocols, and different control group targets [ 1 ]. NICE-SUGAR allowed the use of inaccurate glucometers, capillary and venous measurements (confounded by poor perfusion and intravenous glucose infusions, respectively), and insulin boluses. Together, these aspects may have induced prolonged/undetected hypoglycemia. In contrast, the Leuven TGC protocol included accurate glucose measurements on arterial blood, prevented/treated insulin-induced hypokalemia through simultaneous potassium monitoring, and only used continuous insulin infusion via a central venous catheter, which avoided large glucose fluctuations. However, the Leuven studies were conducted in the context of early supplementation of insufficient enteral nutrition by intravenous glucose and parenteral nutrition (PN), which may have exacerbated hyperglycemia to more harmful levels in the control group [ 8 , 9 ]. In contrast, NICE-SUGAR patients received fewer parenteral calories in the acute phase, potentially increasing the risk of hypoglycemia with TGC. Hence, efficacy and safety of TGC in the absence of early PN, combined with a protocol that strictly avoids hypoglycemia, remained unclear. To address this knowledge gap, the TGC-Fast multicenter RCT was conducted, in which 9230 ICU patients were randomized to accept hyperglycemia up to 215 mg/dL [11.9 mmol/L] or to TGC guided by the LOGIC-Insulin algorithm [ 10 ]. This computer algorithm had been validated previously through a multicenter RCT in patients not receiving early PN [ 11 ]. LOGIC-Insulin-guided TGC resulted in excellent glucose control and effectively avoided hypoglycemia, a finding later confirmed in the TGC-Fast RCT [ 10 ]. In TGC-Fast, TGC did not affect ICU dependency (primary endpoint) or mortality (safety endpoint), but it was associated with less severe acute kidney injury, lower need for renal replacement therapy, and decreased cholestatic liver dysfunction [ 10 ]. There were largely no subgroup differences for the primary and safety endpoints, except for a potential mortality benefit from TGC in neuro-ICU patients, which requires further study. A recent meta-analysis, with individual patient data available for < 50% of eligible patients, suggested no benefit of TGC [ 12 ]. However, design and performance of included trials varied, with differences in measurement accuracy, insulin titration, nutrition strategy, blood glucose target, target attainment, and incidence of hypoglycemia [ 12 ]. Because of these confounders, subgroup analyses addressing the potential importance of a single confounder (often only available at the trial level) have limited added value. Clinical implications: which target? To extrapolate RCT findings to the bedside, the clinical context and individual study protocols must be considered. Collectively, landmark RCTs have revealed the importance of avoiding severe hyperglycemia and iatrogenic hypoglycemia, and elucidated the confounding impact of early high glucose doses and early PN. Indeed, mortality harm by accepting hyperglycemia was only observed in such context evoking more severe hyperglycemia. In contrast, mortality harm by TGC was only observed when hypoglycemia was substantially increased. These findings support avoidance of early high glucose and PN, and suggest caution regarding liberal use of drugs aggravating insulin resistance, including corticosteroids, unless there is an evidence-based indication [ 1 ]. Once iatrogenic factors have been avoided, the optimal blood glucose target for ICU patients remains unclear but likely depends on available logistics (Fig. 1 ). TGC applied with accurate tools and a validated algorithm that prevent hypoglycemia and large glucose fluctuations may be appropriate to reduce morbidity [ 10 ]. However, without tools to safely provide TGC, it is advisable to tolerate slightly higher levels [ 13 ]. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Proposed framework for blood glucose control in ICU. The optimal blood glucose target likely varies according to the clinical setting. If the patient can eat, if there is no accurate glucose control protocol, if there is no need for a central venous and arterial line, or if nursing staff lacks experience with tight glucose control, liberal blood glucose control is advised with insulin used only to maintain blood glucose below 181–215 mg/dL [< 10–11.9 mmol/L]. If there is a validated protocol with confirmed high time in blood glucose target range and a minimal risk of hypoglycemia, tight glucose control (80–110 mg/dL [4.4–6.1 mmol/L]) can be considered [ 10 ]. Otherwise, an intermediate glucose control (insulin only to maintain blood glucose below 150–180 mg/dL [< 8.3–10 mmol/L]) strategy is advised. Safe tight (80–110 mg/dL [4.4–6.1 mmol/L]) and intermediate (< 150–180 mg/dL [< 8.3–10 mmol/L]) blood glucose control requires a protocol that includes accurate and frequent measurements of blood glucose and avoids insulin boluses. Blood glucose is preferably measured using arterial blood and a blood gas analyzer, which yields a fast and accurate blood glucose measurement, and, simultaneously, a measurement of potassium (to act upon and prevent insulin-induced hypokalemia). Capillary blood glucose measurements are unreliable in ICU patients with poor tissue perfusion, and venous measurements may report falsely elevated blood glucose values during intravenous glucose infusion, increasing the risk of hypoglycemia. Short-acting insulin is preferably administered via continuous infusion through a dedicated lumen of a central venous line, to ensure continuous systemic availability and avoid large blood glucose fluctuations. When the arterial or central venous line is no longer needed, when the patient is being discharged to the ward, or when oral feeding is initiated, a more liberal approach to glucose control is advised, with insulin used only to maintain levels below 181–215 mg/dL [10–11.9 mmol/L]. Overly aggressive treatment of postprandial hyperglycemia may provoke hypoglycemia and large blood glucose fluctuations. In patients with known diabetes mellitus and in patients with persistent intravenous insulin need after resumption of oral feeding and at ICU discharge, transition to maintenance treatment (oral antidiabetics, subcutaneous insulin and/or other glucose-lowering treatments) should be performed in consultation with an endocrinologist to ensure long-term follow-up. Any form of blood glucose control should avoid iatrogenic and severe hyperglycemia, hypoglycemia, and large blood glucose variability Toward individualized glucose control? Observational studies have suggested that ICU patients suffering from diabetes may benefit from higher targets because of adaptations to chronic hyperglycemia. However, the CONTROLING RCT ( N = 2075) found no benefit by hemoglobin A1c-guided glucose control, although blood glucose considerably overlapped between groups [ 14 ]. In the LUCID RCT ( N = 419), accepting severe hyperglycemia (180–252 mg/dL [10–14 mmol/L]) in ICU patients with type-2 diabetes reduced the risk of hypoglycemia (primary endpoint), but associated with 5% higher mortality [ 15 ], supporting prevention of severe hyperglycemia, also in diabetic ICU patients. Strategies to improve quality and safety of glucose control Any protocol for glucose control in ICU requires frequent and accurate measurements, which becomes particularly important with tighter targets. Regular potassium monitoring is also necessary to prevent insulin-induced hypokalemia. Although (near-)continuous glucose monitoring could reduce glucose variability, routine in-ICU use is off-label, and subcutaneous sensors lack accuracy for hemodynamically unstable patients [ 16 ]. Short-acting insulin is currently the preferred glucose-lowering drug in ICU. Theoretically, other drugs could be superior because insulin suppresses cellular repair processes that are important in critical illness [ 1 ]. For example, the potential benefits of sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) -inhibitors, and the place of non-medicinal strategies that lower insulin needs, including alternative nutritional regimens and exercise, merit further study. Take-home message Current evidence supports avoiding iatrogenic factors that aggravate hyperglycemia, such as early-PN and liberal corticosteroid use, and does not support TGC, except with accurate tools and protocols that prevent iatrogenic hypoglycemia. Author contributions JG wrote the first draft, which was reviewed and edited by GVdB and GEU. All authors read and approved the final version. Funding JG holds a senior clinical investigator fellowship granted by Research Foundation–Flanders (1842724N) and receives research funding by KU Leuven (STG/23/032) and the European Society of Intensive Care Medicine (2022 Fundamental Research Award). GVdB receives structural research financing through KU Leuven via the Methusalem Program of the Flemish Government (METH14/06) and research funding by the European Research Council of the European Union (advanced grants AdvG-2017-785809 and 101133276 (ERC-2023-AdG) from the Horizon Europe Program). GEU is supported by research grants from the National Institutes of Health (NATS UL 3UL1TR002378-05S2), from the Clinical and Translational Science Award program, and from the National Institutes of Health and National Center for Research Resources (NIH/NIDDK 2P30DK111024-06), and has served as a member of advisory boards for Dexcom and GlyCare. Declarations Conflicts of interest On behalf of all authors, the corresponding author states that there is no conflict of interest. GEU has previously served as a member of advisory boards for Dexcom and GlyCare. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Van den Berghe G, Vanhorebeek I, Langouche L, Gunst J (2024) Our scientific journey through the ups and downs of blood glucose control in the ICU. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 209:497–506 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Van den Berghe G, Wouters P, Weekers F, Verwaest C, Bruyninckx F, Schetz M, Vlasselaers D, Ferdinande P, Lauwers P, Bouillon R (2001) Intensive insulin therapy in critically ill patients. N Engl J Med 345:1359–1367 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Van den Berghe G, Wilmer A, Hermans G, Meersseman W, Wouters PJ, Milants I, Van Wijngaerden E, Bobbaers H, Bouillon R (2006) Intensive insulin therapy in the medical ICU. N Engl J Med 354:449–461 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Vlasselaers D, Milants I, Desmet L, Wouters PJ, Vanhorebeek I, van den Heuvel I, Mesotten D, Casaer MP, Meyfroidt G, Ingels C, Muller J, Van Cromphaut S, Schetz M, Van den Berghe G (2009) Intensive insulin therapy for patients in paediatric intensive care: a prospective, randomised controlled study. Lancet 373:547–556 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Mesotten D, Gielen M, Sterken C, Claessens K, Hermans G, Vlasselaers D, Lemiere J, Lagae L, Gewillig M, Eyskens B, Vanhorebeek I, Wouters PJ, Van den Berghe G (2012) Neurocognitive development of children 4 years after critical illness and treatment with tight glucose control: a randomized controlled trial. JAMA 308:1641–1650 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Finfer S, Chittock DR, Su SY, Blair D, Foster D, Dhingra V, Bellomo R, Cook D, Dodek P, Henderson WR, Hebert PC, Heritier S, Heyland DK, McArthur C, McDonald E, Mitchell I, Myburgh JA, Norton R, Potter J, Robinson BG, Ronco JJ (2009) Intensive versus conventional glucose control in critically ill patients. N Engl J Med 360:1283–1297 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Finfer S, Liu B, Chittock DR, Norton R, Myburgh JA, McArthur C, Mitchell I, Foster D, Dhingra V, Henderson WR, Ronco JJ, Bellomo R, Cook D, McDonald E, Dodek P, Hebert PC, Heyland DK, Robinson BG (2012) Hypoglycemia and risk of death in critically ill patients. N Engl J Med 367:1108–1118 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Casaer MP, Mesotten D, Hermans G, Wouters PJ, Schetz M, Meyfroidt G, Van Cromphaut S, Ingels C, Meersseman P, Muller J, Vlasselaers D, Debaveye Y, Desmet L, Dubois J, Van Assche A, Vanderheyden S, Wilmer A, Van den Berghe G (2011) Early versus late parenteral nutrition in critically ill adults. N Engl J Med 365:506–517 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Fivez T, Kerklaan D, Mesotten D, Verbruggen S, Wouters PJ, Vanhorebeek I, Debaveye Y, Vlasselaers D, Desmet L, Casaer MP, Guerra GG, Hanot J, Joffe A, Tibboel D, Joosten K, Van den Berghe G (2016) Early versus late parenteral nutrition in critically ill children. N Engl J Med 374:1111–1122 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Gunst J, Debaveye Y, Guiza F, Dubois J, De Bruyn A, Dauwe D, De Troy E, Casaer MP, De Vlieger G, Haghedooren R, Jacobs B, Meyfroidt G, Ingels C, Muller J, Vlasselaers D, Desmet L, Mebis L, Wouters PJ, Stessel B, Geebelen L, Vandenbrande J, Brands M, Gruyters I, Geerts E, De Pauw I, Vermassen J, Peperstraete H, Hoste E, De Waele JJ, Herck I, Depuydt P, Wilmer A, Hermans G, Benoit DD, Van den Berghe G (2023) Tight blood-glucose control without early parenteral nutrition in the ICU. N Engl J Med 389:1180–1190 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Dubois J, Van Herpe T, van Hooijdonk RT, Wouters R, Coart D, Wouters P, Van Assche A, Veraghtert G, De Moor B, Wauters J, Wilmer A, Schultz MJ, Van den Berghe G, Mesotten D (2017) Software-guided versus nurse-directed blood glucose control in critically ill patients: the LOGIC-2 multicenter randomized controlled clinical trial. Crit Care 21:212 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Adigbli D, Li Y, Hammond N, Chatoor R, Devaux AG, Li Q, Billot L, Annane D, Arabi Y, Bilotta F, Bohe J, Brunkhorst FM, Cavalcanti AB, Cook D, Engel C, Green-LaRoche D, He W, Henderson W, Hoedemaekers C, Iapichino G, Kalfon P, de La Rosa G, Lahooti A, Mackenzie I, Mahendran S, Melot C, Mitchell I, Oksanen T, Polli F, Preiser JC, Garcia Soriano F, Vlok R, Wang L, Xu Y, Delaney AP, Di Tanna GL, Finfer S (2024) A patient-level meta-analysis of intensive glucose control in critically ill adults. NEJM Evid EVIDoa2400082 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 13. Umpierrez GE (2023) Glucose control in the ICU. N Engl J Med 389:1234–1237 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Bohe J, Abidi H, Brunot V, Klich A, Klouche K, Sedillot N, Tchenio X, Quenot JP, Roudaut JB, Mottard N, Thiolliere F, Dellamonica J, Wallet F, Souwe