INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5814,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4474830','Patterns of Lymph Node Pathology; Fine Needle Aspiration Biopsy as an Evaluation Tool for Lymphadenopathy: A Retrospective Descriptive Study Conducted at the Largest Hospital in Africa.','Reddy DL, Venter WD, Pather S.','2015','hani baragwanath chbah nhls fnas nhls range12 entertained chbah hani baragwanath chbah soweto johannesburg johannesburg reserving chbah hani baragwanath johannesburg chbah nhls requisition fnas nhls disalab nhls chbah ziehl neelsen ziehl neelsen perifollicular fnas fnas graphpadquickcalcs witwatersrand m130626 entertained 20yr 35yr 50yr 65yr 65yr axilla subtotals plasmablastic fnas fnas granulom inflam lnh granulom inflam lnh fnas chbah chbah chbah chbah chbah miliary chbah fnas chbah martinson misdiagnosing zambian lymphotropism perifollicular cryptococcosis microbiologically extramedullary hhv8 affirms chbah 64kb nhls hani baragwanath pepfar pather satbat fic 3u2rtw007370 05s1 pepfar satbat fic edginton phofa mahlaba hodkinson hani baragwanath setlhako osih e47542 handa mundi fontanilla fordham reyn cir454 muthuphei rankuwa sibanda stanczuk olu eddo omoti adenopathies beha wiggill mantina willem perner 0b013e31820bb06a proxenos marais antituberculosis martinson chaisson pats 064wr churchyard mametja mvusi ndjeka hesseling ligthelm hoek kgp helden marais karstaedt hani baragwanath johannesburg samj puvaneswaran shoba samj streicher chihota mlambo tait bem bharucha namaambo mahe dupin kellam ariad tulliez franck latently razack louw samj 64kb','Abstract Background Lymphadenopathy is a common clinical presentation of disease in South Africa (SA), particularly in the era of Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) and tuberculosis (TB) co-infection. Methods Data from 560 lymph node biopsy reports of specimens from patients older than 12 years at Chris Hani Baragwanath Academic Hospital (CHBAH) between 1 January 2010 and 31 December 2012 was extracted from the National Health Laboratory Service (NHLS), division of Anatomical Pathology. Cytology reports of lymph node fine needle aspirates (FNAs) performed prior to lymph node biopsy in 203 patients were also extracted from the NHLS. Consent was not obtained from participants for their records to be used as patient information was anonymized and de-identified prior to analysis. Results The majority of patients were female (55%) and of the African/black racial group (90%). The median age of patients was 40 years (range12–94). The most common indication for biopsy was an uncertain diagnosis (more than two differential diagnoses entertained), followed by a suspicion for lymphoma, carcinoma and TB. Overall, malignancy constituted the largest biopsy pathology group (39%), with 36% of this group being carcinoma and 27% non-Hodgkin lymphoma. 22% of the total sampled nodes displayed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation (including histopathology and cytology demonstrating definite, and suspicious for mycobacterial infection), 8% comprised HIV reactive nodes; in the remainder no specific pathology was identified (nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia). Kaposi sarcoma (KS) accounted for 2.5% of lymph node pathology in this sample. Concomitant lymph node pathology was diagnosed in four cases of nodal KS (29% of the subset). The co-existing pathologies were TB and Castleman disease. HIV positive patients constituted 49% of this study sample and the majority (64%) of this subset had CD4 counts less than 350 cells/ul. 27% were HIV negative and in the remaining nodes, the HIV status of patients was unknown. The most common lymph node pathologies in HIV positive patients were Mycobacterial infection (31%), HIV reactive nodes (15%), non-Hodgkin lymphoma (15%) and nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia (15%). Only 8.7% were of Hodgkin lymphoma. In contrast, the most common lymph node pathologies in HIV negative patients were nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia (45%), carcinoma (25%) and Mycobacterial infection (11%). In this group, non-Hodgkin lymphoma and Hodgkin lymphoma constituted 9% and 8%, respectively. There were more cases of high-grade non-Hodgkin lymphoma in the HIV positive group compared to the HIV negative group. FNA and lymph node biopsy had statistically significant good agreement with regard to Hodgkin lymphoma (K 0.774, SE 0.07, 95% CI 0.606-0.882, p=0.001), non-Hodgkin lymphoma (K 0.640, SE 0.07, 95% CI 0.472-0.807, p=0.001), carcinoma (K 0.723, SE 0.069, 95% CI 0.528-0.918, p=0.001), and mycobacterial infection (K 0.726, SE 0.07, 95% CI 0.618-0.833, p=0.001). Conclusions The most common lymph node pathologies in CHBAH are malignancies, nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia, necrotizing granulomatous inflammation and HIV reactive nodes. The distribution of disease differs in HIV positive patients. Overall, adequate FNA samples of lymph nodes have been found to have good correlation with lymph node biopsy findings in our setting. Introduction Lymphadenopathy is a common presentation of disease in South Africa (SA), particularly in the era of Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) and tuberculosis (TB) co-infection. There are only a limited number of studies that have described lymph node pathology in Southern Africa. Chris Hani Baragwanath Academic Hospital (CHBAH) which is a provincial tertiary hospital in Soweto, Johannesburg, contains close to 3300 beds and is the largest hospital in Africa [ 1 ]. In a post-mortem study performed in Johannesburg, TB was found to be the cause of death in the majority of patients (69%) with advanced Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome (AIDS), both before and after starting antiretroviral therapy [ 2 ]. Lymph nodes were the fourth most common site of positive mycobacterial cultures in this study (16%), following liver, spleen and lung [ 2 ]. The study also suggested that TB often goes unrecognized in these patients, and can be accompanied by other infections or neoplasms [ 2 ]. Excisional biopsy of a clinically appropriate lymph node is traditionally favored as a diagnostic procedure for a multitude of infectious and neoplastic disorders. However, fine-needle aspiration (FNA) is replacing it as a first line diagnostic procedure, reserving excisional biopsy for non-diagnostic FNA results that require further investigation [ 3 – 4 ]. We conducted a retrospective review of the lymph node pathology identified at CHBAH to contribute to medical knowledge of common causes of lymphadenopathy requiring biopsy in South Africa, and the role of FNA as an adjunct to excisional lymph node biopsy. Methods The study was a retrospective descriptive audit conducted at Chris Hani Baragwanath Academic Hospital, Johannesburg, South Africa. The study population consisted of all patients over the age of 12 years who underwent lymph node biopsies at CHBAH between 1 January 2010 and 31 December 2012. Lymph node biopsy reports were extracted from the database of the National Health Laboratory Service (NHLS) Division of Anatomical Pathology. Patient demographic details were obtained from the laboratory requisition forms submitted by attending clinicians and from pathology reports. There was no review of patient charts or files. Further information regarding HIV status (determined with antibody testing using 4 th generation HIV enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay), CD4 count (cells/ul), if relevant, and FNAs performed (smears in our setting) were accessed through the NHLS DisaLab system, using the patients’ name and hospital number. Consent was not obtained from participants for their records to be used as patient information was anonymized and de-identified prior to analysis. All lymph node biopsy and FNA biopsy specimens were analyzed by pathologists employed by the NHLS at CHBAH, including one of the authors. Unfortunately, the authors reviewed not all the slides during this retrospective study. Histologic diagnoses were based on morphologic findings and, in the appropriate context, ancillary tests including Mycobacterial staining and culture, immunohistochemistry, FISH (fluorescent in-situ hybridization) techniques, and flow cytometry. The World Health Organization diagnostic criteria were applied to the histopathology demonstrated on lymph node biopsies. The diagnosis of tuberculous lymphadenitis was confirmed by Ziehl-Neelsen stain for acid fast bacilli and positive Mycobacterial culture. Mycobacterial culture and Mycobacterial PCR testing using Xpert MTB/RIF are performed in cases of clinically and histopathologically (granulomatous) suspected Mycobacterial lymphadenitis. Ziehl-Neelsen stains for acid fast bacilli were routinely performed on all lymph node biopsies and FNA biopsies that displayed granulomatous inflammation (necrotizing and non-necrotizing). HIV lymphadenitis was graded as follows: grade 1 if associated with hyperplastic features and enlargement of germinal centres with increased apoptosis and phagocytosis by macrophages; grade 2 if there was a reduction in lymphoid follicles and mature lymphocytes, but an increase in plasma cells and perifollicular blood vessels; and grade 3 if the germinal centres were sclerotic. HIV lymphadenitis was not used as a surrogate marker for HIV infection, as it describes a histological pattern not entirely specific to HIV. There were no specific exclusion criteria. Specimens deemed unsuitable for histopathologic or cytologic analysis by the pathologist were excluded. Unsuitable samples were attributed to tissue samples not representative of lymphoid tissue or poor quality specimens that could not be evaluated. Non-diagnostic specimens were included in the analysis. In cases where a single patient had more than one lymph node biopsy during the period of the study, the repeat biopsies were evaluated as a subset. A total of 203 reports of FNAs preceding lymph node biopsy were evaluated. Findings of the FNA were analyzed in conjunction with adequacy of FNA samples submitted for cytology, prior to comparison with biopsy. Of the FNAs, 23% were inadequate for assessment, 76% were adequate and in 1% adequacy was not commented on. Statistical analysis was performed using STATA version 12 for Windows (StataCorp LP, Texas; www.stata.com ) and GraphPadQuickCalcs (GraphPad Software Inc, California; www.graphpad.com ). A statistician was consulted. The Human Research Ethics committee at the University of the Witwatersrand granted ethical approval of this study (Clearance Certificate number M130626). Results The majority of patients (55%) who had lymph node biopsies were female ( Table 1 ). Over 90% of patients were of the African/black racial group, in keeping with the racial demographic distribution in SA. The median age of patients was 40 years, with a minimum of 12 and a maximum of 94. The most common indication for biopsy was an uncertain diagnosis (more than two differential diagnoses entertained), followed by a suspicion for lymphoma, carcinoma and TB ( Table 2 ). Table 1. Incisional, Excisional and Core Biopsy Diagnosis in relation to Demographics. Demographic Malignancy, n = 219 Necrotizing nodes, n = 125 HIV 1 React nodes, n = 46 Not definitive, n = 170 TOTAL, n = 560 KS 2 NHL 3 CHL 4 Car 5 Other Subtotal n(%) GENDER Male 9 29 27 21 11 97 58 17 80 252(45) Female 5 31 22 58 6 122 67 29 90 308(55) RACE Asian 1 1 2 3(0.5) Black 14 58 46 69 16 203 116 44 160 523(93.3) White 2 9 11 3 1 9 24(4.2) Mixed Race 2 1 1 4 4 1 1 10(2) AGE Median 40 (12–94) AGE BAND 12-20yr 4 5 2 11 11 1 18 41(7.3) 21-35yr 11 13 20 13 9 66 59 22 40 187(33.4) 36-50yr 3 25 17 31 6 82 46 20 53 201(36) 51-65yr 15 7 25 1 48 8 3 48 107(19) >65yr 3 8 1 12 1 11 24(4.3) Open in a new tab 1 HIV: Human Immunodeficiency Virus 2 KS: Kaposi sarcoma 3 NHL: non-Hodgkin Lymphoma 4 CHL: Hodgkin Lymphoma 5 Car: carcinoma. Table 2. Incisional, Excisional and Core Biopsy Diagnosis in relation to Indications and site of biopsy. Malignancy, n = 219 Necrotizing nodes, n = 125 HIV 1 React nodes, n = 46 Not definitive, n = 170 TOTAL, n = 560 KS 2 NHL 3 CHL 4 Car 5 Other Subtotal n(%) INDICATION FOR BIOPSY Suspect TB 6 1 5 2 2 1 11 46 9 8 74(13) Suspect lymphoma 2 33 28 4 4 71 17 13 19 120(22) Staging Car 5 60 60 54 114(20) Uncertain Diagnosis 9 16 14 12 9 60 45 20 77 202(36) Unknown 2 6 5 1 3 17 17 4 12 50(9) SITE OF LYMPH NODE BIOPSY Cervical 8 26 37 18 9 98 73 16 31 218(40) Inguinal 2 10 2 10 1 25 5 9 19 58(10) Axilla 2 11 7 35 6 61 18 12 38 129(23) Other 2 13 3 16 1 35 29 9 82 155(27) Open in a new tab 1 HIV: Human Immunodeficiency Virus 2 KS: Kaposi sarcoma 3 NHL: non-Hodgkin Lymphoma 4 CHL: Hodgkin Lymphoma 5 Car: carcinoma 6 TB: Tuberculosis. The most frequent site of biopsy was cervical, followed by “other” which included laparoscopic biopsies (of intra-abdominal nodes), axillary and inguinal nodal biopsies. Laparoscopic biopsies were performed as part of a staging or curative process for patients known to have carcinoma. In other instances, lymph nodes were found incidentally at laparotomy or laparoscopy and sampled. Interestingly, of the suspected lymphoma cases, 28% were diagnosed with non-Hodgkin lymphoma, and 23% with Hodgkin lymphoma. Of the suspected TB cases, 62% displayed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation on biopsy, while 53% of staging carcinoma had confirmed carcinoma on biopsy. Overall, malignancy was the largest biopsy pathology group (39%), with 36% of this group having carcinoma and 27% non-Hodgkin lymphoma. 22% of the total sample comprised nodes that displayed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation (including histopathology demonstrating definite mycobacterial infection and suspicious for mycobacterial infection). 8% of the sample comprised HIV reactive nodes and in the remainder no specific pathology was identified (including nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia). KS accounted for 2.5% of lymph node pathology in the sample. Concomitant lymph node pathology was diagnosed within four cases of nodal KS (29% of the subset). The co-existing pathologies were mycobacterial infection in one patient, Castleman disease in two patients, and a combination of mycobacterial infection and Castleman disease in the fourth patient. HIV positive patients constituted 49% of the sample, with the majority of patients in this subset (64%) having a CD4 count below 350 cells/ul. 27% were HIV negative and in the remaining nodes, the HIV status of patients was unknown. The most common lymph node pathologies in HIV positive patients were mycobacterial infection (31%), HIV reactive nodes (15%), non-Hodgkin lymphoma (15%) and non-specific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia (15%) ( Table 3 ). Only 8.7% were of Hodgkin lymphoma. In contrast, the most common lymph node pathologies in HIV negative patients were nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia (45%), carcinoma (25%) and Mycobacterial infection (11%). In this group, non-Hodgkin lymphoma and Hodgkin lymphoma constituted 9% and 8%, respectively. Table 3. Incisional, Excisional and Core Biopsy diagnosis in relation to HIV status. HIV Status Malignancy, n = 219 Necrotizing nodes, n = 125 HIV 1 React nodes, n = 46 Not definitive, n = 170 TOTAL, n = 560 KS 2 NHL 3 CHL 4 Car 5 Other Subtotal n(%) Subtotals 14 60 49 79 17 219 125 46 170 560(100) HIV 1 NEGATIVE 14 12 37 1 64 17 1 68 150(27) HIV 1 UNKNOWN 1 6 13 28 1 49 23 4 60 136(24) HIV 1 POSITIVE 13 40 24 14 15 106 85 41 42 274(49) CD4 6 BAND (cells/ul) <100 7 4 3 14 35 2 6 57(10) 100–200 3 13 5 5 2 28 19 5 6 58(10.4) 201–350 5 8 8 1 6 28 10 12 11 61(11) 351–500 2 2 5 1 10 1 7 4 22(4) >500 1 3 3 4 11 2 5 6 24(4.3) Nil/Unknown 2 7 2 4 15 18 10 9 52(9.3) Open in a new tab 1 HIV: Human Immunodeficiency Virus 2 KS: Kaposi sarcoma 3 NHL: non-Hodgkin Lymphoma 4 CHL: Hodgkin Lymphoma 5 Car: carcinoma 6 CD4: Cluster of Differentiation 4 T-cells. Amongst the cases with non-Hodgkin lymphoma, 66% were HIV positive. Of the HIV positive subset, 50% were diagnosed with Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL) and 17.5% with Burkitt lymphoma. Plasmablastic lymphoma, Intermediate Burkitt/ DLBCL and Anaplastic Large Cell Lymphoma each contributed a further 10% to the HIV positive group. In contrast, DLBCL was found in only 21% of the HIV negative group. Only one HIV negative case was classified as HIV lymphadenitis. This diagnosis was influenced by incorrectly specified clinical information regarding HIV status in the laboratory request form. As mentioned earlier, HIV lymphadenitis is a histological pattern not entirely specific to HIV and therefore, cannot be used as a surrogate marker for HIV infection. There were 203 (36%) patients in our study sample who also underwent FNAs prior to biopsy. Of the FNAs, 23% were inadequate for assessment, 76% were adequate and in 1% adequacy was not commented on. When compared with the histopathology diagnosis as per lymph node biopsies, FNA was found to have statistically significant good agreement/reliability with regards to Hodgkin lymphoma (K 0.774, SE 0.07, 95% CI 0.606–0.882, p = 0.001), non-Hodgkin lymphoma (K 0.640, SE 0.07, 95% CI 0.472–0.807, p = 0.001), carcinoma (K 0.723, SE 0.069, 95% CI 0.528–0.918, p = 0.001), and mycobacterial infection (K 0.726, SE 0.07, 95% CI 0.618–0.833, p = 0.001) ( Table 4 ). Table 4. Statistical Agreement between LN FNA and LN Biopsy. Pathological Diagnosis % observed agreements % agreements expected by chance Kappa SE 95% Confidence Interval P-value CHL 1 94.09 76.92 0.744 0.07 0.606–0.882 0.001 NHL 2 92.61 79.50 0.640 0.07 0.472–0.807 0.001 Car 3 96.55 87.55 0.723 0.069 0.528–0.918 0.001 Granulom inflam NOS 4 97.04 95.20 0.385 0.07 -0.005–0.775 0.001 MI 5 89.16 60.49 0.726 0.07 0.618–0.833 0.001 Non-specific reactive LNH 6 80.30 67.28 0.398 0.067 0.249–0.547 0.001 Open in a new tab 1 CHL: Hodgkin Lymphoma 2 NHL: non Hodgkin Lymphoma 3 Car: carcinoma 4 Granulom inflam NOS: Granulomatous Inflammation not otherwise specified 5 MI: Mycobacterial Infection 6 Non-specific reactive LNH: non-specific reactive lymph node hyperplasia. There were two cases where a single patient had more than one lymph node biopsy during the period of the study. Both patients were HIV positive with CD4 counts below 200 cells/ul. FNAs were performed prior to biopsy in both cases and were not definitive. The sites of the biopsies were, respectively, axillary and cervical in the first patient, and cervical both times, in the second patient. The definitive biopsies were both repeated due to a high index of clinical suspicion, and subsequently confirmed to be non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Discussion Our study affirmed local demographic patterns, in that the majority of patients who underwent lymph node biopsy at CHBAH were middle-aged African/black females. We have demonstrated that nodal pathology diagnoses at CHBAH are inclusive of malignancy, necrotizing granulomatous inflammation and reactive nodes (due to HIV or other). The most common lymph node pathologies at CHBAH as per biopsy are in keeping with local African literature [ 5 – 7 ]. However, our data is unique in that it also describes differences in pathology between HIV positive and negative patients, the former further stratified by CD4 count. Our study highlights the known significant correlation between HIV incidence and incidence of aggressive B-cell lymphomas and Hodgkin lymphoma [ 8 – 9 ]. Associated risk factors for the development of these malignancies are high HIV viral loads and low CD4 counts, although malignancies may occur at any CD4 count. The pathogenesis is thought to be immune dysregulation with loss of T-cell immunity against onco-viruses like Epstein-Barr Virus and human herpesvirus 8 [ 8 ]. The most common indications for requesting a lymph node biopsy in our setting were an uncertain diagnosis and the suspicion of malignancy or TB, which reflects the relative dominance of the latter two diseases in Southern Africa. The global prevalence and death rates from TB are on the decline. However, in contrast, Africa is showing an increase in the tuberculous burden [ 10 ]. In a study performed at CHBAH, TB was diagnosed in 1291 patients over a period of two months [ 1 ]. The association between HIV infection and TB is well described in South Africa. The high prevalence of TB is also reflected by HIV statistics for SA [ 11 ]. South Africa carries the highest global burden of HIV with an estimated 5.6 million people infected, with TB being the most common serious opportunistic infection [ 11 – 13 ]. The previous CHBAH study showed that of the patients diagnosed with TB, 74% had pulmonary TB and of the patients with extrapulmonary TB, pleural and miliary were the most common forms [ 1 ]. However, more recent publications suggest that TB lymphadenitis is the most common form of extrapulmonary TB [ 3 – 4 , 14 ]. The high prevalence of TB and TB suspicious lymphadenitis at CHBAH has been described, notably in the age band 25–44 years [ 15 ]. In our study, 22% of lymph node biopsies and 27% of FNAs comprised nodes that displayed necrotizing granulomatous inflammation (including histopathology and cytology demonstrating definite mycobacterial infection and suspicious for mycobacterial infection), confirming the high prevalence of TB in CHBAH. The study by Martinson et al stated that the risk of TB increases at lower CD4 counts, and suggested that early antiretroviral therapy reduced the population prevalence of TB in HIV infected patients [ 12 ]. This suggestion may also be evident in our study as the majority of HIV positive patients in our sample had CD4 counts less than 350 cells/ul and the commonest lymph node pathology in this group was confirmed and suspected TB. There is a wide differential diagnosis for peripheral TB lymphadenitis. Patients with lymphoma in rural KwaZulu Natal were misdiagnosed based on clinical similarities to TB and were placed on empiric TB treatment, delaying the lymphoma diagnosis by a median of five months [ 16 ]. The dangers of misdiagnosing TB, and subsequent empiric TB treatment, are progression of underlying disease (malignancy or other infection); toxicity of TB therapy; and development of drug-resistant therapy [ 11 , 17 ]. A Zambian study suggested that primary HIV lymphadenopathy was a significant cause of superficial lymphadenopathy [ 18 ]. This finding is also highlighted in our study, as HIV reactive nodes comprised 8% of the total sample, and was amongst the largest nodal pathology group in HIV positive patients (15%). HIV-associated lymphadenitis (reported in our setting as HIV reactive nodes) is a well-characterized pattern of histological findings in lymph nodes of many HIV-infected individuals. It is likely due to the lymphotropism of the HI virus. Grade 1 is associated with hyperplastic features, enlargement of germinal centres with increased apoptosis and phagocytosis by macrophages. In Grade 2 there is a reduction in lymphoid follicles and mature lymphocytes, but an increase in plasma cells and perifollicular blood vessels. In Grade 3, the germinal centres become sclerotic [ 19 ]. Rare causes of lymphadenopathy in our study included one case of Cryptococcosis (microbiologically confirmed) in an HIV positive male patient who had profound immunosuppression (CD4 count of 8 cells/ul). In addition, there was a case of myeloid sarcoma (extramedullary counterpart of chronic myeloid leukaemia) in an HIV positive female who had a CD4 count of 1626 cells/ul. The association between KS and multicentric Castleman disease due to the common causal agent human herpesvirus 8 (HHV8) is well described in the literature [ 20 ]. This association has been demonstrated by the co-existence of both these pathologies in lymph nodes in our study. Interestingly, two patients in our study with coexisting KS and Castleman disease had CD4 counts above 350 cells/ul. CD4 counts were not available for the remaining two patients who had concomitant lymph node pathology. A possible explanation for the relatively high CD4 counts may be immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome. We then evaluated the outcomes of fine needle aspiration biopsy in our setting. FNA is widely regarded as the diagnostic modality of choice in diagnosing TB lymphadenitis [ 10 , 21 ]. Our study affirms a good correlation between FNA diagnosis and biopsy diagnosis on lymph node specimens. In the event of inadequate FNA samples, the result should be treated with reserve. If there is a clinical concern for nodal-based mycobacterial disease or malignancy despite a non-contributory FNA, a clinically appropriate lymph node should be submitted for histopathologic assessment. In the event of a persistent clinical concern, despite a negative histopathology result, the nodal biopsy should be repeated with emphasis on selection of a clinically appropriate node. The importance of a repeat biopsy where there is a high clinical suspicion for disease, particularly in HIV positive patients, was highlighted in the two patients from our study subsequently diagnosed with non-Hodgkin lymphoma [ 16 ]. Our findings suggest that negative biopsy findings in HIV positive patients should be treated with reserve if there is a high index of clinical suspicion for lymphoma. Limitations of the study include its selection bias, retrospective nature, and possible information bias as data was extracted from a database in which variable SNOMED codes for nodes at different topographic sites may be used. However, our findings correlate well with both local and international literature on the subject. In correlating FNA with excisional lymph node biopsy, there is scope for prospective studies to directly compare the two modalities and develop clinical algorithms taking into account the clinical and demographic profile of patients who should proceed directly to lymph node biopsy in order to expedite diagnosis and treatment. Conclusions Our study showed that the most common lymph node pathologies occurring in patients who underwent biopsies at CHBAH are malignancies, nonspecific reactive lymphoid hyperplasia, necrotizing granulomatous inflammation due to mycobacterial infection, and HIV reactive nodes. The distribution of disease differs in HIV positive patients. FNA was found to have good overall correlation with histopathology biopsy diagnoses in our setting. The diagnosis specified in an FNA report should be interpreted in conjunction with the comment/s about adequacy of the aspirated specimen for assessment. Due to the coexistence of nodal KS with other pathologies, patients who have suspected nodal KS would benefit from proceeding directly to have biopsies for histopathological assessment. Supporting Information S1 Dataset. FNA Stratification. (XLS) Click here for additional data file. (64KB, xls) Acknowledgments We would like to acknowledge the staff at the NHLS for their help during data collection, and the masters in epidemiology students who assisted with data analysis. Dr Alison Bentley provided invaluable academic guidance. And finally, we would like to acknowledge our patients at Chris Hani Baragwanath Academic Hospital who inspire us daily. Data Availability All relevant data are within the paper and its supporting information files. Funding Statement Professor Venter is supported by PEPFAR (President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief). Dr. Pather was supported by SATBAT/FIC (South Africa Tuberculosis AIDS Training/Fogarty International Center) grant 3U2RTW007370-05S1. There was no specific funding from PEPFAR or SATBAT/FIC for this study. References 1. Edginton ME, Wong ML, Phofa R, Mahlaba D, Hodkinson HJ (2005) Tuberculosis at Chris Hani Baragwanath Hospital: numbers of patients diagnosed and outcomes of referrals to district clinics. International Journal of Tuberculosis and Lung Disease : 9(4):398–402. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Wong EB, Omar T, Setlhako GJ, Osih R, Feldman C, Murdoch DM et al. (2012) Causes of Death on Antiretroviral Therapy: A Post-Mortem Study from South Africa. Public Library of Science (PLoS) ONE : 7(10):e47542 10.1371/journal.pone.0047542 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Handa U, Mundi I, Mohan S (2012) Nodal tuberculosis revisited: a review. Journal of Infection in Developing Countries : 6(1):6–12. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Fontanilla JM, Barnes A, Fordham von Reyn C (2011) Current Diagnosis and Management of Peripheral Tuberculous Lymphadenitis. Clinical Infectious Diseases : 53(6):555–562. 10.1093/cid/cir454 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Muthuphei MN (1998) Cervical lymphadenopathy at Ga-Rankuwa Hospital (South Africa): a histological review. Central African Journal of Medicine : 44(12):311–2. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Sibanda EN, Stanczuk G (1993) Lymph node pathology in Zimbabwe: a review of 2194 specimens. Quarterly Journal of Medicine : 86(12):811–817. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Olu-eddo AN, Omoti CE (2011) Diagnostic evaluation of primary cervical adenopathies in a developing country. Pan African Medical Journal :10:52 [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Kaplan LD (2012) HIV-associated lymphoma. Best Practice and Research Clinical Haematology : 25:101–117. 10.1016/j.beha.2012.01.001 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Wiggill TM, Mantina H, Willem P, Perner Y, Stevens WS (2011) Changing Pattern of Lymphoma Subgroups at a Tertiary Academic Complex in a High-Prevalence HIV Setting: A South African Perspective. Journal of Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndromes : 56(5):460–466. 10.1097/QAI.0b013e31820bb06a [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Department of Health. Republic of South Africa. National Tuberculosis Management Guidelines (2009). [ Google Scholar ] 11. Schutz C, Ismail Z, Proxenos CJ, Marais S, Burton R, Kenyon C et al. (2013) Burden of antituberculosis and antiretroviral drug-induced liver injury at a secondary hospital in South Africa. South African Medical Journal : 102(6):506–511. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Martinson NA, Hoffmann CJ, Chaisson RE (2011) Epidemiology of Tuberculosis and HIV: Recent advances in Understanding and Responses. Proceedings of the American Thoracic Society : 8:288–293. 10.1513/pats.201010-064WR [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Churchyard GJ, Mametja LD, Mvusi L, Ndjeka N, Hesseling AC, Reid A et al. (2014) Tuberculosis control in South Africa: Successes, challenges and recommendations. South African Medical Journal : 104(3):244–248. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Ligthelm LJ, Nicol MP, Hoek KGP, Jacobson R, van Helden PD, Marais BJ et al. (2011) Xpert MTB/RIF for Rapid Diagnosis of Tuberculous Lymphadenitis from Fine-Needle-Aspiration Biopsy Specimens. Journal of Clinical Microbiology : 49(11):3967 10.1128/JCM.01310-11 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Karstaedt AS (2014) Extrapulmonary tuberculosis among adults: Experience at Chris Hani Baragwanath Academic Hospital, Johannesburg, South Africa. South African Medical Journal : 104(1):22–24. 10.7196/samj.6374 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Puvaneswaran B, Shoba B (2013) Misdiagnosis of tuberculosis in patients with lymphoma. South African Medical Journal : 103(1):32–33. 10.7196/samj.6093 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Streicher FM, Muller B, Chihota V, Mlambo C, Tait M, Pillay M et al. (2012) Emergence and treatment of multidrug resistant (MDR) and extensively drug-resistant (XDR) tuberculosis in South Africa. Infection , Genetics and Evolution : 12(4):686–694. 10.1016/j.meegid.2011.07.019 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Bem C, Patil PS, Bharucha H, Namaambo K, Luo N (1996) Importance of human immunodeficiency virus-associated lymphadenopathy and tuberculous lymphadenitis in patients undergoing lymph node biopsy in Zambia. British Journal of Surgery : 83(1):75–78. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Mahe E, Ross C, Sur M (2011) Lymphoproliferative Lesions in the Setting of HIV Infection: A Five-Year Retrospective Case Series and Review. Pathology Research International. Volume 2011 10.4061/2011/618760 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Dupin N, Fisher C, Kellam P, Ariad S, Tulliez M, Franck N et al. (1999). Distribution of human herpesvirus-8 latently infected cells in Kaposi’s Sarcoma, multicentric Castleman’s disease, and primary effusion lymphoma. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America : 96(8):4546–4551. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Razack R, Louw M, Wright CA (2014) Diagnostic yield of fine needle aspiration biopsy in HIV-infected adults with suspected mycobacterial lymphadenitis. South African Medical Journal :104(1):27–28. 10.7196/samj.7492 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials S1 Dataset. FNA Stratification. (XLS) Click here for additional data file. (64KB, xls) Data Availability Statement All relevant data are within the paper and its supporting information files.',35,'2025-10-15 11:35:50','2025-10-15 11:35:50');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5815,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9790188','Pragmatic clinical trial design in emergency medicine: Study considerations and design types.','Gettel CJ, Yiadom MYAB, Bernstein SL, Grudzen CR, Nath B, Li F, Hwang U, Hess EP, Melnick ER.','2022','lellouch pendulum collaboratory collaboratory stagnant overwhelms tolerating resultantly loudon paring proceduralist foresight rushing schmucker yiadom melnick opportunistically rigidly allowance endopints resultantly crash2 crash2 biasing biostatisticians intuitional convening practicably efic efic intoxicated kalkman broadcast bulleted vaso balamuth melnick alshahrani abernethy charlesworth rickard grudzen kerber vaillancourt noncritically 2x2 reminiscence securement translatable collaboratory u24at009676 uh3da047003 gettel p30ag021342 r03ag073988 imbedded collaboratory u54ag063546 yiadom grudzen ug3at009844 uh3at009844 broderick ranney vaca califf aberle tasneem e124 lellouch therapeutical califf norrie stoney videocast uh3 unitedhealth bamlanivimab cov555 investor unitedhealth collaboratory rethinkingclinicaltrials nihdatabook semler dcricollab dcri nihkr carrithers pragmatism loudon treweek donnan zwarenstein h2147 neta dember collaboratory janz kanzler mcgeary mcgeary taljaard brehaut eldridge peduzzi kerns quinones loudon zwarenstein biros droperidol ziprasidone schmicker daya schmucker flannery goldfeld grudzen empalla empalla telephonic yiadom myab domenico e019600 dewitt semler wanderer noncritically melnick nath e200003 khoo chinna myfait e0199219 kharbanda e182430 collaboratory rethinkingclinicaltrials collaboratory rethinkingclinicaltrials vollmer heagerty collaboratory gallis collaboratory rethinkingclinicaltrials lokhnygina heagerty wears marquis gravel caulfield kalkman syh gjmw grobbee delden ohrp kesselheim e197591 intoxicated thehastingscenter irb_article septimus vollmer petrik beskow ohrp _ftn1 richesson marsolo douthit collaboratory ocab202 rifai itchhaporia virani e2140212 sinsky bavafa collaboratory rethinkingclinicaltrials balamuth kittick mcbridge melnick jeffery dziura e028488 alshahrani alsulaibikh eltahan i6165 preisser lavados mcalister esserman deluca coulton e99463 dziura abernethy currow isrctn charlesworth burnell crellin reminiscence rickard securement pisces 2x2 e015291 turecamo cuthel grudzen grudzen e030099 kerber damschroder vaillancourt charette taljaard e16966','Abstract Pragmatic clinical trials (PCTs) focus on correlation between treatment and outcomes in real-world clinical practice, yet a guide highlighting key study considerations and design types for emergency medicine investigators pursuing this important study type is not available. Investigators conducting ED-based PCTs face multiple decisions within the planning phase to ensure robust and meaningful study findings. The PRagmatic Explanatory Continuum Indicator Summary 2 (PRECIS-2) tool allows trialists to consider both pragmatic and explanatory components across nine domains, shaping the trial design to the purpose intended by the investigators. Aside from the PRECIS-2 tool domains, ED-based investigators conducting PCTs should also consider randomization techniques, human subjects concerns, and integration of trial components within the electronic health record. The authors additionally highlight the advantages, disadvantages, and rationale for the use of four common randomized study design types to be considered in PCTs: parallel, crossover, factorial, and stepped-wedge. With increasing emphasis on the conduct of PCTs, emergency medicine investigators will benefit from a rigorous approach to clinical trial design. Keywords: clinical trial, pragmatic, emergency, study design Randomized clinical trials are considered the standard design to assess an intervention’s effect due to their ability to minimize confounding and potential selection biases that could influence the outcome of interest. 1 However, prior analyses of clinical trials have shown that the majority are too small in sample size, thereby lacking statistical power, or are prone to other critical issues in design and execution. 2 With explanatory clinical research often conducted in highly-controlled patient populations with strict inclusion criteria, results may not be replicated under real-world conditions. 3 To develop valid and reliable treatment effect evidence, the number of clinical trials in the emergency department (ED) setting is increasing, particularly with ‘pragmatic’ clinical trials (PCTs) focusing on the intervention effect on outcomes in routine clinical practice. The term ‘pragmatic’ was first used by Schwartz and Lellouch to describe approaches to trial design comparing two treatments under the conditions in which they would be applied in practice. 4 In general, PCTs are distinguished from explanatory trials by three key attributes: 1) an intent to inform decision-makers (e.g. patients, policymakers, clinicians) rather than elucidating a biological or social mechanism, 2) an intent to enroll a representative population relevant to the decision in practice, and 3) either an intent to streamline procedures and data collection or measuring a broad range of outcomes. 5 Few clinical trials can be fully pragmatic, but efforts to prioritize features of pragmatic trials when feasible should be considered by emergency medicine investigators given the increasing emphasis placed on dissemination and implementation of interventions with proven benefit in real-world settings. 6 – 9 As the pendulum continues to swing more towards value-based care, Medicare and other payers, as well as the National Institutes of Health (NIH), have shown an increasing interest in the conduct of PCTs. For example, Medicare and United Healthcare insurance coverage decisions are being influenced by PCTs of older adults receiving acupuncture for low back pain and neutralizing antibodies for COVID-19. 10 , 11 The NIH Health Care Systems Collaboratory, supported by the NIH Common Fund and multiple Institutes, is a particular example of a broader initiative aiming to improve the ways in which PCTs are conducted. To support the design and rapid execution of PCTs across healthcare systems, the NIH Collaboratory has also developed the electronic Living Textbook including expert consensus, standard approaches, best practices, peer-reviewed articles, and guidance documents related to the conduct of PCTs. 12 Despite the increased interest in PCTs, investigators are being increasingly asked to ‘do more with less’. Adjusted for inflation, the average R01-equivalent grant award amount has remained essentially stagnant over the last 25 years. 13 With limited funds available, investigators may encounter challenges meeting desired enrollment numbers, which runs counter to the often-needed greater enrollment within PCTs given their variability and flexibility of intervention development and assessment. To discover non-neutral results of novel interventions and treatments, ED investigators must balance the possibility of it simply being too hard to find a ‘signal’ of treatment difference that overwhelms the ‘noise’ of potential reduced sample size and increased variation inherently present within PCTs. 14 To overcome these challenges and conduct an efficient trial, the planning phase of PCTs is central to its success and requires somewhat different approaches to study design, data collection, and ethical oversight compared to traditional clinical trials. 15 However, a guide for planning and conducting ED-based PCTs is lacking despite the increasing call for their completion. This article reviews concepts related to ED-based PCTs, including: 1) the PRagmatic Explanatory Continuum Indicator Summary 2 (PRECIS-2) framework, 2) additional key study considerations, and 3) illustrative ED-based examples. The goal of this article is to provide a resource for emergency medicine trialists to reference in the study design phase when planning ED-based PCTs given intervention characteristics and available resources. The Explanatory-Pragmatic Spectrum When establishing the research question to evaluate an intervention, investigators must consider the explanatory-pragmatic spectrum. Trials are rarely completely explanatory or completely pragmatic in nature. Frequently, components of both are present, suggesting that a trial may fall somewhere on a continuum based on decisions made by investigators. Explanatory trials are conducted under ideal settings to standardize intervention delivery often with research staff on hand to enroll and administer the intervention itself. Explanatory trials tend to have fairly restrictive inclusion and exclusion criteria, often resulting in a small proportion of individuals, with comparable clinical and demographic characteristics, at risk for the condition of interest to be enrolled. Explanatory trials answer the question, ‘Does intervention X work under ideal conditions?’, and typically is aligned with clinical efficacy research. Conversely, pragmatic trials study an intervention’s effect in real-world clinical practice by capturing a more heterogeneous population and tolerating some flexibility in the delivery of the intervention. PCTs answer the question, ‘Does intervention X work in routine clinical practice?’, and is more frequently aligned with clinical effectiveness research. Resultantly, explanatory trials generally exhibit a high degree of internal validity while pragmatic trials prioritize generalizability and external validity. 16 With widespread concern that explanatory trials may be poor predictors of real-world effectiveness, the original PRECIS (PRagmatic Explanatory Continuum Indicator Summary) tool was developed to help investigators work through study design decisions to avoid designing a trial that did not meet their own intentions. 17 In 2015, the PRECIS-2 wheel diagram was developed and offers a framework guide for trialists to consider the pragmatic or explanatory nature of their trial across 9 domains, including: eligibility, recruitment, setting, organization, flexibility – delivery, flexibility – adherence, follow-up, primary outcome, and primary analysis. 17 , 18 Domains are rated from 1 (very explanatory) to 5 (very pragmatic). ED investigators should strongly consider the use of the PRECIS-2 wheel diagram during the study design phase to increase chances that results of the trial are generalizable across the intended practice settings ( Figure 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab The PRagmatic-Explanatory Continuum Indicator Summary 2 (PRECIS-2) wheel Note – Used with permission from the authors of Loudon et al. 15 PRECIS-2 Considerations in ED-based PCT Design Unique challenges exist when conducting PCTs in the ED setting that span the PRECIS-2 domains. For trials involving time-sensitive conditions in the ED, one characteristic of note is the brief therapeutic window for decisions surrounding critical procedures. 14 The PreVent trial is an example ED-based study of bag-mask ventilation during tracheal intubation that addressed pragmatic approaches to time-sensitive conditions by strategically placing randomization envelopes, ensuring broad eligibility criteria, paring down the data collection form to the minimum information required, and having a non-proceduralist that was familiar with the procedure complete the form. 14 , 19 Second, the broader ED population may also include patients who are not regular visitors to that health system, thereby limiting electronic health record data available at the point-of-care, in comparison to PCTs performed in the primary care or outpatient cardiology settings, as examples. Third, and to be discussed further below, is the added complexity surrounding capacity and consent for certain vulnerable ED populations with mental illness or substance use disorder. Researchers face multiple decisions when conducting clinical trials, with the planning phase of the trial serving as a critical step to optimize robust and meaningful study findings. Lack of foresight to address potential pitfalls a priori or rushing into study enrollment can leave the investigator searching for ways to ‘save’ the trial post hoc. Detailed below, the 9 domains within the PRECIS-2 framework are expanded upon to identify decision points for investigators to consider in the design of ED-based clinical trials. 17 , 20 – 24 Each domain is not meant to be a ‘yes’ or ‘no’ in regards to a pragmatic approach, but instead the PRECIS-2 framework is rather a series of decisions to fit a trial design to the purpose intended by the investigators. We additionally provide illustrative examples of studies across disciplines that have mapped the PRECIS-2 framework domains and were considered to have taken a ‘very pragmatic’ approach within a specific domain ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Pragmatic and explanatory characteristics with illustrative ‘very pragmatic’ study examples of the PRECIS-2 domains PRECIS-2 Domain Pragmatic Explanatory ‘Very Pragmatic’ Example ED Study Eligibility criteria – Who is selected to participate in the trial? Broad eligibility criteria Limited inclusion criteria Martel et al. 25 Recruitment – How are participants recruited into the trial? Recruitment through usual care Extra effort beyond usual care (e.g. targeted letters, endorsement from professional societies) Wang et al. 26 Setting – Where is the trial being done? Diverse patient recruitment locations that reflect intended broader population Single center or only specialized academic medical center EDs Schmucker et al. 27 Organization – What expertise and resources are needed to deliver the intervention? Uses existing clinical workflow Relies on increased staffing, training requirements, or other resources Yiadom et al. 28 Flexibility (delivery) – How should the intervention be delivered? Non-rigid prescription of intervention delivery or implementation Strict protocol with measures to improve intervention compliance Bernstein et al. 29 Flexibility (adherence) – What measures are in place to ensure participants adhere to the intervention? Allows interaction with the intervention as able by the end user Monitors patient compliance or adherence to the intervention, with potential exclusion Self et al. 30 Follow-up – How closely are participants followed up? No increased follow-up in comparison to usual care More frequent and/or longer follow-up visits Melnick et al. 31 Primary outcome – How relevant is it to participants? Inherent importance to key stakeholders Surrogate outcomes, composite outcomes, or outcomes measured at an earlier time than usual care Tan et al. 32 Primary analysis – To what extent are all data included? Intention-to-treat analyses Consideration of per-protocol analyses Hess et al. 33 Open in a new tab Eligibility criteria – Enrolling all individuals in a clinical setting with minimal eligibility criteria is a hallmark of PCTs. If an intervention has been previously shown to not be beneficial for children, then a reasonable pragmatic design decision would be to exclude only that population. However, if there remains clinical equipoise regarding an intervention’s efficacy across populations of interest, then the most pragmatic approach would be to have very limited exclusion criteria. Recruitment – Pragmatic approaches to recruitment might consider only patients who present for healthcare with a condition of interest, without overt recruitment effort. ED trials may be more likely to fall towards the pragmatic end of the spectrum for this domain, insofar as investigators opportunistically enroll participants who present for acute clinical care rather than relying on healthy volunteers or individuals seen in clinic settings with pre-diagnosed conditions (e.g. cancer). Setting – Trialists must consider the match between the trial setting and where the results will be applied. At a glance, it may seem that all ED trials would be pragmatic with respect to the setting. However, conducting a trial in highly-resourced EDs with immediate access to neuro- and cardiac-intensivists as well as trauma may limit the pragmatic nature if the findings are intended for a broader population. Conducting the trial in diverse ED settings will increase the trial’s PRECIS-2 pragmatic score. Organization – A central tenet to this domain is the expertise and resources needed to deliver the intervention, with the goal of a PCT being to add the ‘successful’ intervention to the usual care of the condition or population of interest without additional resources or healthcare staff. This domain offers a high degree of risk for trialists to move away from the pragmatic end of the spectrum, given a potential reliance on a greater number of staff, increased training requirements, or additional resources. Flexibility (delivery) – Real-world clinical practice suggests that clinicians will implement an intervention with varying fidelity to the protocol. A pragmatic approach to this domain would be for investigators to not rigidly prescribe intervention implementation. Specific mention in the study protocol of allowance of co-interventions, managing side effects of the intervention, or tightly defining intervention delivery to maximize effect suggests a more explanatory approach. Flexibility (adherence) – Variation in adherence to clinical interventions is a reality of designing trials. Highly pragmatic studies have limited compliance or engagement requirements, and instead allow the end user to modify the intervention within certain constraints. This includes observing variation in compliance and not excluding non-adherent patients in order to capture whether the intervention will have an impact on outcomes despite the presence of this real-world challenge. Follow-up – Compared to usual care, a pragmatic approach to this domain involves trials that have no more follow-up than usual care and do not rely on additional data collection. ED trialists are typically interested in the brief follow-up time period immediately after receipt of the intervention. If follow-up does not differ in care management compared to usual care, then trialists may gather short-term outcomes without undue burden to patients or compromise to the pragmatic nature of the domain. Primary outcome - A general principle of PCTs is that there should be relatively few endpoints, and the ones chosen should be easily measured and salient to stakeholders. If unknown at the time of investigation, outcomes of significance to stakeholders may be determined by gathering qualitative data through interviews or focus groups. Pragmatic measurement of outcomes may also be completed by EHR surveillance, as opposed to the intensive follow-up assessments often done with more explanatory trials. Surrogate outcomes (e.g., process outcomes or other lead metrics) can be misleading due to potential non-linear relationships between some surrogate endpoints and the clinical outcome of interest. However, these may be more readily available and thus appropriate for interpreting the impact of identified results. Composite endpoints, combining at least two component outcomes of interest, may be difficult to interpret given the wide variability of clinical importance with regards to individual component endopints. 34 Resultantly, the pragmatic nature of a study may be decreased when investigators select surrogate outcomes only important to clinicians (e.g., blood test), use a composite outcome with component parts not important to patients, or measure short-term outcomes when longer-term outcomes are more relevant. 35 Primary analysis – Using all available data in an intention-to-treat analysis is generally recognized as a more pragmatic approach. Excluding non-adherent participants (per protocol analysis) or study sites that recruit a lower volume would suggest a more explanatory approach to this domain. However, a per protocol analysis may be considered as a secondary or explanatory analysis for future hypothesis generation. While ED-based PCTs are available and discussed further in the following sections, few have explicitly mapped their study to the PRECIS-2 wheel diagram. CRASH2 trial is one well-known example of an ED-based pragmatic trial that started with the end in mind. Researchers first thought how the outcome data would be used in clinical practice, forcing investigators to shift along the spectrum from a more explanatory approach to a more pragmatic approach to allow clinicians to use anti-fibrinolytic therapy in ‘patients who the treating clinician thinks are bleeding or at significant risk of bleeding.’ As a PCT, CRASH2 benefited from its broad inclusion criteria, generalizability of findings, and a reduced risk of bias incorporation. 16 , 36 Another example of an ED-based pragmatic trial is the Resuscitation Outcomes Consortium Pragmatic Airway Resuscitation Trial (PART) comparing the effectiveness of initial laryngeal tube insertion and endotracheal tube insertion on outcomes in adult out-of-hospital cardiac arrest. The PART benefited from a ‘very pragmatic’ approach in the eligibility, recruitment, setting, organization, flexibility (adherence), and follow-up domains of the PRECIS-2 framework. 26 Additional Study Considerations for PCTs Aside from domains within the PRECIS-2 framework, ED-based investigators must also consider several other decision points during the design phase. While not completely exhaustive, this list includes: randomization, human subjects concerns, and EHR integration. Randomization Randomization is the process of assigning participants by chance to groups that receive different treatments, without influence by the investigator. Randomization offers two critical benefits, namely the reduction of selection bias and the avoidance of known and, more importantly, unknown confounders. Bias in clinical trials is described as a systematic error that encourages one result or outcome over another based on methodological errors. While chance imbalance may arise, 37 selection bias includes systematic differences in baseline characteristics between the compared groups and can generally be overcome with adequate randomization techniques and trial sample size. Specific techniques of randomization depend on the randomization unit, including individual participants or clusters (e.g. entire EDs, health systems) with cluster randomization particularly relevant to PCTs. Individual randomization results in each participant receiving an experimental intervention, a comparator therapy (e.g., usual care), or a placebo when no interaction among trial participants is anticipated after randomization. Cluster randomization involves groups of participants being assigned to the same study conditions in which interaction among participants within the same clustered is anticipated or unavoidable before and after randomization. Key questions for investigators to consider in determining if individual or cluster randomization is appropriate include 38 : Is the phenomenon of interest something that takes place primarily at the level of the individual patient or study participant (e.g. response to an experimental drug)? Is the proposed intervention delivered at the level of the group (e.g. adoption of a new treatment guideline)? If randomized at the individual level, would it be difficult for clinicians to avoid contamination? Cluster randomized trials are preferred when there is potential for significant treatment contamination – i.e. participants randomized to the control group may receive aspects of the intervention. Contamination has the potential to reduce the study’s reliability and can also decrease the observed differences between groups, therefore biasing results toward the null hypothesis. 39 To be discussed below, two key examples of cluster randomized trials include parallel and stepped-wedge designs. 40 Of note, clinician-level randomization may be considered either in individual or cluster randomized techniques depending on the target of the intervention and the objective of the trial. From an analytic perspective, it is important to note that outcomes of participants within a cluster may be correlated. The intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) is a standard measure of how similar outcomes of individuals within the same cluster are likely to be. 41 With ICC values ranging from 0 to 1, individual outcomes within clusters are increasingly correlated with one another as the ICC increases. Intuitively, if the ICC is zero, then a cluster randomized trial will often behave just like an individual level RCT. In the most extreme case when the ICC is 1, a cluster randomized trial will behave like an RCT with a sample size equal to the number of clusters because the outcomes will simply be identical within each cluster. In practice, the reported ICC values are usually in the 0.05 - 0.1 range. However, it has been noted that even a small ICC can lead to a substantial sample size inflation relative to an individual level RCT, especially when the cluster size becomes large. 42 If considering cluster randomization, the goal should be to determine the randomization unit that yields the most clusters, while still being feasible to deliver the intervention without risk of contamination. A greater number of clusters provides improved statistical properties when analyzing cluster-level data by increasing power and reducing potential imbalances of baseline characteristics. Additionally, there are several approaches to balance between-cluster differences, including pair matching, stratification, and constrained randomization. 39 , 43 Prior to planning the study, emergency investigators should partner with biostatisticians to optimize randomization approaches and determine a preliminary ICC to facilitate accurate sample size estimation. 44 Human Subjects Concerns in PCTs Ethical review from institutional review boards (IRBs) is a key component in the conduct of all clinical research, 45 with the implementation of PCTs often accompanied by unique challenges. 46 With PCTs often recruiting across several institutions, lead investigators typically will use a single, centralized IRB to eliminate redundant reviews of the same material across multiple sites with reliance agreements between local intuitional IRBs and the central IRB. These reliance agreements defer to the centralized IRB’s protocol determinations thereby overcoming the need for local amendment determinations across study sites and substantially increasing efficiency of the protection of human subjects review process. 45 To further streamline IRB review, investigators may benefit from convening calls with IRB administrators before the protocol is submitted at each institution to help understand up-front concerns that may delay participant enrollment. Regulatory boards often recommend that any human subject provide written informed consent. However, proponents of PCTs have noted that existing standards for informed consent are incompatible with a pragmatic study’s nature and intent. 47 Waivers of informed consent are becoming increasingly common, with investigators required to meet four criteria of the Common Rule (45 Code of Federal Regulations (CFR) 46.116) 48 , 49 : 1) research must present no greater than minimal risk to subjects, 2) waiver or alteration will not adversely affect the rights and welfare of the subjects, 3) research could not practicably be carried out without the waiver or alteration and, 4) whenever appropriate, the subjects will be provided with additional pertinent information after participation. To be considered by ED investigators, public approval has also been expressed for Exception from Informed Consent (EFIC) in emergency clinical trials. 50 As an ED-based example, Martel et al. 25 used an EFIC to study the treatment of acute agitation given previously described limitations with obtaining informed consent among intoxicated ED patients. 51 In collaboration with the institutional IRB, the authors additionally performed ‘community consultation’, by reviewing the study protocol with local detoxification facilities, acute psychiatric treatment facilities, and residents of facilities for homeless patients with alcohol use disorder. 25 It is important to note that the third criterion (i.e. waiver or modification of planned consent) is not met by inconvenience, cost, time, speed, or other resource demands to the team of investigators. In the case of ED-based PCTs, ‘practicality’ does not need to refer solely to the ability to conduct the study at all , but also includes if the study’s goals are unlikely to be achieved. 52 Counter to the goal of PCTs, non-routine workflow procedures associated with informed consent process can hinder recruitment, introduce selection bias, and impact generalizability. In determining if a waiver or modification of planned consent may be appropriate for ED-based PCTs, three questions pertaining to practicality outlined previously by Kalkman et al. should be considered. 48 First, ‘Is the loss of data quality caused by selection bias or inability to recruit a sufficient number of patients, and can this loss be reasonably attributed to regulatory consent?’ Second, ‘Is the expected loss of data quality so extreme that the trial is not worth doing?’ Finally, ‘Apart from modifying regulatory consent, are there no means or resources available that may be reasonably expected to counter the loss of data quality?’ Aside from a waiver of consent, additional options for consent in ED-based PCTs may include broadcast notification, 53 opt-out consent, 54 a ‘short form’ consent, 55 or electronic consent. 56 EHR Integration An additional key study consideration is the role of the EHR in ED-based PCTs. Integration of PCT interventions within existing clinical workflows and data streams is essential to limit the costs and burden of ongoing intervention testing. 57 EHR integration requires early engagement with local information technology staff to reach consensus on protocol details and to build the necessary technological infrastructure for the trial. Previous PCT studies have used the EHR for eligibility determination, intervention delivery, and outcome measurement, yet prior work has identified challenges. Primarily, these limitations have been related to a lack of EHR data interoperability across heterogeneous systems, a lack of usable interfaces and standard workflows, and the inadequate ability to collect clinically appropriate outcome data. 58 Aside from more widespread incentives needed to invest in interoperability and the routine collection of patient-reported outcomes, investigators conducting ED-based PCTs would benefit from working with their healthcare organization to promote EHR standardization (e.g. developing algorithms or computable phenotypes for patient identification) and avoiding overly customized digital interventions at individual sites in multi-center PCTs ( Figure 2 ). 57 , 59 Phenotyping allows for the identification of patients with concern for particular conditions or potential for improvement after a specific treatment or intervention. Maximizing the phenotyping potential of the EHR may be particularly critical in the time-sensitive ED environment when investigators or clinicians are attempting to overcome treatment response heterogeneity. 14 , 60 Figure 2. Open in a new tab Additional key decision points to be considered by investigators conducting ED-based pragmatic clinical trials Note – Options are bulleted below each decision point. The order of boxes is not the only order in which factors can be considered. PCT Study Design Types and Examples The various study design types discussed within this section offer unique advantages and disadvantages to be considered by emergency investigators conducting PCTs. Historically, the parallel group trial design type has been favored, but other types such as cross-over, factorial, and stepped-wedge designs are becoming more common. 61 Advantages, disadvantages, and rationale for the common randomized PCT study design types with illustrative examples are provided in Table 2 . In parallel group trials, participants are randomized to different study arms and stay within their assigned group for the duration of the study. Parallel group trials in the ED have studied pediatric septic shock treatment, 62 computerized clinical decision support to streamline the workflow for ED-initiated buprenorphine/naloxone and referral, 63 ketamine infusion for acute sickle vaso-occlusive crisis, 64 and shared decision-making in low-risk chest pain. 65 Table 2. Study selection considerations and illustrative examples for common randomized PCT study design types. Study Type Pros/Cons/Rationale Example Studies Parallel Pro – No inadvertent contamination by unplanned interventions or cross-over Con – Often require larger sample sizes due to between-subject variation, which may increase cost and resource utilization Rationale – Most common study type, appropriate if concerns regarding cross-over may be present or if the disease or condition being studies may progress over time. Balamuth et al., 62 Melnick et al., 63 Alshahrani et al., 64 Hess et al. 65 Cross-over Pro – Comparison of treatment effects both within and between participants can increase power Con – Risk of contamination if the intervention cannot be turned ‘on’ and ‘off’ without residual practices being carried over from one period to the next; Duration of follow-up generally longer Rationale – Appropriate if concerns exist regarding temporal confounders or significant population variation that may prevent balanced distribution between groups. Wang et al., 26 Anderson et al., 67 Self et al. 30 Factorial Pro – Efficient, in that multiple research questions may be answered with limited sample sizes; Con – Complex in design and statistical analysis; Difficulty meeting inclusion criteria for both intervention(s) or components Rationale – Allows assessment of several intervention(s)/components and even interactions between them, often providing information whether varying levels or doses of an intervention affects different populations in different ways. Drummond et al., 70 Bernstein et al., 71 Abernethy et al., 72 Charlesworth et al., 73 Rickard et al. 74 Stepped wedge Pro – All participants receive the intervention; Comparison of treatment effects both within and between participants can increase power Con – Increased complexity may require additional statistical expertise and resources; May be subject to temporal confounding Rationale – Developed to address feasibility and ethical concerns that all participants should eventually receive the intervention within the study timeframe when the intervention is anticipated to produce a positive outcome. Grudzen et al., 76 Kerber et al., 77 Vaillancourt et al. 78 Open in a new tab Cross-over trial design types overcome the ethical limitations of a placebo control group by allowing all participants to receive the intervention, just in a different order. Of note, an adequate washout period must be included to eliminate the carry-over effect from the intervention that was first delivered. 66 Previous cross-over trials in the ED have assessed optimal airway management in out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, 26 head positioning in acute stroke, 67 and fluid resuscitation in noncritically ill patients. 30 Factorial (2x2, or more) design trials allow study of multiple interventions or multi-level interventions in varying combinations. Although this design type allows for comparisons between smaller sample sizes, investigators conducting full factorial design studies often need to ensure the trial functions as two independent RCTs without interaction between the two treatments. 68 In the presence of interaction between two treatments, variations in treatment effect estimands exist and more sophisticated modeling techniques should be considered to account for such an interaction effect. 69 Factorial designs have rarely been employed in the ED setting to date, 70 , 71 but studies from other disciplines and settings that could be adapted to the ED setting have assessed palliative care educational outreach, 72 peer support and reminiscence therapy for persons living with dementia and their caregivers, 73 and dressing and securement of peripherally inserted central catheters. 74 Stepped-wedge designs may be considered in multicenter studies, particularly when all participants (or clusters) can begin in the control group. As time passes, participants (or clusters) are randomized to the treatment arm in a sequential fashion, with all participants (or clusters) receiving the intervention within the study period. Illustrative ED-based pragmatic trials using stepped-wedge designs have addressed multidisciplinary primary palliative care, 75 , 76 treatment strategies for benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, 77 and paramedic assessment of low-risk trauma patients to selectively transport without immobilization. 78 Group allocation across the PCT study timeframe for common randomized study design types is depicted in Figure 3 . Figure 3. Open in a new tab Group allocation across the study timeframe for randomized PCT study design types Note – Groups are delineated by A-F. Conclusions There is growing interest in ED-based PCTs for their ability to test interventions in real-world settings and develop evidence that is more widely translatable than traditional explanatory research. To ensure the collection of meaningful results, emergency investigators must consider use of the PRECIS-2 tool as well as additional decisions within the study design phase. This manuscript can serve as a guide to navigate the unique advantages and disadvantages of various design types to be considered by investigators pursuing PCTs. Financial Support: This work is supported within the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Health Care Systems Research Collaboratory by the NIH Common Fund through cooperative agreement U24AT009676 from the Office of Strategic Coordination within the Office of the NIH Director and cooperative agreement (UH3DA047003) from the National Institute on Drug Abuse of the NIH. Dr. Gettel is also supported by the Claude D. Pepper Older Americans Independence Center at Yale School of Medicine (P30AG021342), the National Institute on Aging (NIA; R03AG073988), and the NIA Imbedded Pragmatic Alzheimer’s and AD-Related Dementias Clinical Trials (IMPACT) Collaboratory (U54AG063546). Dr. Yiadom is supported by the Institute for Human-centered Artificial Intelligence and the Emergency Department Benchmarking Alliance. Dr. Grudzen is supported by the NIH (UG3AT009844 and UH3AT009844) and the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PLC-1609-36306). The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health or other sponsors. Footnotes Conflict of Interest Disclosure: The authors report no conflicts of interest. Prior Presentations: This work was selected for presentation at the 2022 Society for Academic Emergency Medicine annual meeting. References 1. Broderick KB, Ranney ML, Vaca FE, et al. Study designs and evaluation models for emergency department public health research. Acad Emerg Med 2009;16(11):1124–31. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Califf RM, Zarin DA, Kramer JM, Sherman RE, Aberle LH, Tasneem A. Characteristics of clinical trials registered in Clinicaltrials.gov , 2007-2010. JAMA 2012;307(17):1838–47. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ioannidis JPA. Why most published research findings are false. PLoS Med 2005;2(8):e124. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Schwartz D, Lellouch J. Explanatory and pragmatic attitudes in therapeutical trials. J Chronic Dis 1967;20(8):637–48. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Califf RM, Sugarman