INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5853,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10213720','The impact of Anatomic Features of Asymptomatic Third Molars on the Pathologies of Adjacent Second Molars: A Cross-sectional Analysis.','Yang Y, Tian Y, Sun LJ, Qu HL, Li ZB, Tian BM, Chen FM.','2022','pd4 abl3 nonimpacted nonimpacted nonimpacted pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 abl3 pericoronitis nonimpacted erupted nonimpacted angulation hypothesise stomatology orthodontic orthopantomograms opgs stomatology opgs congenitally erupt occlusal erupted occlusal orthopantomograph oc100d instrumentarium tuusula cp15unc friedy distobuccal distolingual pd4 cementoenamel cej abl3 cej periodontist intraexaminer pd4 abl3 denominators pd4 abl3 opgs nonsmoker pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 nonimpacted pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 nonimpacted nonsmoking pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 abl3 abl3 pd4 abl3 pd4 abl3 nonimpacted pd4 abl3 abl6 abl3 abl6 abl3 abl6 abl6 abl6 abl3 abl6 abl3 nonimpacted pd4 abl3 abl6 nunn pd4 hypothesise pd4 abl3 papilla vermylen interdental abl6 abl3 alveola congenitally opgs opgs nonimpacted toothbrushing floss occlusal subproject shaanxi 2020jq identj coms nunn venta huumonen suominen s00784 venta s41 s47 joms ghaeminia nienhuijs cd003879 ghaeminia nienhuijs toedtling cd003879 pub5 joms odi nonimpacted immunologically microbiologically s41368 polat ozan pathoses e41 e47 tripleo petsos korte eickholz borchard jcpe kindler holtfreter koppe jcpe madianos offenbacher joms chugh ganeshkar revankar interradicular orthodontics embrasure papilla 051x vermylen quincey wolffe renggli 051x eke ryden buhlin ekstrand parokrank anjrini watchful bdj heaton aljoghaiman mcardle renton bdj toedtling tickle bjoms hounsome pilkington mahon hta24300 proenca morgado botelho jcm9072313 prato rotundo cortellini tinti azzi interdental papilla periodontics','Abstract Background We aimed to comprehensively examine how the anatomic characteristics of asymptomatic third molars (As-M3s) affect distal pathologies of adjacent second molars (Ad-M2s). Materials and methods Patients with at least 1 quadrant having intact As-M3s and first and second molars were enrolled. Distal pathologies of Ad-M2s, including caries, pocket depth of 4 mm or more (PD4+), and alveolar bone loss of 3 mm or more (ABL3+), were analysed based on As-M3 status (absent/impacted/nonimpacted). Especially within nonimpacted M3s (N-M3s), the effects of regions (maxillary vs mandibular) and dental intervals (narrow vs wide) on Ad-M2 pathologies were further compared. Results A total of 248 patients with their 805 quadrants were finally included in this cross-sectional study. The impacted and nonimpacted As-M3s elevated the risk of any distal pathology (caries, PD4+, or ABL3+) of Ad-M2s vs M3 absence with odds ratios (ORs) of 8.33 and 3.27, respectively. Within N-M3s, mandibular regions increased the odds of PD4+ (OR, 1.96); wide dental intervals increased the odds of ABL3+ (OR, 3.01). However, maxillary regions and narrow dental intervals contributed to more severe bone loss in Ad-M2s with ABL3+. Conclusions The presence of As-M3 is a risk factor for Ad-M2 pathologies irrespective of impaction status. Within N-M3s, Ad-M2 pathologies are significantly influenced by anatomic characteristics such as regions and dental intervals. Key words: Anatomic characteristic, Pathology, Second molar, Third molar Introduction The presence of third molars (M3s) has been reported to contribute to many oral diseases, especially involving pathologies on their adjacent second molars (Ad-M2s). 1 , 2 , 3 Removal of symptomatic M3s is widely accepted, but making clinical decisions on asymptomatic M3s (As-M3s) is amongst the most controversial clinical issues. 4 , 5 , 6 The focus on the controversy is that some As-M3s lead to poor outcomes (e.g., pericoronitis, periodontitis, caries) with aging, whilst others may remain silent for life. The crucial solution for this controversy is to distinguish high-risk As-M3s, and the impact of As-M3s on Ad-M2 health can provide a valuable reference. In recent years, the relationship between the As-M3 impaction status and Ad-M2 pathologies has attracted increasing attention. In a clinical survey of a large sample, when impacted and nonimpacted As-M3s were present, the incidence of deep periodontal pockets around Ad-M2s was 42.9% and 33.9%, respectively; the odds of periodontal pathology were elevated by 3.20-fold and 1.67-fold, respectively, vs M3 absence. 7 Another longitudinal study over 25 years also suggested that the risk of any distal pathology (caries or/and periodontitis) of Ad-M2s was elevated with erupted, soft-impacted, and bony-impacted As-M3 presence by 1.74-fold, 4.88-fold, and 2.16-fold, respectively, compared to those with M3 absence. 2 These studies suggest that the risk of Ad-M2 pathologies changes with the As-M3 impaction status, and the influence of nonimpacted third molars (N-M3s), which have been ignored for a long time, comes to the view of researchers. 8 , 9 , 10 Except for the impaction status of As-M3s, other anatomic characteristics are needed to identify high-risk As-M3s. Abundant literature has explored the anatomic characteristics of impacted third molars (I-M3s), including region (maxillary or mandibular), angulation (Winters’ classification), height (P&G classification), and covered tissue (soft tissue or bony), which are influencing factors for Ad-M2 pathologies. 2 , 11 , 12 , 13 However, which anatomic characteristics of N-M3s contribute to Ad-M2 pathologies is still unknown. The plaque control and bone density in N-M3 regions exhibit differences between maxillary and mandibular; hence, N-M3 regions may have an impact on Ad-M2 pathologies. 14 , 15 Additionally, the dental interval width has been identified to be related to the development of periodontal pathologies in other teeth. 16 , 17 Therefore, we hypothesise that the regions and dental intervals of N-M3s would be important influencing factors for Ad-M2 pathologies. Our primary objective in this study was to assess the occurrence of distal pathologies of Ad-M2s associated with As-M3s using clinical and radiographic examinations. Second, we aimed to compare the incidence of distal pathologies of Ad-M2s between different anatomic characteristics of As-M3s, including the impaction status of As-M3s and the regions and dental intervals of N-M3s. Materials and methods Patient selection Data were consecutively collected from patients seeking routine dental care from October 2020 to September 2021, at the School of Stomatology, Fourth Military Medical University. All patients aged 18 years or older with at least 1 quadrant that had visible As-M3s with intact first and second molars were eligible for inclusion. Patients with the following conditions were excluded: (a) systemic diseases such as hypertension, diabetes, and cancer; (b) pregnancy; (c) orthodontic experience; (d) periodontal therapy within 6 months; (e) anti-inflammatory drug use within 3 months; or (f) refusal to provide sociodemographic information or take orthopantomograms (OPGs). The sociodemographic characteristics of all included patients were collected with a structured questionnaire, which included age, sex, education level (senior high school or below; college graduate or above) and smoking status. This cross-sectional study was conducted in accordance with the Helsinki Declaration of Human Studies, and the research protocol was ethically approved by the Ethics Committee and Institutional Review Board, School of Stomatology, Fourth Military Medical University (IRB-REV-2017034). Informed consent was obtained from all participants included in the study to participate and use their data and radiographic images for publication. Quadrant selection and classification All quadrants in included patients were further screened according to OPGs, and only the quadrants having intact first and second molars, either with visible As-M3s or without M3s, were finally included. As-M3 status was categorised as absent M3s, I-M3s, and N-M3s. Absent M3s included congenitally missing and later removed M3s. I-M3s were defined as M3s that failed to erupt to the occlusal plane, and N-M3s were defined as M3s that erupted to the occlusal plane, with a 0 to 10 angle between the long axis of M2s and M3s. 8 The sample size was estimated by the z statistic with calculations based on the study of Qu et al. 7 The results showed that at least 640 quadrants with As-M3s were sufficient to estimate the prevalence of pathologies on A-M2s (parameters: significance level α = 0.05, δ = 10%, expected prevalence = 38.5%). Clinical and radiographic examinations All included quadrants were subjected to clinical and radiographic examinations (Orthopantomograph, OC100D-1-4-1, Instrumentarium, Tuusula, Finland). Periodontal data were recorded with a manual periodontal probe (CP15UNC; Hu-Friedy), and the scale nearest to the gingival margin was recorded as the probing depth (PD). Two M2 sites (distobuccal and distolingual) were measured, and a mean PD greater than 4 mm was recorded as PD4+. Distal caries of M2s were confirmed by the combination of clinical examinations and radiographic assessments. Any low-density shadow in the distal surface of M2s was diagnosed as caries, and filled distal caries were also identified as caries. Distal alveolar bone loss (ABL) of M2s was deemed present if the distances from the alveolar bone to the cementoenamel junction (CEJ) were 3 mm or more (ABL3+) in OPG examinations. 18 In addition, the dental intervals of N-M3s and overall periodontal status were further identified by OPG examinations (Supplemental Figure 1). The horizontal distance at the proximal CEJ between N-M3s and their Ad-M2s is the dental interval. A horizontal width of 2 mm or greater was defined as the wide group and that less than 2 mm was defined as the narrow group ( Supplemental Figure 1a ). 16 The overall periodontal status was evaluated by measuring the remaining bone proportion in root length ( Supplemental Figure 1b ). When the mean value of all teeth (mesial and distal) except M3s was less than 80%, the patients were diagnosed with periodontitis. 19 All clinical and radiographic examinations were conducted by one experienced periodontist (Y.Y.). The intraexaminer reproducibility of OPG analysis was 92%. Statistical analyses Data were imported into SPSS software (version 22) and analysed. Distal pathologies of Ad-M2s included caries, PD4+, and ABL3+. The relationship of anatomic characteristics of As-M3s and distal pathologies of Ad-M2s was analysed at quadrant level, which means the percentage of diseased Ad-M2s was calculated using the number of quadrants with different As-M3 status as denominators. The differences in distal caries, PD4+, ABL3+, and any one of the 3 pathologies of Ad-M2s amongst different As-M3 statuses were compared. In particular, the N-M3s were subdivided according to regions (maxillary or mandibular) and dental intervals (narrow or wide), and their effects on distal pathologies of Ad-M2s were further compared. All analyses were conducted using generalised estimating equation logistic regression models to correct correlation data at the patient level, and other confounding factors, including sex, age, education level, smoking status, and periodontal status, were adjusted. All bivariate associations were explained using the odds ratio (OR) and 95% confidence interval (95% CI) throughout the statistical analyses. All tests were 2-sided with a significance level of .05. Results A total of 296 patients met the inclusion criteria and were invited to enroll; 43 of them were filtered by exclusion criteria. The main reason for exclusion was systemic diseases (65.1%), followed by periodontal treatment history. Additionally, OPGs from 5 patients were excluded for poor quality. Finally, 248 patients were included in this study, and their 992 quadrants were further screened and classified according to the anatomic characteristics of As-M3s. Based on the bias of the screening criteria, 187 quadrants were excluded for having symptomatic or completely impacted M3s and broken or lost first/second molars. In those enrolled 805 quadrants, the number of quadrants with N-M3s (n = 518) was greater than that with I-M3s (n = 137). Within N-M3s, slightly more than half of them were in the maxillary (n = 276), but when referring to dental intervals, close to three-quarters of them had narrow dental intervals (n = 383; Supplemental Figure 2 ). The demographic characteristics of the included patients (40.9 ± 12.3 years) and the distribution of enrolled quadrants (1–4) at patient level are presented in Table 1 . Table 1. Descriptive statistics for the enrolled patients and patient-level distribution of enrolled quadrants (N = 248). a Variables N % Age, y <35 106 42.7% ≥35 142 57.3% Sex Male 126 50.8% Female 122 49.2% Periodontal status Healthy 117 47.2% Periodontitis 131 52.8% Educational status Senior high school or below 117 47.2% College graduate or above 131 52.8% Smoking status Smoker 35 14.1% Nonsmoker 213 85.9% Distribution of enrolled quadrants 1 15 6.0% 2 44 17.7% 3 54 21.8% 4 135 54.4% Open in a new tab a N, number of patients. Distal pathologies of Ad-M2s with different impact status of As-M3s Table 2 reveals the influence of As-M3 status on the incidence of each distal pathology of Ad-M2s. The results suggested that I-M3 presence significantly increased the odds of distal caries, PD4+, and ABL3+ of Ad-M2s vs M3 absence by 2.61-fold, 9.75-fold, and 8.75-fold, respectively. Different from I-M3s, there was no significant difference between quadrants with or without N-M3s regarding the incidence of distal caries of Ad-M2s, but the odds of distal PD4+ and ABL3+ of Ad-M2s was significantly elevated with N-M3 presence by 3.39-fold and 2.95-fold, respectively. Table 2. The influences of As-M3 status on the occurrence of each distal pathology (caries, PD4+, or ABL3+) of Ad-M2s. Distal caries of Ad-M2s Distal PD4+ 