 probing depth ≥4 mm; ABL3+, alveolar bone loss ≥3 mm; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. ⁎ Statistically significant difference ( P < .05) by generalised estimating equation logistic regression models. Distal pathologies of Ad-M2s with different regions and dental intervals of N-M3s Table 4 indicates the difference in each distal pathology of Ad-M2s between different N-M3 regions (maxillary vs mandible) and dental intervals (narrow vs wide). When N-M3s were located in the mandible, their Ad-M2s were at significantly higher risk for distal PD4+ (OR, 1.96). Although the risks of distal caries and ABL3+ of Ad-M2s were reduced in the mandible, the difference was not statistically significant. When N-M3s had wide dental intervals, the risks of all 3 distal pathologies of Ad-M2s were elevated, but only the difference in the occurrence of distal ABL3+ was significant (OR, 3.01). Representative radiographic images for this ABL3+ trend are shown in Supplemental Figure 3 . Table 4. The influences of N-M3 regions or dental intervals on the occurrence of each distal pathology (caries, PD4+, or ABL3+) of Ad-M2s. Distal caries of Ad-M2s Distal PD4+ of Ad-M2s Distal ABL3+ of Ad-M2s N-M3s n (%) OR, P value 95% CI n (%) OR, P value 95% CI n (%) OR, P value 95% CI Regions Maxillary 32 (11.6%) 1 48 (17.4%) 1 111 (40.2%) 1 Mandibular 20 (8.3%) 0.59, .113 0.31-1.13 82 (33.9%) 1.96, .002 * 1.29-2.99 111 (45.9%) 0.63, .073 0.38-1.04 Dental intervals Narrow 41 (10.7%) 1 78 (20.4%) 1 143 (41.5%) 1 Wide 11 (8.1%) 1.06, .860 0.55-2.06 52 (38.5%) 1.42, .175 0.86-2.34 79 (58.5%) 3.01, <.001 * 1.66-5.49 Open in a new tab n, number of quadrants; N-M3s, nonimpacted third molars; Ad-M2s, adjacent second molars; PD4+, probing depth ≥4 mm; ABL3+, alveolar bone loss ≥3 mm; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. ⁎ Statistically significant difference ( P < .05) by generalised estimating equation logistic regression models. However, we found a contrary trend in the impact of N-M3 regions and dental intervals on the severity of Ad-M2 distal bone loss. Further analyses within N-M3s were conducted using the proportion of alveolar bone loss of 6 mm or more (ABL6+) in Ad-M2s with ABL3+ ( Table 5 ). The data were analysed using the chi-square test for limited data. Intriguingly, the results identified that when compared with mandibular regions, maxillary regions significantly increased the ABL6+ proportion in Ad-M2s with ABL3+ (OR, 1.84). Meanwhile, the ABL6+ proportion was significantly elevated with narrow dental intervals vs that with wide dental intervals (OR, 3.86). Representative radiographic images for this ABL6+ trend are shown in Supplemental Figure 4 . Table 5. The influences of the N-M3 locations or dental intervals on the ABL6+ proportion in Ad-M2s with ABL3+. ABL6+ in Ad-M2s with ABL3+ N-M3s n (%) OR, P value 95% CI Regions Mandibular 26 (23.4%) 1 Maxillary 40 (36.0%) 1.84, .040 * 1.03-3.31 Dental intervals Wide 11 (13.9%) 1 Narrow 55 (38.5%) 3.86, <.001 * 1.88-7.94 Open in a new tab n, number of quadrants; N-M3s, nonimpacted third molars; Ad-M2s, adjacent second molars; PD4+, probing depth ≥4 mm; ABL3+, alveolar bone loss ≥3 mm; ABL6+, alveolar bone loss ≥6 mm; OR, odds ratio; CI, confidence interval. ⁎ Statistically significant difference ( P < .05) by chi-square test. Discussion There has been no conclusion about the prophylactic removal of As-M3s until now due to a lack of sufficient and reliable evidence. Retaining asymptomatic and disease-free M3s and monitoring with active surveillance are more acceptable methods for both patients and clinicians. 4 , 20 , 21 However, abundant literature has identified that even As-M3s can induce Ad-M2 pathologies, and M2 pathologies associated with M3s may lead to increased expenses and poor prognoses for patients. 22 , 23 , 24 This study aimed to elucidate the role of anatomic characteristics of As-M3s in Ad-M2 pathologies with a comprehensive approach. We found that the impaction status of As-M3s and the regions and dental intervals of N-M3s were influencing factors for distal caries and periodontitis of Ad-M2s. Irrespective of impaction status, the presence of As-M3s increased the odds of distal pathologies (caries and/or periodontitis) of their Ad-M2s, even after controlling for periodontal status, age, and other confounding factors ( Table 3 ). Periodontal status was measured by the OPG method, which has been verified as a reliable tool for periodontitis screening under the 2018 periodontitis case definition. 25 This finding is also in accord with the observations of Nunn et al and Li et al, who found that the presence of N-M3s threatened Ad-M2 health. 2 , 8 Moreover, we noticed that N-M3s accounted for three-quarters of As-M3s. Although the odds of Ad-M2 pathologies related to I-M3s were higher than those related to N-M3s, the total number of diseased M2s associated with N-M3s was greater than that associated with I-M3s. This means that M2 pathologies associated with N-M3s might pose a heavy burden on health care resources in the general population with As-M3s. To distinguish high-risk N-M3s more quickly and easily in clinical practice, understanding the relationship of N-M3 anatomic characteristics and Ad-M2 pathologies is crucial. Therefore, we conducted the following analysis on N-M3s. Within the N-M3 regions, we found that N-M3s in the mandible were more than twice as likely to contribute to distal PD4+ of Ad-M2s than those in the maxilla ( Table 4 ). We hypothesise that plaque elimination is more difficult in mandibular N-M3 regions, which may partly explain the difference in distal PD4+ of Ad-M2s between N-M3 regions. However, once alveolar bone loss began, M2s with N-M3s in the maxilla were prone to have more severe bone loss than those in the mandible ( Table 5 ). The possible reason for this trend is that the bone density at the molar area is lower in the maxilla than in the mandible. 15 Within the dental intervals, N-M3s with wide dental intervals increased the odds of distal ABL3+ of Ad-M2s by 3 times vs that with narrow dental intervals ( Table 4 ). The space below the contact area results in food impaction, which contributes to plaque accumulation and causes later periodontal pathologies. 26 Our finding is consistent with an earlier observation, which showed that central papilla recession was more associated with a wide dental interval of more than 2 mm. 16 Moreover, Vermylen et al revealed that when patients have bilateral root proximity, a distance between interdental spaces less than 1 mm, they had 3.6 times higher risk of experiencing bone loss greater than one-third of root length at least at one site. 17 Although with different criteria for ABL, their result also agreed with our observations, which showed that narrow dental interval (<2 mm) N-M3s were related to a higher proportion of ABL6+ in Ad-M2s with ABL3+ ( Table 5 ). On the one hand, narrow dental intervals could hamper oral hygiene measures and mechanical debridement delivery at the site. On the other hand, alveola bone is thinner in narrow dental intervals than in wide dental intervals. Therefore, narrow dental intervals contributed to more severe bone loss in the disturbed situation. In this study, we compared the difference in Ad-M2 pathologies between different anatomic characteristics of As-M3s, especially involving the regions and dental intervals of N-M3s, which have not been explored. Our results will help clinicians make As-M3 clinical decisions for individuals based on the anatomic characteristics of As-M3s. In addition to As-M3 status, we explored the relationship of other factors (eg, overall periodontal status) and Ad-M2 pathologies using generalised estimating equation logistic regression models. However, our study also has limitations. The reference group included both congenitally missing M3s and later removed M3s. Such conditions mean that our analyses led to an underestimated risk of Ad-M2 pathologies associated with As-M3 presence. Moreover, we used OPGs instead of periapical radiographs to measure the dental-interval width. Periapical radiographs were difficult to conduct in M3 regions due to limited space. Cone-beam computed tomography could be used to conduct more precise measurements. Although the measurement of OPGs leads to bias, we hope this study could draw more attention to how N-M3s affect their Ad-M2 pathologies. Our study provides evidence for more active intervention for As-M3s. When As-M3s are impacted, prophylactic removal is recommended to decrease the risk of Ad-M2 pathologies under appropriate conditions. When the As-M3s are nonimpacted, the situation would be different by N-M3 regions and dental intervals. If the N-M3s were in the mandible or had wide dental intervals, mechanical cleaning methods, including toothbrushing and dental floss use, should be emphasised for patients to prevent periodontal pathologies in the N-M3 regions. However, if M2s have periodontal pathologies associated with maxillary or narrow dental interval N-M3s, clinicians should understand that the periodontal pathologies would develop rapidly and eventually lead to M2 loss. Early therapeutic removal may be a better clinical choice. Prevention and early treatment are crucial, and we hope our study provides new perspectives regarding As-M3 decisions for clinicians. In the near future, we plan to further explore more clinical parameters associated with As-M3 pathologies (eg, root resorption, odontogenic cyst, occlusal trauma) and the impact of other As-M3 anatomic characteristics (eg, contact area) on Ad-M2 pathologies in subsequent studies. Conclusions Within As-M3s, both I-M3s and N-M3s contributed to higher odds of distal pathologies of their Ad-M2s compared with M3 absence, even after controlling for periodontal status and other factors. Although the odds associated with N-M3s were lower than those associated with I-M3s, the number of diseased A-M2s was greater with N-M3s than with I-M3s. Within N-M3s, regions and dental intervals significantly influenced the occurrence and development of distal periodontitis of Ad-M2s. There is a close relationship between the anatomic characteristics of As-M3s and distal pathologies of Ad-M2s, and clinical decisions on As-M3s should be made based on the relationship. Author contributions Yang Yang and Yi Tian contributed equally to this work. Conflict of interest None disclosed. Acknowledgments This study was financially supported by the Major Research Program of the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Beijing, Subproject No. 81991503) and the Natural Science Basic Research Program of Shaanxi (2020JQ-447). Footnotes Supplementary material associated with this article can be found in the online version at doi:10.1016/j.identj.2022.09.001 . Appendix. Supplementary materials mmc1.docx (2.3MB, docx) REFERENCES 1. Campbell JH. Pathology associated with the third molar. Oral Maxillofac Surg Clin North Am. 2013;25:1–10. doi: 10.1016/j.coms.2012.11.005. v. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Nunn ME, Fish MD, Garcia RI, et al. Retained asymptomatic third molars and risk for second molar pathology. J Dent Res. 2013;92:1095–1099. doi: 10.1177/0022034513509281. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Ventä I, Huumonen S, Suominen AL. How many third molars remain unnoticed in a population survey without panoramic radiographs? Clin Oral Investig. 2020;24:2727–2733. doi: 10.1007/s00784-019-03134-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Venta I. How often do asymptomatic, disease-free third molars need to be removed? J Oral Maxillofac Surg. 2012;70:S41–S47. doi: 10.1016/j.joms.2012.04.037. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Ghaeminia H, Perry J, Nienhuijs ME, et al. Surgical removal versus retention for the management of asymptomatic disease-free impacted wisdom teeth. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2016 doi: 10.1002/14651858.CD003879.pub4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Ghaeminia H, N