INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5854,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12055618','Beyond perfection: why imperfect routinely collected intensive care data still hold value.','Rodemund N, Wernly B, Stundner O, Rockenschaub P, Koköfer A.','2025','porteners trash trash imperfections imperfections unparalleled imperfections messiness sanitized brittle perfecting rejecting imperfections amsterdamumcdb hirid eicu sicdb flaw adage trash trash cautionary imperfections forging unlock porteners meyfroidt trash trash sande genderen huiskens bytes lijovic elbers shillan champneys gibbison rodemund wernly salzburg sicdb','We read the Editorial by Pörteners et al. in Intensive Care Medicine (ICM), titled “Trash in/Trash out? Using Routinely Collected Clinical Data for Data Science in the ICU: Con.” with great interest. However, we believe that in certain aspects, their perspective does not fully reflect the broader potential offered by large-scale intensive care unit (ICU) datasets collected through clinical routine. In the rapidly evolving world of data science, the application of artificial intelligence (AI) promises to revolutionize ICU care. However, the routine clinical data collected in these settings are often criticized for their inherent imperfections, including biases and errors, which some argue reduce model quality and reliability. Critics contend that these imperfections result from the data’s primary purpose being clinical documentation, not research. Variations across institutions in how data are collected, processed, and maintained exacerbate these issues, raising concerns about model generalizability. Additionally, models derived from routine data, while showing promise in development phases, are rarely implemented at the bedside [ 1 , 2 ]. Despite these challenges, routinely collected clinical data offer unparalleled opportunities for advancing ICU research and improving patient outcomes [ 3 ]. Routine data imperfections are not merely a challenge; they are an asset. These datasets mirror the complexities and messiness of real-world healthcare settings. AI models trained on routine data are inherently better equipped to navigate real-world complexities, making them more suitable to clinical deployment. If models were trained exclusively on highly curated and sanitized datasets, they risk being brittle and ineffective when faced with the unpredictable realities of real-world ICU environments. Moreover, the nature of clinical care is unlikely to change significantly in terms of data precision within a reasonable timeframe. The cost, effort, and logistical challenges of perfecting data are immense. Rejecting routinely collected data due to imperfections misses the opportunity to develop models that can account for and adapt to them. Real-world data ensure that models are not only theoretically sound but also practically applicable in dynamic and high-stake ICU settings. A substantial number of articles have already leveraged publicly available datasets such as MIMIC, AmsterdamUMCdb, HiRID, eICU, and SICdb, highlighting their usability and potential. Foundational research using these datasets serves as an invaluable stepping-stone for future efforts to bring AI to the bedside [ 4 , 5 ]. While the integration of existing models into ICU workflows remains limited, we believe that this stems more from insufficient data diversity and model validation rather than from any intrinsic flaw of routine data itself. On the contrary, increasingly large and multicenter routine data will be indispensable for robust AI in the ICU, provided it is accompanied by rigorous methodology. While the adage “trash in, trash out” serves as a cautionary tale in data science, it should not deter researchers from leveraging routine data in ICU research. Real-world context, foundational insights, and the sheer volume of available data make a compelling case for accepting imperfections and forging ahead. By embracing routine data, the research community can develop adaptable AI models, establish a basis for continuous improvement, and unlock the full potential of data-driven critical care. Declarations Conflicts of interest The authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest to disclose. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Pörteners B, Jung C, Meyfroidt G (2024) Trash in/trash out? Using routinely collected clinical data for data science in the ICU: Con. Intensive Care Med. 10.1007/s00134-024-07739-3 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. van de Sande D, van Genderen ME, Huiskens J et al (2021) Moving from bytes to bedside: a systematic review on the use of artificial intelligence in the intensive care unit. Intensive Care Med 47:750–760. 10.1007/s00134-021-06446-7 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lijović L, Elbers P (2024) Leveraging the power of routinely collected ICU data. Intensive Care Med. 10.1007/s00134-024-07745-5 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Shillan D, Sterne JAC, Champneys A, Gibbison B (2019) Use of machine learning to analyse routinely collected intensive care unit data: a systematic review. Crit Care 23:284. 10.1186/s13054-019-2564-9 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Rodemund N, Wernly B, Jung C et al (2023) The Salzburg Intensive Care database (SICdb): an openly available critical care dataset. Intensive Care Med 49:700–702. 10.1007/s00134-023-07046-3 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',3,'2025-10-15 11:37:51','2025-10-15 11:37:51');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5855,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1343060','A transatlantic comparison of training in emergency medicine.','Wyatt JP, Weber JE.','1998','transatlantic barsan diebold ferraro moonlighting tb03233 kietzman renner accid langdorf bearie ferkich moonlighting moonlighting tb03227 langdorf bearie ferkich moonlighting inservice tb03228 redux reynard accid rodenberg accid jayne wakeman wols tb03176 hojat veloski indebtedness tb02524','Abstract The system of training in accident and emergency (A&E) medicine in the United Kingdom is at a critical and much earlier stage of development than in the United States. Transatlantic comparison offers the opportunity to explore possible ways of improving training in the United Kingdom. Comparison revealed deficiencies in the UK training system in the following: prehospital care training, formal theoretical teaching, close supervision in a clinical setting, and in-service training examinations. Implementation of measures designed to address these deficiencies would enhance UK training in A&E medicine. Full text PDF Previous 175 176 177 178 179 180 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Barsan W. G., Levy R. C. Duration of training in emergency medicine residencies. Ann Emerg Med. 1982 Nov;11(11):639–640. doi: 10.1016/s0196-0644(82)80212-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Diebold S. E., Ferraro C. M. Moonlighting and student debt. Acad Emerg Med. 1995 Apr;2(4):330–331. doi: 10.1111/j.1553-2712.1995.tb03233.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Dire D. J., Kietzman L. I. A prospective survey of procedures performed by emergency medicine residents during a 36-month residency. J Emerg Med. 1995 Nov-Dec;13(6):831–837. doi: 10.1016/0736-4679(95)02012-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Driscoll P., Cope A., Miles S. A. Adequacy of senior registrar training in accident and emergency medicine over the last 5 y